\\nsd, se, ci_low, ci_hi\\n\",\"ct\":1777871050.039862},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"search_query\\\":[{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Dr. GRPO Correcting Token Aggregation Bias LLM Reinforcement Learning arXiv 2025 Liu\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"GRPO Done Right Dr GRPO arxiv 2025 length bias difficulty bias\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Understanding R1-Zero-Like Training A Critical Perspective Dr. GRPO length bias 2025\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777871116.838677},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve extracted the full 12-page text, and the clearest AC-level vulnerability is evidentiary: the controlled result is non-significant while stronger-looking results appear single-seed, closed-backend, or unmatched.\",\"ct\":1777871566.276473},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve also found two concrete audit hooks: held-out accuracy is mixed with online/peak metrics, and the sample-size definition conflicts between pooled N=15 and within-GSM8K N=19.\",\"ct\":1777872121.652731},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## 1) TOP-OF-STACK\\n\\nThe strongest rejection reason is **result provenance failure**: the PDF’s headline Qwen3-8B/GSM8K numbers do not align with the paper’s linked reproduction artifact, and the PDF itself contradicts which model was used for a key TRL baseline. The PDF reports “post-GRPO last-10 accuracy is 83.3%” on p. 6, §4.1 and “Tinker GRPO, G=8: 0.844 / 1.000” on p. 7, Table 1, while the public reproduction file says reviewers should verify “last-10 average 34.4%” and “Peak accuracy 62.5%”; the same artifact lineage also lists Apache-2.0 while the PDF claims MIT. citeturn731793view2turn731793view0turn731793view1 This is not just conservative framing; a serious AC will read it as an empirical audit failure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2) SEVERITY-TAGGED ISSUES\\n\\n### 1. **Critical — Artifact/reproduction record contradicts the PDF’s headline results**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 2, Contributions: “We release scripts, LoRA adapters, step-level traces, manifests, and diagnostic utilities under MIT License…” p. 6, §4.1: “the post-GRPO last-10 accuracy is 83.3% versus 82.0% for the base model.” p. 7, Table 1: “Qwen3-8B Tinker GRPO, G=8 … Last-10 Acc. 0.844, Peak Acc. 1.000.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The artifact linked from the paper currently presents a different “headline result” for the same general paper lineage: Qwen3-8B/GSM8K, 30 steps, LoRA rank 32, group size 8, with “last-10 average 34.4%” and “Peak accuracy 62.5%,” not 84.4% / 100%. It also says the artifact license is Apache-2.0, while the PDF says MIT. This is the kind of mismatch that makes reviewers stop reading and ask whether the table was regenerated, copied from another manuscript, or evaluated on a different split. citeturn731793view2turn731793view0turn731793view1\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The artifact repository for this version is synchronized with the result tables in this paper: every reported result is traceable to a run identifier, seed, evaluator commit, and artifact manifest entry. We report only numbers generated from that synchronized artifact snapshot and license the released code under the license stated in the repository metadata. Any earlier or alternative reproduction numbers are excluded from the claims in this version.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. **Critical — The TRL baseline model is internally inconsistent**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 5, §3.3, Backends: “TRL on NVIDIA L4: lightweight baseline runs (Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds).” p. 7, Table 1: “Qwen3-8B — TRL on NVIDIA L4 (5 seeds) — 0.701 — 0.734.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** This is not a typo-level issue. The paper uses the TRL row to discuss a “14 pp gap” against Tinker and to contextualize backend effects. If the TRL run is Qwen2.5-0.5B, it cannot be compared to Qwen3-8B Tinker. If it is Qwen3-8B, the methods section is wrong. Either way, the backend comparison is not auditable.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> TRL on NVIDIA L4: lightweight five-seed GRPO baseline runs; the exact model identifier for each TRL row is reported in the corresponding results table. We do not use a TRL row for model-matched comparison unless its model identifier, reward code, evaluation split, seed count, and decoding protocol match the comparison row exactly.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. **Critical — Table 1 appears to mix held-out test accuracy, online training reward, and selection-biased peak metrics**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 7, Table 1 caption: “Last-10 accuracy denotes the mean accuracy over the final ten evaluation checkpoints, while peak accuracy reports the highest single-checkpoint accuracy observed during training.” p. 7, Table 1: “Qwen3-8B Tinker GRPO, G=8 … Peak Acc. 1.000”; “Qwen3-4B Tinker GRPO, G=8 … Peak Acc. 1.000.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** A held-out GSM8K test-set peak of 100% for Qwen3-8B or Qwen3-4B is extraordinary and would require serious validation. If “peak accuracy” is actually online reward, minibatch reward, training-set reward, or a tiny evaluation subset, then Table 1’s label is misleading. The base row is explicitly “held-out eval,” while the Tinker rows are described as “observed during training,” so the table is comparing unlike quantities.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> We separate held-out evaluation from online training metrics. The main table reports only held-out GSM8K test accuracy at the final evaluation protocol; online peak reward is selection-biased and is reported only in the artifact traces, not as a comparable accuracy metric. All rows in the main table use the same evaluator, split, decoding protocol, and answer-extraction script.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. **Critical — The ZVF correlation table has non-nested sample sizes that read as impossible**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 8, §4.3: “The pooled cross-task correlation between mean ZVF and last-10 accuracy is large (r = −0.769, p = 0.0008, N =15).” Same paragraph: “the sign flips from r = −0.769 pooled to r = +0.40 within-GSM8K.” p. 8, Table 3: “Pooled, cross-stack — N 15”; “Within-GSM8K (all) — N 19.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** As written, “within-GSM8K” reads like a subset of the pooled cross-task analysis, but it has more samples than the pooled set. That is possible only if the paper silently changes inclusion criteria between rows. Since the paper’s core diagnostic claim is about stratification, opaque sample definitions destroy the argument.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> Table 3 uses two non-nested analysis sets. The pooled cross-task row contains the 15 runs for which both task-level ZVF and last-10 metrics were available under the pooled audit protocol. The GSM8K-only rows use an expanded 19-run GSM8K audit set and therefore should not be read as subsets of the pooled row. The purpose of the table is to show that the apparent pooled correlation is not stable under task-controlled analysis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. **Critical — The claimed “controlled headline result” is under-specified statistically**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 6, §4.1: “On a five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K base-vs-GRPO comparison under a fixed greedy held-out evaluation protocol, the post-GRPO last-10 accuracy is 83.3% versus 82.0% for the base model: a delta of +1.3 pp with t = 1.32, p = 0.26.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The paper does not show the five seed values, whether the t-test is paired or one-sample, what the degrees of freedom are, or how a deterministic greedy base evaluation gets variance. If the test is across five GRPO seeds against a fixed base, the implied 95% confidence interval is roughly −1.4 to +4.0 percentage points, i.e., compatible with no gain or a small gain. The paper says “not statistically significant,” which is good, but it still calls this a “controlled headline result” without enough audit detail.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The five-seed control is a null result. Across five GRPO seeds, Qwen3-8B achieves 83.3% mean held-out last-10 accuracy versus the deterministic greedy base value of 82.0% (\\\\(\\\\Delta=+1.3\\\\) pp; one-sample \\\\(t_4=1.32\\\\), \\\\(p=0.26\\\\); approximate 95% CI \\\\([-1.4,+4.0]\\\\) pp). We report this only to bound the effect size under this protocol, not as evidence of a reliable GRPO improvement.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 6. **Major — “We introduce ZVF” overclaims novelty relative to known zero-advantage/dynamic-sampling work**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 1, Abstract: “We introduce Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF), the fraction of prompts in a batch with zero within-group reward variance…” p. 6, §3.4: “a prompt’s group contributes zero gradient signal whenever all G completions receive the same reward.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The condition is not new: DAPO explicitly describes the GRPO zero-advantage problem when all outputs for a prompt have reward 1 or 0 and proposes over-sampling/filtering to keep prompts with effective gradients. Your name “ZVF” may be useful as a logging metric, but the paper currently risks sounding like it discovered a known degeneracy. citeturn912241view0\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> We use Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) as a logging name for the known zero-advantage group condition in group-relative RL. Prior dynamic-sampling methods address the same condition by filtering all-correct or all-wrong groups; our contribution is a compact per-step diagnostic and a stratified empirical audit showing that pooled ZVF--accuracy correlations are task-confounded, not a new optimization algorithm.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 7. **Major — Equation (3) is correct only under an iid Bernoulli abstraction, not as a batch-level identity over heterogeneous prompts**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 6, §3.4: “For binary-outcome tasks with per-prompt success probability p and group size G, the closed-form identity ZVF(p,G) = pG + (1 − p)G holds exactly.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The formula is exact for a fixed prompt if the G rollouts are iid Bernoulli with the same success probability \\\\(p\\\\). In the actual experiments, \\\\(p\\\\) varies by prompt, model, step, decoding temperature, and backend. The expected batch ZVF is an average over prompt-specific \\\\(p_x\\\\), not simply a function of mean reward.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> Under an iid Bernoulli abstraction for a fixed prompt \\\\(x\\\\) with rollout success probability \\\\(p_x\\\\),  \\n> \\\\[\\n> \\\\Pr[\\\\sigma_G(x)=0] = p_x^G + (1-p_x)^G .\\n> \\\\]  \\n> For a batch \\\\(B\\\\), the corresponding expected ZVF is \\\\(|B|^{-1}\\\\sum_{x\\\\in B}[p_x^G+(1-p_x)^G]\\\\). We use this identity only to interpret degenerate regimes; we do not estimate ZVF from aggregate accuracy alone.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 8. **Major — “Zero gradient signal” is too broad because the KL term still contributes gradients**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 1, Abstract: “this shortcut can silently eliminate gradient signal when all completions for a prompt receive the same binary reward.” p. 5, §3.3: “A KL penalty term \\\\(\\\\beta \\\\cdot D_{KL}(\\\\pi_\\\\theta \\\\| \\\\pi_{ref})\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\beta = 0.01\\\\) constrains policy drift…”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** If the GRPO reward advantage is zero, the reward-driven policy-gradient part vanishes for that group. But the paper’s own objective includes a KL penalty, and some implementations also include entropy or other regularization. A technically sharp reviewer will object to the unqualified phrase “zero gradient signal.”\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> When all completions for a prompt receive the same binary reward, the group-normalised reward advantage is zero, so the reward-driven relative policy-gradient term contributes no learning signal for that prompt. Other objective terms, such as KL regularisation, may still produce gradients; throughout the paper, “collapse” refers specifically to the disappearance of the relative reward-advantage signal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 9. **Major — The group-size conclusion is based on a single seed but still reads like a default recommendation**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 4, §2.3: “Our group-size ablation … identifies G=8 as a practical default at the model and task scales tested.” p. 7, §4.2: “The G=8 configuration achieves perfect peak accuracy and the highest last-10 accuracy (84.4%).”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The paper itself says the ablation is single-seed. Group size changes memory, effective batch composition, sampling diversity, and the probability of all-correct/all-wrong groups. Without multiple seeds and matched token/compute budgets, “G=8 default” is too strong.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> In this single-seed Tinker sweep, \\\\(G=8\\\\) was the best observed setting among \\\\(G\\\\in\\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}\\\\) for Qwen3-8B/GSM8K. We treat this as a tuning hypothesis for this configuration, not as a default recommendation across models, tasks, or backends.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 10. **Major — The “platform gap” is acknowledged as confounded but still used as a result**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 10, §5.2: “On Qwen3-8B/GSM8K with G=8, the Tinker backend produces a single-seed last-10 of 84.4% and the open-stack TRL backend on NVIDIA L4 produces a five-seed last-10 of 70.1%.” Same paragraph: “The 14 pp gap is therefore a property of the configurations as a whole…”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** You cannot present a “backend gap” and then disclaim every axis that could explain it. Single-seed closed backend vs five-seed open-stack L4 is not a platform comparison. The current text is careful, but the table and contribution still invite a conclusion the design cannot support.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The Tinker and TRL rows are not a backend comparison. They differ in implementation, hardware, seed count, batching, and possibly model/evaluator details, so we report them as separate configuration records only. We do not interpret their numerical gap as evidence for or against any backend.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 11. **Major — PPO comparison attributes wall-clock cost to the critic without a matched design**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 8, §4.4: “PPO baselines on Qwen3-8B (Modal H100) achieve a last-10 accuracy of 81.2% on GSM8K, compared to 84.4% for GRPO at G=8 (Tinker).” Same paragraph: “Wall-clock training time is 1.8× longer for PPO due to critic forward passes.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The paragraph says backend, implementation, hardware, and seed count differ, but then attributes wall-clock time to the critic. That is causal language from an unmatched experiment. Also, the PPO result is not in Table 1, so the reader cannot audit it in the main result table.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> In this unmatched case study, PPO on Modal H100 reaches 81.2% last-10 accuracy, while the Tinker GRPO row reaches 84.4%. Because backend, batching, implementation, hardware, and seed count differ, we do not attribute either the accuracy gap or the 1.8× wall-clock difference to the critic alone.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 12. **Major — xLAM strict-reward collapse and relaxed-reward success are being blended**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 9, §4.5: “every prompt sits at ZVF=1 from step 1 onward while the batch reward mean stays at 0…” Same paragraph: “On the full xLAM-60k run with the relaxed schema reward, GRPO with G=8 achieves 94.2% schema-validity at training convergence… held-out schema-validity plateaus at 89.4%.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The strict reward produces all-zero learning signal; the relaxed reward produces high schema validity. Those are different reward functions. Without defining the relaxed reward, split construction, baseline protocol, and variance, the 94.2/89.4 numbers are not interpretable as evidence about the strict schema-validity setting.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The xLAM experiments use two different reward predicates. Under the strict schema-validity reward, the 35-prompt probe collapses immediately with ZVF=1 and mean reward 0. A separate relaxed-reward run reaches 94.2% training schema-validity and 89.4% held-out schema-validity; because the reward predicate differs, this run is evidence that reward design can avoid the all-wrong regime, not evidence that strict schema-validity GRPO succeeds.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 13. **Major — The xLAM probe has a grouping ambiguity: 10 rollouts per prompt but \\\\(G=8\\\\)**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 5, §3.2: “We use a fixed seed-sampled 35-prompt subset with 10 rollouts each for the saturation analysis…” p. 9, §4.5: “35 prompts, 10 rollouts per prompt, G=8, 30 steps.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** GRPO groups \\\\(G\\\\) completions per prompt. If there are 10 rollouts per prompt and \\\\(G=8\\\\), the paper must say which 8 are grouped, whether the remaining 2 are discarded, whether groups overlap, or whether “10 rollouts” refers to evaluation and not training. Otherwise, the ZVF estimate is not reproducible.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> For the xLAM saturation probe, each training group contains exactly \\\\(G=8\\\\) completions per prompt. The “10 rollouts” count refers only to the diagnostic evaluation pass; ZVF is computed from the 8 completions used by the GRPO update. Diagnostic rollouts are not mixed into the training groups.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 14. **Major — The 14B regression is likely an evaluation/configuration failure, but the table makes it look like a scale result**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 7, Table 1: “Qwen3-14B — Tinker GRPO, G=8 — 0.437 — 0.750.” p. 9, §4.6: “We therefore report it as evidence that this 14B Tinker configuration failed under inherited 8B defaults, not as a generic failure of GRPO at scale.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** You have no Qwen3-14B base row, no matched 14B control, one seed, and a closed backend. A 14B model falling far below an 8B base screams “configuration/evaluation bug” unless heavily documented. The text hedges, but Table 1 still places the row in “Main GSM8K results.”\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The Qwen3-14B row is excluded from main comparisons. We report it only as a failed single-run configuration under inherited 8B defaults; without a 14B base evaluation, replicated seeds, and a matched evaluator, it cannot support any scale-level conclusion.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 15. **Major — Reward hacking and length-bias claims are asserted from a correlation threshold without examples or controls**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 4, §2.4: “For verifiable rewards the failure mode manifests differently: length bias, where models learn to produce longer outputs that satisfy formatting requirements without improving reasoning quality.” p. 8, §4.2: “The G=2 curve exhibits the characteristic ‘reward spike then collapse’ pattern associated with reward hacking, accompanied by the reward-stability coefficient rising above 0.6 at step 15.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** A length–reward correlation over a rolling 20-step window is not enough to diagnose reward hacking. It may reflect answer formatting, longer correct solutions, truncation, prompt mix, or evaluation artifacts. Recent GRPO literature specifically discusses length and optimization biases, so a reviewer will expect more careful positioning. citeturn420383search1\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> We use the reward-stability coefficient as an alert, not as a definitive reward-hacking diagnosis. In the \\\\(G=2\\\\) run, rising length--reward correlation coincides with a reward spike and subsequent collapse, but without trajectory-level examples and matched controls we describe this as a collapse-like length-correlation pattern rather than proof of reward hacking.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 16. **Major — Model and run configuration are not specified tightly enough for reproducibility**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 4, §3.1: “All experiments use LoRA adapters with rank 8–64…” p. 5, §3.3: “All runs use AdamW with learning rate \\\\(5 \\\\times 10^{-5}\\\\), linear warmup over 10% of steps, and a maximum sequence length of 512 tokens.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** “Rank 8–64” is a range, not a configuration. The paper does not list exact model identifiers, LoRA rank per row, batch size, rollout temperature/top-p, prompt template, thinking mode, evaluation decoding, truncation handling, or run IDs. For Qwen3 specifically, prompt template and thinking/non-thinking mode can change math behavior materially.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> Each result row is tied to a run manifest containing the exact model identifier, adapter rank, target modules, seed, batch size, group size, rollout temperature, top-\\\\(p\\\\), maximum generation length, prompt template, evaluator commit, and reward script. Rows lacking a complete manifest are treated as descriptive and are not used for quantitative comparisons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 17. **Major — GSM8K answer extraction may be measuring format compliance as much as math accuracy**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 5, §3.2: “We extract the gold answer … with the pattern `####\\\\s*([\\\\-\\\\d,\\\\.]+)` and the model’s answer from generated text with `####\\\\s*(.+?)(?:\\\\s|$)`…”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** If a model gives the correct number without the `####` delimiter, does it receive reward 0? If so, early RL may be optimizing delimiter compliance. If the regex captures only the first token after `####`, decimal/negative/fraction formatting matters. Reviewers will ask for missing-answer handling and a separate format-compliance metric.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> If a generated answer lacks the `####` delimiter, it is scored as missing and receives reward 0. We therefore report exact-match accuracy together with delimiter-compliance rate in the artifact traces, and we interpret GSM8K reward as “format-normalised exact match” rather than pure mathematical correctness.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 18. **Moderate — The SFT baseline is introduced but not reported coherently**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 5, §3.3: “A standard cross-entropy fine-tuning baseline is run on chain-of-thought demonstrations from the GSM8K training set.” p. 9, §4.5: “compared to 78.5% for the SFT baseline on the same number of gradient steps.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The methods introduce a GSM8K SFT baseline, but Table 1 has no GSM8K SFT row. The only SFT number appears in xLAM, where “same number of gradient steps” is not the same token or sample budget as GRPO. This creates a dangling baseline.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> SFT is used only as a descriptive xLAM baseline in this paper; we do not report or use a GSM8K SFT baseline for the main claims. For xLAM, the SFT comparison is matched by optimizer-update count, not by generated-token budget, and should be interpreted as a same-update-count baseline.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 19. **Moderate — Main claims depend on supplementary file paths rather than in-paper evidence**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 7, §4.2: “Training reward curves for all group sizes are reported in our supplementary material (`figures/v2/group_size_ablation.pdf`).” p. 8, §4.3: “A ZVF heat-map across all Tinker experiments is provided in the supplementary material (`figures/v2/zvf_heatmap`).”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The paper has no figures. For an empirical diagnostic paper, the ZVF scatter/heatmap and the group-size curves are central evidence, not optional decoration. File paths are not stable supplementary citations, especially in a camera-ready LNCS PDF.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> The main text reports only claims supported by the tables in this paper. Training curves, ZVF heatmaps, and scatter plots are archived with the artifact snapshot and are treated as diagnostic visualisations rather than independent evidence for the main conclusions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 20. **Moderate — “One of the first systematic multi-backend comparisons” is not supported by the design**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 3, §2.2: “Our work benchmarks these implementations against each other and against the closed-source Tinker SDK, providing one of the first systematic multi-backend comparisons.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The comparison is not systematic: different hardware, seed counts, implementations, and possibly models. Also, Tinker is closed, and the paper itself says it cannot isolate backend effects. This sentence invites a reviewer to reject the framing as overclaiming.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> We report a small multi-backend case study spanning Tinker, veRL/Modal, and TRL/L4 configurations. Because the configurations are not fully matched, we use these runs to expose reproducibility and configuration sensitivity rather than to rank backends.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 21. **Moderate — The ethics mitigation about LoRA adapters is weak**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 10–11, Broader Impact: “we mitigate by releasing adapters rather than merged weights…”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** LoRA adapters are easily merged with base weights. Releasing adapters rather than merged weights may reduce convenience, but it is not a meaningful misuse mitigation unless access controls or model-card restrictions are present.\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> Releasing LoRA adapters rather than merged weights reduces distribution size but should not be treated as a strong misuse mitigation, since adapters can be merged with the corresponding base checkpoints. Our primary mitigation is transparent reporting of failure modes and diagnostics rather than access restriction.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 22. **Minor — The license statement in the PDF should not say MIT unless the repository is actually MIT**\\n\\n**Quote.** p. 2, Contributions: “We release scripts, LoRA adapters, step-level traces, manifests, and diagnostic utilities under MIT License…” p. 11, Broader Impact: “All datasets used are publicly licensed; GSM8K under MIT and xLAM-60k under CC BY 4.0.”\\n\\n**Why a reviewer will hit this.** The current artifact metadata and license file show Apache-2.0 for the repository, not MIT. This is easy to fix, but leaving it in creates a low-effort credibility hit. citeturn731793view0turn731793view1\\n\\n**Paste-ready fix.**  \\n> We release the code and diagnostic artifacts under the license stated in the artifact repository, and we report dataset licenses separately from code licenses.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3) AUDIT BEFORE SUBMISSION\\n\\n### Numbers and statistics to re-check\\n\\nThe **highest-priority audit** is the mismatch between the PDF and artifact. The PDF’s Table 1 says Qwen3-8B/Tinker/G=8 has 0.844 last-10 and 1.000 peak, while the public reproduction document says the headline Qwen3-8B/GSM8K result is 34.4% last-10 and 62.5% peak. That must be reconciled before submission. citeturn731793view2\\n\\nThe **TRL model identity** must be fixed. The methods say “Qwen2.5-0.5B,” while Table 1 says “Qwen3-8B.” This single inconsistency invalidates the 14 pp platform-gap discussion as written.\\n\\nThe **correlation p-values appear arithmetically plausible**, but the sample definitions are not. For \\\\(r=-0.769, N=15\\\\), two-sided \\\\(p \\\\approx 0.0008\\\\), matching the paper. For \\\\(r=+0.40, N=19\\\\), two-sided \\\\(p \\\\approx 0.09\\\\), so “marginal” is fair. For \\\\(r=+0.13, N=7\\\\), \\\\(p \\\\approx 0.78\\\\). The problem is not the p-value arithmetic; it is that “pooled N=15” and “within-GSM8K N=19” are presented as if they are nested.\\n\\nThe **five-seed control should include per-seed values**. With \\\\(\\\\Delta=+1.3\\\\) pp, \\\\(t=1.32\\\\), \\\\(n=5\\\\), the implied standard deviation of seed-level differences is about 2.2 pp and the approximate 95% CI is \\\\([-1.4,+4.0]\\\\) pp. That makes the correct takeaway “null / bounded effect,” not “GRPO improves.”\\n\\nThe **peak accuracy values must be relabeled or removed**. If they are held-out GSM8K full-test accuracies, 1.000 is extraordinary. If they are online training reward or small-checkpoint maxima, they should not sit beside held-out accuracy in Table 1.\\n\\nThe **PPO 81.2% number should be put in a table or removed**. It currently appears only in prose and is then used for a GRPO/PPO comparison that the paper admits is unmatched.\\n\\nThe **xLAM 94.2% / 89.4% numbers need a table** with reward predicate, train/held-out split, seed, prompt count, rollout count, decoding, SFT baseline protocol, and whether schema validity is exact JSON parsing, function-name accuracy, argument presence, or type correctness.\\n\\nThe **group-size ablation needs seed variance**. With one seed, Table 2 can support only “observed in this run,” not “G=8 is a default.”\\n\\nThe **artifact license must be synchronized**. The paper says MIT; the current artifact metadata/license says Apache-2.0. citeturn731793view0turn731793view1\\n\\n### Claims that are statistically unsupported as written\\n\\n“G=8 gives the best practical default” is unsupported beyond a single-seed Tinker/Qwen3-8B/GSM8K observation.\\n\\n“Reward hacking” is unsupported without trajectory examples, matched controls, or an explicit reward-exploit mechanism. The current evidence is a rolling correlation and a curve referenced outside the PDF.\\n\\n“The platform gap” is unsupported as a backend conclusion. The paper can say “configuration gap,” but not backend performance.\\n\\n“14B regression” is unsupported as a scale claim. It is one failed run with no 14B base row.\\n\\n“Tool-use generalisation” is unsupported unless the strict and relaxed xLAM rewards are separated. The strict setup collapses; the relaxed setup succeeds. Those are different experiments.\\n\\n“ZVF predicts / diagnoses accuracy” is already weakened in the paper, but the abstract still makes it sound central. The safe claim is: ZVF detects all-identical reward groups and is useful for logging collapse/saturation regimes.\\n\\n### Missing baselines a serious reviewer will expect\\n\\nAdd or explicitly waive a **matched open-stack GRPO baseline**: same Qwen3-8B model, same GSM8K reward extractor, same seeds, same held-out evaluator, same group size, and ideally same hardware class.\\n\\nAdd **base rows for every model in Table 1**, especially Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-14B. Without base rows, the 4B and 14B GRPO rows do not say whether RL helped, hurt, or exposed an evaluator issue.\\n\\nAdd a **dynamic-sampling / zero-variance filtering baseline**. Since the paper’s diagnostic is exactly the all-correct/all-wrong GRPO issue, reviewers will expect comparison to DAPO-style filtering or at least a simple “drop zero-variance groups” ablation. DAPO explicitly filters prompts with accuracy 0 or 1 to keep effective gradients. citeturn912241view0\\n\\nAdd **RLOO or REINFORCE-style critic-free baseline** if the paper discusses critic-free methods. You cite RLOO, but no RLOO experiment appears.\\n\\nAdd a **matched PPO baseline** or remove comparative PPO language. Current PPO vs GRPO differs in backend and hardware.\\n\\nAdd **SFT GSM8K baseline** or delete the GSM8K SFT method sentence. The paper currently introduces SFT but does not report it for GSM8K.\\n\\nAdd **strict-vs-relaxed xLAM baselines**: strict SFT, relaxed SFT, strict GRPO, relaxed GRPO, and possibly a partial-credit reward. Right now the xLAM result is not cleanly interpretable.\\n\\nAdd a **format-compliance metric** for GSM8K. Because the reward extractor expects `####`, report delimiter compliance separately from numeric exact match.\\n\\n### Prior work an expert reviewer will expect cited or more directly acknowledged\\n\\nDAPO should be discussed not just as “revisiting clipping and group-sampling defaults,” but as direct prior art on the zero-advantage/all-correct/all-wrong condition. Its dynamic sampling section explicitly identifies zero advantage and filters accuracy-0/accuracy-1 prompt groups. citeturn912241view0\\n\\nDr. GRPO / “Understanding R1-Zero-Like Training” should be cited in the length-bias discussion, because it directly analyzes GRPO optimization bias and progressively longer incorrect responses. citeturn420383search1\\n\\nFor function calling, BFCL should be cited or at least mentioned as the standard evaluation context. xLAM is a valid dataset choice, but a reviewer in tool-use/function-calling will expect BFCL-style AST/execution-validity framing, especially when the paper reports only schema validity. citeturn457329search9\\n\\nFor artifacts/backends, cite the actual veRL/TRL/OpenRLHF/SkyRL stack used and pin versions. Right now “Tinker SDK v0.16.1,” “Modal H100,” and “TRL on NVIDIA L4” are not sufficient to reproduce the optimizer semantics.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4) AC RECOMMENDATION\\n\\n**Reject** — the paper has an interesting diagnostic framing, but the current camera-ready candidate is not empirically audit-stable: internal model/baseline contradictions, artifact-result mismatch, mixed metrics, and underpowered unmatched comparisons make the main claims unreviewable at an LNCS-tier venue.\",\"ct\":1777872534.664354}]},{\"id\":\"69f810ac-5450-83e8-b9cc-a5fa4ea384ad\",\"title\":\"RL Fine-Tuning for Enhanced Model Performance\",\"create_time\":1777864877.53601,\"update_time\":1777865213.15713,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nTitle workshop. The current title is on line 38 of the .tex. Generate 10 alternative titles, each: (a) ≤ 12 words, (b) precise (no buzzwords), (c) signaling the actual contribution. For each, give a 1-sentence rationale and rank from strongest to weakest. End with a recommendation that takes into account search/citation discoverability for an RL-fine-tuning audience.\",\"ct\":1777