enchmark (GSM8K for arithmetic/group size/backends, xLAM-60k for tool use); resolve by repeating each claim on a second benchmark. \\\\textbf{(L7)} Evaluation is automated and outcome-level only: we do not run human evaluation or inspect reasoning traces/process quality; resolve with blind human ratings and trace-level error coding. Until then, claims are configuration-descriptive, not population-level.'''\\nwords=re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)?|\\\\\\\\[A-Za-z]+|\\\\$[^$]+\\\\$\\\", snip)\\nprint(len(words))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864956.296246},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\norig='''After analysis, we judge the marginal misuse risk acceptable relative to the reproducibility and pedagogical value of the release, and we mitigate by releasing adapters rather than merged weights and by publishing the ZVF and stability diagnostics that would allow downstream users to detect reward hacking.'''\\nprop='''We expect the marginal misuse risk to be limited for the released adapters, but this is not a formal safety evaluation. We mitigate by releasing adapters rather than merged weights and by publishing ZVF/stability logs as warning signals for downstream audit, not certified detectors of reward hacking.'''\\nfor t in [orig,prop]: print(len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)?\\\", t)))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864973.017191},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\nprop='''We expect the marginal misuse risk to be limited for released adapters, but this is not a formal safety evaluation. We mitigate by releasing adapters rather than merged weights and by publishing ZVF/stability logs as audit warning signals, not certified reward-hacking detectors.'''\\nprint(len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)?\\\", prop)))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864985.626222},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\nprop=r'''We presented a stratified audit of advantage-collapse diagnostics in GRPO, embedded in a targeted empirical study of critic-free RLVR on Qwen3 backbones for exact-match arithmetic and schema-valid function calling. The evidence supports four configuration-level takeaways: (1) in a single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation, $G{=}8$ achieved the best observed last-10 accuracy among the tested group sizes; (2) ZVF is a cheap descriptive sentinel for all-wrong/all-correct advantage collapse, not a standalone reward-hacking detector; (3) the tested Tinker and TRL configurations differ substantially, but the gap cannot be attributed to backend alone; and (4) a single xLAM-60k run suggests GRPO can improve schema validity with modest reward engineering. We release the artefacts to support replication and broader tests.'''\\nprint(len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)?|\\\\$[^$]+\\\\$\\\", prop)))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864991.013202},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nlines=Path('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex').read_text().splitlines()\\ndef count(t):\\n    return len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)?|\\\\\\\\[A-Za-z]+|\\\\$[^$]+\\\\$\\\", t))\\norig_5_1='\\\\n'.join(lines[581:599]) # 582-599\\nprop_5_1=r'''In the single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation, accuracy is non-monotonic across the tested group sizes ($G{=}2,4,8,16$): it improves up to $G{=}8$ and declines at $G{=}16$. This pattern is compatible with a bias--variance account, but the present design cannot identify the mechanism. The $G{=}2$ run shows collapse-like low-entropy dynamics and a reward--length correlation; the $G{=}16$ run is consistent with signal suppression near ability-boundary prompts. Within this setup, $G{=}8$ is the best observed operating point, not a population-level optimum.\\n\\nAs a practical default for similar Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker-style settings, $G{=}8$ is a reasonable value to try first. On harder tasks or lower-starting policies, $G{=}4$ is better framed as an ablation point than as a recommendation.'''\\nprint('5.1 orig', count(orig_5_1), 'prop', count(prop_5_1), 'delta', count(prop_5_1)-count(orig_5_1))\\norig_lim='\\\\n'.join(lines[627:642]) # 628-642\\nprop_lim=r'''Each limitation below names a minimal resolving experiment. \\\\textbf{(L1)} All runs use LoRA; resolve with matched full fine-tuning. \\\\textbf{(L2)} Tinker case studies are single-seed; resolve with $\\\\geq 3$ seeds. \\\\textbf{(L3)} Tinker horizons are short (30--50 steps); resolve with $\\\\geq 500$-step checkpoint evaluation. \\\\textbf{(L4)} Benchmark coverage is narrow; resolve with additional math and tool-use distribution-shift tests. \\\\textbf{(L5)} All backbones are Qwen3 variants, so model-family generality is untested; resolve with matched runs on at least one non-Qwen family. \\\\textbf{(L6)} Each major claim rests on one primary benchmark (GSM8K for arithmetic/group size/backends, xLAM-60k for tool use); resolve by repeating each claim on a second benchmark. \\\\textbf{(L7)} Evaluation is automated and outcome-level only: we do not run human evaluation or inspect reasoning traces/process quality; resolve with blind human ratings and trace-level error coding. Until then, claims are configuration-descriptive, not population-level.'''\\nprint('lim orig', count(orig_lim), 'prop', count(prop_lim), 'delta', count(prop_lim)-count(orig_lim))\\norig_impact='\\\\n'.join(lines[651:656]) # 652-656\\nprop_impact='''We expect the marginal misuse risk to be limited for released adapters, but this is not a formal safety evaluation. We mitigate by releasing adapters rather than merged weights and by publishing ZVF/stability logs as audit warning signals, not certified reward-hacking detectors.'''\\nprint('impact orig', count(orig_impact), 'prop', count(prop_impact), 'delta', count(prop_impact)-count(orig_impact))\\norig_conc='\\\\n'.join(lines[662:675])\\nprop_conc=r'''We presented a stratified audit of advantage-collapse diagnostics in GRPO, embedded in a targeted empirical study of critic-free RLVR on Qwen3 backbones for exact-match arithmetic and schema-valid function calling. The evidence supports four configuration-level takeaways: (1) in a single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation, $G{=}8$ achieved the best observed last-10 accuracy among the tested group sizes; (2) ZVF is a cheap descriptive sentinel for all-wrong/all-correct advantage collapse, not a standalone reward-hacking detector; (3) the tested Tinker and TRL configurations differ substantially, but the gap cannot be attributed to backend alone; and (4) a single xLAM-60k run suggests GRPO can improve schema validity with modest reward engineering. We release the artefacts to support replication and broader tests.'''\\nprint('conc orig', count(orig_conc), 'prop', count(prop_conc), 'delta', count(prop_conc)-count(orig_conc))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864999.039554},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc mkdir -p /mnt/data/rendered_pages && pdftoppm -f 9 -l 11 -png -r 100 /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/rendered_pages/page >/tmp/pdftoppm.log 2>&1 && ls -l /mnt/data/rendered_pages\",\"ct\":1777865033.527115},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"Section 7 already has several caveats, so I’m narrowing the audit to remaining overclaims in conclusion/practitioner language and group-size claims, especially beyond Qwen, GSM8K/xLAM, and no-human-eval scope.\",\"ct\":1777865069.370685},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"All line numbers below refer to the uploaded `.tex`. I cross-checked the rendered PDF: it is exactly 12 pages, with Section 5 spanning pages 9–11, so the edit plan below is replacement-only and does not touch the author block.\\n\\n## Overclaiming audit for Section 7, lines 578–684\\n\\n| Lines | Phrase to soften | Why it overclaims | Safer replacement |\\n|---:|---|---|---|\\n| 582–584 | “**The non-monotonic relationship between \\\\(G\\\\) and accuracy** … **is consistent with a bias--variance trade-off**” | Sounds like a general relationship. The evidence is a single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation. “Bias–variance” is plausible but not directly measured. | “In the single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation, last-10 accuracy is non-monotonic over the tested \\\\(G\\\\) values; this pattern is compatible with, but does not identify, an advantage signal/noise trade-off.” |\\n| 585–588 | “**the single-bit difference … provides too coarse a signal**” | Causal mechanism is asserted from one run. | “the \\\\(G{=}2\\\\) setting may provide a noisier group-normalised signal in this run” |\\n| 586–587 | “**the advantage estimator has high variance**” | Estimator variance is not directly estimated or reported. | “the observed behaviour is compatible with a high-variance advantage estimate” |\\n| 587–588 | “**the policy collapses prematurely to a degenerate, low-entropy mode dominated by length-biased outputs**” | “Dominated by” implies output-level/trace analysis. The paper reports diagnostics, not a reasoning-trace or human evaluation. | “logs show a collapse-like low-entropy regime with increased length--reward correlation” |\\n| 589–591 | “**the reward distribution within each group becomes nearly uniform for prompts near the model’s current ability boundary, driving ZVF high and reducing effective gradient**” | This infers prompt-level ability-boundary dynamics and causality that are not isolated. | “for \\\\(G{=}16\\\\), higher ZVF coincides with lower last-10 accuracy; by Eq. (3), this is consistent with more zero-variance groups contributing little gradient signal.” |\\n| 592–593 | “**\\\\(G{=}8\\\\) appears to sit at a sweet spot**” | “Sweet spot” reads like a general recommendation. | “\\\\(G{=}8\\\\) is the best observed setting among the four values tested in this bounded run.” |\\n| 595 | “**This finding has practical implications**” | Too strong for a single-seed ablation. | “This result is useful as a tuning hypothesis.” |\\n| 595–597 | “**consistent with the recommended choice in prior TinkerRL deployments**” | Unsupported in this section and could invite requests for a citation or disclosure. | “matches the default used in our Tinker runs” or delete. |\\n| 597–599 | “**practitioners applying GRPO to harder tasks … may benefit from \\\\(G{=}4\\\\)**” | Gives prescriptive guidance beyond the tested benchmark/model family. | “lower-accuracy tasks should include smaller values such as \\\\(G{=}4\\\\) in a sweep before assuming \\\\(G{=}8\\\\) or larger is appropriate.” |\\n| 612–614 | “**Three mechanisms could in principle account for the gap; each names the controlled experiment that would test it**” | “Would test it” sounds definitive; the proposed experiments would help isolate factors, not fully resolve them. | “Three plausible mechanisms could explain the gap; each suggests an experiment that would help isolate it.” |\\n| 614–617 | “**Server-side reward normalisation undisclosed by Tinker**” | Implies a specific undisclosed mechanism. Safer to frame as possible implementation opacity. | “implementation-specific reward normalisation or scoring differences in Tinker’s closed implementation” |\\n| 620–621 | “**with all other settings fixed**” | Backend/hardware comparisons rarely hold literally everything fixed. | “with the remaining settings matched as closely as possible” |\\n| 628–630 | “**the concrete experiment that would resolve it**” | A single follow-up rarely resolves a limitation completely. | “a follow-up that would reduce, not eliminate, the uncertainty” |\\n| 630–640 | Repeated “**Resolved by:**” labels | Same issue: too definitive. | Replace with “Test with:” or fold into shorter clauses. |\\n| 648–651 | “**We assess four misuse pathways**” plus “**capability uplift for adversarial mathematical reasoning**,” “**circumventing safety training**,” “**adversarial fine-tuning**” | “Assess” implies a safety evaluation. The paper has no human/deployment-risk evaluation. | “We briefly discuss four possible misuse pathways: math capability uplift, reward-hacking amplification, misuse of tool-call distillation, and easier compute-efficient fine-tuning.” |\\n| 652–653 | “**we judge the marginal misuse risk acceptable**” | Normative conclusion without formal human/safety evaluation. | “we expect the marginal misuse risk to be limited, but have not performed a deployment-risk or human-safety evaluation” |\\n| 654–656 | “**diagnostics that would allow downstream users to detect reward hacking**” | Overstates diagnostic validation. ZVF is framed elsewhere as descriptive, not a standalone detector. | “diagnostics that may help downstream users flag zero-gradient or length-bias symptoms in similar settings” |\\n| 663–666 | “**a systematic study … for LLM reasoning across multiple model families, group sizes, and execution backends**” | Main experiments use Qwen-family/Qwen3 backbones; “multiple model families” is not supported. “LLM reasoning” is broader than GSM8K/xLAM automatic rewards. | “a bounded empirical study of Qwen-family LoRA GRPO on GSM8K and xLAM-60k across tested group sizes and execution backends” |\\n| 667–668 | “**GRPO with \\\\(G{=}8\\\\) achieves the best accuracy--compute trade-off on GSM8K**” | Single-seed group-size ablation; compute trade-off is implied by \\\\(G\\\\), not rigorously measured. | “\\\\(G{=}8\\\\) is the best observed group size in the single-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation” |\\n| 668–669 | “**ZVF is an effective early indicator of reward hacking**” | No standalone predictive validation, no human eval, no reasoning-trace analysis. | “ZVF is a cheap descriptive sentinel for zero-gradient groups and, with length--reward diagnostics, can flag collapse-like runs” |\\n| 669–671 | “**backend choice is associated with a substantial performance gap between open-source and closed-source GRPO implementations**” | Better to emphasize configuration-level confounding, consistent with lines 603–624. | “the tested Tinker and TRL configurations differ substantially, but the root cause is not isolated” |\\n| 672–673 | “**GRPO benefits transfer from arithmetic to structured function-calling with minimal reward engineering**” | One xLAM-60k schema-validity run; no broader tool-use or human-quality validation. | “one xLAM-60k run suggests that simple schema-validity rewards can support GRPO-style function-calling training” |\\n| 674–675 | “**future research on efficient RL for language model reasoning**” | Broad “reasoning” claim without trace-level evidence. | “future tests of RLVR/GRPO diagnostics and efficient fine-tuning” |\\n\\n## Three limitations to add, page-neutral\\n\\nThe three missing limitations I would add are:\\n\\n1. **Single base-model family.** The main claims are Qwen-family/Qwen3-local, not evidence across model families.\\n2. **Automatic evaluation only / no human evaluation.** GSM8K exact match and xLAM schema validity do not establish human-perceived usefulness, safety, or reasoning quality.\\n3. **No reasoning-trace analysis.** The paper measures final-answer/schema rewards and logging diagnostics, but does not code or evaluate solution trajectories.\\n\\nThe most page-safe insertion point is the existing limitations paragraph. Replace lines **628–642** with the block below. This adds the three limitations while deleting the “Resolved by:” boilerplate; the replacement is slightly shorter than the current paragraph.\\n\\n```latex\\nEach limitation below names a follow-up that would reduce, not eliminate,\\nthe uncertainty. \\\\textbf{(L1)} LoRA-only training; test with matched-compute\\nfull fine tuning. \\\\textbf{(L2)} Tinker case studies are single-seed; rerun\\nQwen3-8B/$G{=}8$ with $\\\\geq 3$ seeds. \\\\textbf{(L3)} Horizons are short\\n(30--50 Tinker steps); extend to $\\\\geq 500$ steps with checkpoint evaluation.\\n\\\\textbf{(L4)} Claims are benchmark-local: group size, backend, and scaling\\nare tested on GSM8K, while transfer is tested on xLAM-60k; add\\nGSM8K-Plus/MATH and a held-out schema family. \\\\textbf{(L5)} All base models\\nare Qwen-family checkpoints; replicate on at least one non-Qwen backbone.\\n\\\\textbf{(L6)} Evaluation is automatic only; add human or expert review for\\nusefulness and safety of generated traces/tool calls. \\\\textbf{(L7)} We do\\nnot analyse reasoning traces; add trajectory-level coding of failure modes\\nbefore making claims about reasoning quality. Until then, claims should be\\nread as configuration-level descriptions.\\n```\\n\\n## Page-neutral deletion/replacement plan\\n\\nTo keep the 12-page LNCS limit, make these replacements rather than inserting standalone prose:\\n\\n| Delete/replace lines | Purpose | Page effect |\\n|---:|---|---|\\n| 582–599 | Replace group-size interpretation with a single-run, benchmark-local framing. | Shorter. |\\n| 612–624 | Replace platform-gap hypotheses with “help isolate” language and remove “undisclosed by Tinker.” | Shorter. |\\n| 628–642 | Replace limitations paragraph with compressed L1–L7 block above. | Slightly shorter while adding 3 limitations. |\\n| 648–656 | Replace broader-impact risk language with “briefly discuss” and “may help flag” wording. | Shorter. |\\n| 663–675 | Replace conclusion with bounded Qwen-family/GSM8K/xLAM framing. | Slightly shorter. |\\n\\nA compact replacement for lines **663–675** would be:\\n\\n```latex\\nWe presented a stratified audit of advantage-collapse diagnostics in GRPO,\\nembedded in a bounded empirical study of Qwen-family LoRA GRPO on GSM8K and\\nxLAM-60k across tested group sizes and execution backends. The results show\\nthat: (1) $G{=}8$ is the best observed group size in the single-seed\\nQwen3-8B/GSM8K Tinker ablation; (2) ZVF is a cheap descriptive sentinel for\\nzero-gradient groups and, with length--reward diagnostics, can flag\\ncollapse-like runs; (3) the tested Tinker and TRL configurations differ\\nsubstantially, but the root cause is not isolated; and (4) one xLAM-60k run\\nsuggests that simple schema-validity rewards can support GRPO-style\\nfunction-calling training. We release all artefacts to support replication and\\nfurther tests of these hypotheses.\\n```\\n\\nThis preserves the paper’s punch: it still claims a reproducible, stratified GRPO diagnostic audit, but no longer invites rejection for overgeneralizing beyond Qwen-family models, GSM8K/xLAM, automatic rewards, and diagnostic logs.\",\"ct\":1777865457.356198}]},{\"id\":\"69f8106f-c50c-83e8-96c2-82dba72921d4\",\"title\":\"Introduction Restructure TeX\",\"create_time\":1777864817.438311,\"update_time\":1777865232.394868,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nRestructure the Introduction (Section 1, lines 110–177). Diagnose its current weaknesses (hook strength, motivation cohesion, contribution clarity, transition into Related Work). Provide a full TeX rewrite of the Introduction that fits the same vertical space (count lines). Use a 4-move structure: hook → tension → contribution list → roadmap. Show the rewrite as a ```diff block.\",\"ct\":1777864815.659},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && echo '---LINES---' && nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '100,190p' && echo '---PDFINFO---' && pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | 