] # 110-173\\nprint(len(intro), sum(len(l) for l in intro), sum(len(l.split()) for l in intro))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864869.579192},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat > /mnt/data/new_intro.tex <<'EOF'\\n\\\\section{Introduction}\\\\label{sec:intro}\\n\\nReasoning-capable language models are increasingly judged by tasks whose\\noutcomes can be checked automatically: solving arithmetic problems, emitting\\ncorrect code, or producing schema-valid tool calls. Such settings make\\nreinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR) attractive because the\\nreward can be computed by a deterministic checker rather than by an annotated\\nchain-of-thought trace or a learned preference model. For resource-constrained\\nlabs, however, the practical question is not only whether RLVR works at scale,\\nbut how critic-free RLVR behaves under the choices that dominate day-to-day\\ntraining: model size, backend, and number of sampled completions.\\n\\nThat question remains open for Group Relative Policy Optimisation\\n(GRPO)~\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}. PPO~\\\\cite{schulman2017proximal}, the\\nstandard policy-gradient method in RLHF pipelines, trains a value network\\nalongside the policy, increasing memory pressure and implementation complexity.\\nGRPO removes this critic by sampling $G$ completions per prompt and normalising\\neach reward relative to its group. The same simplification creates a diagnostic\\ntension: when all completions in a group receive the same binary reward, the\\nwithin-group advantage is zero and the update carries no preference signal.\\nAlthough GRPO is now exposed through open implementations~\\\\cite{openrlhf2024,\\ntrl2020,verl2024}, its sensitivity to group size, backend, and\\nadvantage-collapse diagnostics is still under-measured across models and tasks.\\n\\n\\\\textbf{Contributions.}\\nThis paper makes the following contributions:\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Backend comparison.} We run GRPO and