results. Section~\\\\ref{sec:discussion} interprets the limits of the\\nclaims and concludes.\\nEOF\\npython - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nold=Path('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex').read_text().splitlines()[109:173]\\nnew=Path('/mnt/data/new_intro.tex').read_text().splitlines()\\nprint('old lines/ chars/ words:', len(old), sum(len(l) for l in old), sum(len(l.split()) for l in old))\\nprint('new lines/ chars/ words:', len(new), sum(len(l) for l in new), sum(len(l.split()) for l in new))\\nfor i,l in enumerate(new,110): print(f'{i:3d}: {l}')\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864896.907318},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc which pdflatex || true; which latexmk || true\",\"ct\":1777864914.746563},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc command -v tectonic || true; command -v xelatex || true; command -v lualatex || true; command -v pdftotext || true\",\"ct\":1777864923.598458},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python - <<'PY'\\nimport subprocess, re\\ntext=subprocess.check_output(['pdftotext','-layout','/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf','-']).decode('utf-8',errors='ignore')\\n# Isolate from 1 Introduction to 2 Related Work\\nm=re.search(r'1\\\\s+Introduction\\\\n(.*?)\\\\n\\\\s*2\\\\s+Related Work', text, re.S)\\nprint(bool(m))\\nintro=m.group(1)\\nlines=intro.splitlines()\\nfor i,l in enumerate(lines,1): print(f'{i:02d}: {l}')\\nprint('nonblank lines',sum(1 for l in lines if l.strip()), 'chars',len(intro), 'words',len(re.findall(r'\\\\S+',intro)))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864926.241541},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat > /mnt/data/new_intro2.tex <<'EOF'\\n\\\\section{Introduction}\\\\label{sec:intro}\\n\\nReasoning-capable LLMs are increasingly evaluated on tasks whose outcomes can\\nbe checked automatically: arithmetic, code generation, and schema-valid tool\\ncalls. These settings make reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards\\n(RLVR) attractive because the reward can be computed by a deterministic checker,\\nwithout human-preference data or annotated chain-of-thought traces. For\\nresource-constrained labs, the practical question is therefore not only whether\\nRLVR works at scale, but which critic-free training choices remain stable when\\nmodel, backend, and sampling budget change.\\n\\nGroup Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO)~\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} is a\\npromising but under-characterised answer. PPO~\\\\cite{schulman2017proximal}, the\\nstandard policy-gradient method in RLHF pipelines, trains a separate value\\nnetwork and therefore adds memory and engineering overhead. GRPO removes this\\ncritic by sampling $G$ completions per prompt and normalising each reward\\nrelative to its group. The simplification creates a diagnostic tension: when all\\ncompletions in a group receive the same binary reward, the within-group\\nadvantage is zero and the update carries no preference signal. Although GRPO is\\nnow available in open implementations~\\\\cite{openrlhf2024,trl2020,verl2024}, the\\ncombined effects of group size, backend, and advantage collapse remain\\nunder-measured across model families and tasks.\\n\\n\\\\textbf{Contributions.}\\nThis paper makes the following contributions:\\n\\\\begin{enumerate}\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Backend comparison.} We run GRPO and PPO-style configurations on\\n  Tinker SDK, Modal H100 with veRL, and TRL on NVIDIA L4, reporting observed\\n  configuration-level gaps while separating measurements from hardware and\\n  implementation hypotheses.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Group-size ablation.} We evaluate $G \\\\in \\\\{2, 4, 8, 16\\\\}$ on\\n  Qwen3-8B/GSM8K; the single-seed Tinker case study is consistent with\\n  $G{=}8$ as the best tested setting, while $G{=}2$ collapses early.\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Descriptive diagnostics.} We introduce the Zero-Variance Fraction\\n  (ZVF), give its binary-reward identity (Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:zvf_identity}), and pair\\n  it with