' /mnt/data/old_intro.tex /mnt/data/new_intro2.tex || true\",\"ct\":1777864955.900777},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve narrowed the edit to lines 110–173, with boundary comments at 175–177, and am checking contribution claims against later results to avoid unsupported attribution.\",\"ct\":1777865026.379822},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Diagnosis of current Introduction\\n\\n**Hook strength.** The opening is serviceable but generic: “multi-step reasoning is a key bottleneck” could introduce almost any LLM reasoning paper. It does not immediately foreground the paper’s sharper angle: verifiable binary rewards, critic-free GRPO, and zero-variance advantage collapse.\\n\\n**Motivation cohesion.** The current flow moves from SFT limitations → PPO memory cost → GRPO group size, but the through-line is not yet explicit. The real tension in the paper is not only “GRPO is cheaper than PPO”; it is that GRPO’s cheaper estimator can silently lose signal depending on group size, reward sparsity, backend, and task regime.\\n\\n**Contribution clarity.** Several contribution bullets overstate relative to the later Results/Discussion. In particular, “quantify the gap attributable to backend implementation versus algorithm choice” is stronger than the paper supports, because the Discussion later says the backend gap is confounded by implementation, hardware, and minibatching. Similarly, the group-size claim should be framed as a single-seed case study, and the xLAM claim should distinguish the all-wrong-collapse analysis from the relaxed-reward run.\\n\\n**Transition into Related Work.** The roadmap is boilerplate. Since Section 2 is organized around RLHF, RLVR/GRPO, group size, reward hacking, tool use, and scaling, the roadmap can do more work by previewing those lanes.\\n\\n## Line-budget check\\n\\nOriginal Introduction content, lines `110–173`: **64 source lines**, **60 nonblank**.  \\nProposed replacement: **56 source lines**, **52 nonblank**.  \\nNet effect: **8 source lines deleted**, with no new citations, no figures, and no author-block changes.\\n\\nApply this as a replacement for lines `110–173`; leave the separator comments and `\\\\section{Related Work}` unchanged.\\n\\n```diff\\n--- Conference_paper_springer.tex:110-173\\n+++ Conference_paper_springer.tex:110-165\\n@@ -1,64 +1,56 @@\\n \\\\section{Introduction}\\\\label{sec:intro}\\n \\n-The capacity for multi-step reasoning is a key bottleneck in deploying large\\n-language models (LLMs) in high-stakes settings such as code generation,\\n-mathematical problem solving, and autonomous tool use.\\n-Supervised fine-tuning on chain-of-thought demonstrations provides one\\n-path to improved reasoning, but it is bounded by the quality of available\\n-demonstrations and does not expose the model to exploration over novel\\n-solution strategies.\\n-Reinforcement learning (RL) from verifiable rewards offers an appealing\\n-complement: if a reward signal can be computed automatically (e.g., exact-match\\n-against a ground-truth answer), the model can be trained directly on task\\n-success without any annotated reasoning steps.\\n+Tasks such as arithmetic verification, code checking, and structured tool\\n+invocation have a useful property: success can often be judged by a deterministic\\n+procedure rather than by a learned preference model. This makes reinforcement\\n+learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR) attractive for reasoning-oriented LLM\\n+fine-tuning: the policy can explore candidate solutions and receive automatic\\n+feedback without annotated chain-of-thought traces. In this setting, the central\\n+question is not only whether RL improves accuracy, but whether the update signal\\n+remains usable under realistic compute and implementation constraints.\\n \\n-The dominant RL algorithm applied to LLM alignment, Proximal Policy\\n-Optimisation (PPO)~\\\\cite{schulman2017proximal}, requires a separate value\\n-(critic) network trained alongside the policy.\\n-For billion-parameter models, this doubles memory requirements and complicates\\n-distributed training.\\n-Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO)~\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}\\n-eliminates the critic by replacing the per-token advantage with a\\n-\\\\emph{group-normalised} reward: multiple completions are sampled for each\\n-prompt, and the advantage of each completion is computed relative to the\\n-group mean.\\n-Despite GRPO's practical appeal and widespread adoption in open-source\\n-pipelines~\\\\cite{openrlhf2024,trl2020,verl2024}, its sensitivity to the\\n-group size $G$ --- the number of completions sampled per prompt --- has not\\n-been rigorously characterised across model families and backends.\\n+Proximal Policy Optimisation (PPO)~\\\\cite{schulman2017proximal}, the standard\\n+RLHF workhorse, trains a value critic alongside the policy. For LoRA-tuned\\n+billion-parameter models, the critic adds memory pressure and engineering\\n+complexity. Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO)~\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}\\n+eliminates this critic by sampling $G$ completions per prompt and computing each\\n+advantage relative to the group's reward mean. The simplification also creates a\\n+specific fragility: when all completions in a binary-reward group are all wrong\\n+or all correct, the within-group variance is zero and GRPO contributes no\\n+relative advantage for that prompt. This zero-variance regime depends on group\\n+size, task difficulty, reward design, and backend details, so pooled diagnostics\\n+can appear predictive while actually separating tasks or configurations.\\n \\n \\\\textbf{Contributions.}\\n This paper makes the following contributions:\\n \\\\begin{enumerate}\\n-\\\\item \\\\textbf{Platform comparison.} We train identical GRPO configurations\\n-  on three backends --- the closed-source Tinker SDK, Modal H100 with veRL,\\n-  and TRL on NVIDIA L4 --- and quantify the gap attributable to backend\\n-  implementation versus algorithm choice.\\n-\\\\item \\\\textbf{Group-size ablation.} We run $G \\\\in \\\\{2, 4, 8, 16\\\\}$ on\\n-  Qwen3-8B/GSM8K and show that $G{=}8$ maximises the last-10 accuracy % chktex 8\\n-  while $G{=}2$ exhibits systematic collapse.\\n-\\\\item \\\\textbf{Diagnostic toolkit, descriptively.} We introduce the\\n-  Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) with a closed-form mechanical identity for\\n-  binary-reward GRPO (Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:zvf_identity}), and a reward-stability\\n-  coefficient. We position both as descriptive logging quantities and\\n-  audit the pooled ZVF--accuracy correlation under stratification, where\\n-  the apparent signal flips sign within-task.\\n-\\\\item \\\\textbf{Cross-task evaluation.} We extend GRPO beyond arithmetic to\\n-  schema-valid JSON function calling on xLAM-60k --- the all-wrong-collapse\\n-  regime predicted by the ZVF identity --- showing that RL benefits transfer\\n-  to a second exact-match-rewardable task.\\n-\\\\item \\\\textbf{Reproducible artefact.} We release training scripts, LoRA\\n-  adapters, step-level W\\\\&B traces, and per-experiment manifests\\n-  (git hash, training command, dataset version, run~ID, config~hash) under\\n-  MIT License at\\n-  \\\\url{https://github.com/pes-llm-research/tinker-rl-lab}; deleting the\\n-  generated tables from \\\\texttt{results/} and rerunning the build pipeline\\n-  reproduces every numeric claim in the paper from the released traces.\\n+\\\\item \\\\textbf{Controlled headline result and backend context.} We report a\\n+  five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K held-out base-vs-GRPO comparison with a post-GRPO\\n+  gain of $+1.3$~pp ($t{=}1.32$, $p{=}0.26$), and treat the result as not\\n+  statistically significant. We place this control beside single-seed Tinker,\\n+  Modal H100/veRL, and TRL-on-L4 runs so that configuration-level\\n+  backend gaps are not mistaken for isolated GRPO effects.\\n+\\\\item \\\\textbf{Group-size case study.} We run $G \\\\in \\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}$ on\\n+  Qwen3-8B/GSM8K and find single-seed evidence that $G{=}8$ gives the best\\n+  last-10 accuracy in this configuration, while $G{=}2$ collapses sharply and\\n+  $G{=}16$ regresses. We present this as a practical case study, not a\\n+  population-level optimum.\\n+\\\\item \\\\textbf{Advantage-collapse diagnostics.} We define the Zero-Variance\\n+  Fraction (ZVF) and its binary-reward identity (Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:zvf_identity}),\\n+  together with a reward-stability coefficient for length bias. We use the\\n+  stratified ZVF--accuracy audit to show why ZVF should be read as a descriptive\\n+  logging signal: the pooled correlation ($r{=}{-}0.769$, $N{=}15$) is\\n+  task-confounded and flips sign within GSM8K ($r{=}+0.40$, $N{=}19$).\\n+\\\\item \\\\textbf{Second task and reproducible artefact.} We test schema-valid JSON\\n+  function calling on xLAM-60k, including the all-wrong-collapse regime predicted\\n+  by the ZVF identity and a relaxed-reward run that reaches $94.2\\\\%$ training /\\n+  $89.4\\\\%$ held-out schema-validity. We release scripts, LoRA adapters,\\n+  step-level traces, manifests, and diagnostic utilities under MIT License at\\n+  \\\\url{https://github.com/pes-llm-research/tinker-rl-lab}.\\n \\\\end{enumerate}\\n \\n-The remainder of the paper is structured as follows.\\n-Section~\\\\ref{sec:related} surveys related work.\\n-Section~\\\\ref{sec:method} describes models, datasets, algorithms, and\\n-diagnostics.\\n-Section~\\\\ref{sec:results} presents experimental results.\\n-Section~\\\\ref{sec:discussion} discusses findings and concludes.\\n+The remainder of the paper follows this structure. Section~\\\\ref{sec:related}\\n+positions the work relative to RLHF, RLVR/GRPO, group size, reward hacking,\\n+tool use, and RL scaling. Section~\\\\ref{sec:method} defines the models, datasets,\\n+algorithms, and diagnostics. Section~\\\\ref{sec:results} reports the experiments,\\n+and Section~\\\\ref{sec:discussion} interprets the results and limitations.\\n```\",\"ct\":1777865232.265755}]},{\"id\":\"69f8105f-c55c-83e8-86b3-5909f76a2d51\",\"title\":\"LNCS Paper Space Optimization\",\"create_time\":1777864800.783344,\"update_time\":1777865512.866991,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nPage-budget surgery. Find at least 25 specific deletions in the .tex source with exact line ranges (e.g., \\\"lines 145–149\\\"), each with a one-line justification. Prioritize: redundancy, hedging language, throat-clearing, paragraphs that restate the abstract, and verbose figure-style descriptions where a sentence would do. Estimate cumulative line savings. Goal: free up ~15 lines of vertical space so a future addition is possible without reflow.\",\"ct\":1777864799.652},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n---DOCX? skip---\\\\n'\",\"ct\":1777864804.654866},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -R /home/oai/skills | sed -n '1,200p'\",\"ct\":1777864814.532464},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll audit the TeX by exact line number, using the PDF only to judge page impact, then return deletion-only cuts with ranges, justifications, and estimated savings.\",\"ct\":1777864815.086072},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -l /mnt/data && file /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf && wc -l /mnt/d