d with a recommendation of which to use.\",\"ct\":1777864807.894},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md\",\"ct\":1777864813.172863},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls /home/oai/skills && find /home/oai/skills -maxdepth 3 -type f -name SKILL.md -print\",\"ct\":1777864822.467759},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll use the source/PDF to ground the current abstract and claims, then draft five same-length, drop-in alternatives that preserve the 12-page LNCS budget and leave authors/figures untouched.\",\"ct\":1777864823.617662},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find / -path '*skills*' -name SKILL.md -print 2>/dev/null | head -50\",\"ct\":1777864825.620856},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -l /mnt/data && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '1,140p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864847.015408},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport re, pathlib\\ntex=pathlib.Path('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex').read_text()\\nabs=re.search(r'\\\\\\\\begin\\\\{abstract\\\\}(.*?)\\\\\\\\keywords',tex,re.S).group(1)\\n# crude remove latex commands\\ns=re.sub(r'%.*','',abs)\\ns=re.sub(r'~',' ',s)\\ns=re.sub(r'\\\\\\\\[a-zA-Z]+\\\\*?(?:\\\\[[^\\\\]]*\\\\])?(?:\\\\{([^{}]*)\\\\})?',lambda m: ' '+(m.group(1) or '')+' ',s)\\ns=re.sub(r'[{}$\\\\\\\\&]', ' ',s)\\nwords=re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)*\\\",s)\\nprint(len(words))\\nprint(s)\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864850.431009},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n \\\"GSM8K\\\\|14B\\\\|regression\\\\|ZVF\\\\|collapse\\\\|all-wrong\\\\|wrong\\\\|platform\\\\|scal\\\" -n /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | head -200\",\"ct\":1777864856.616451},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc sed -n '120,180p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '409,580p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '580,690p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864868.58801},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | sed -n '1,80p'\",\"ct\":1777864872.054592},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc mkdir -p /mnt/data/pdf_pages && pdftoppm -png -f 1 -l 1 -r 120 /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/pdf_pages/page >/dev/null && ls -l /mnt/data/pdf_pages\",\"ct\":1777864877.107134},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"import re, textwrap\\ndrafts={}\\ndrafts[1]=\\\"\\\"\\\"Reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR) offers a low-supervision route to improving language-model reasoning, but its gains under critic-free methods such as Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) remain difficult to separate from backend and evaluation effects. This paper audits GRPO on Qwen3 models for GSM8K arithmetic and xLAM-60k schema-valid function calling, using LoRA adapters across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. The central GSM8K result is deliberately conservative: in a five-seed Qwen3-8B held-out comparison, GRPO improves last-10 accuracy from 82.0% to 83.3%, a +1.3 pp gain that is not statistically significant (t=1.32, p=0.26). Single-seed Tinker case studies reach 84.4% at G=8 and 85.0% for Qwen3-4B, but we treat these as unmeasured-variance evidence rather than population-level claims. We also report a group-size ablation, where G=8 is strongest and G=2 collapses, and introduce Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) and a reward-stability coefficient as lightweight diagnostics for advantage collapse. Stratified analysis shows pooled ZVF--accuracy correlation is task-confounded. The artefact release includes scripts, LoRA checkpoints, logs, and diagnostic utilities for reproduction.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ndef wc(s): return len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9]+(?:[-'][A-Za-z0-9]+)*\\\",s))\\nwc(drafts[1])\\n\",\"ct\":1777864884.489274},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"drafts[2]=\\\"\\\"\\\"Critic-free reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards promises cheaper reasoning optimisation for large language models, but its behaviour is tightly coupled to backend, batching, and scale. We audit Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) with LoRA adapters on Qwen3 models from 0.6B to 14B parameters, evaluating GSM8K arithmetic and xLAM-60k schema-valid function calling across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. The most informative finding is a regression rather than a win: the Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO run reaches only 43.7% last-10 GSM8K accuracy, far below the Qwen3-8B base at 82.0% and the Qwen3-4B GRPO run at 85.0%. Diagnostics indicate a degenerate-policy collapse, with ZVF above 0.6 within ten steps, entropy falling to a near-deterministic mode, and the reward-stability coefficient crossing 0.6 at step 15. We also observe a configuration-level platform gap: Qwen3-8B achieves 84.4% on Tinker in a single seed, versus 70.1% for five-seed TRL on L4. These results motivate GRPO audits that report backend details, stratified diagnostics, and negative scaling cases, not just peak scores.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nwc(drafts[2])\\n\",\"ct\":1777864910.720801},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"drafts[2]=\\\"\\\"\\\"Critic-free reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards promises cheaper reasoning optimisation for large language models, but its behaviour is tightly coupled to backend, batching, and scale. We audit Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) with LoRA adapters on Qwen3 models from 0.6B to 14B parameters, evaluating GSM8K arithmetic and xLAM-60k schema-valid function calling across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. The most informative finding is a regression rather than a win: the Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO run reaches only 43.7% last-10 GSM8K accuracy, far below the Qwen\",\"ct\":1777864922.876304},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"drafts[3]=\\\"\\\"\\\"Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) removes the critic from RLVR training, but it can also remove the learning signal whenever all completions in a prompt group receive the same binary reward. This paper centres that failure mode through the Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF): the fraction of prompt groups whose within-group reward variance is zero. For binary rewards, ZVF has the mechanical form ZVF(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G, making both all-wrong collapse and all-correct saturation visible as the same zero-advantage event. We evaluate this diagnostic while training LoRA adapters for Qwen3 models on GSM8K and xLAM-60k across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. ZVF is useful as an online sentinel: it flags G=2 collapse, the Qwen3-14B degenerate run, and the xLAM all-wrong regime where reward remains zero. However, stratification is essential. The pooled ZVF--accuracy correlation is strong (r=-0.769, N=15), but it is dominated by task identity; within GSM8K the sign flips to r=+0.40, and model-varying GSM8K runs fall to r=+0.13. We therefore position ZVF as a cheap descriptive diagnostic, not a causal predictor of accuracy.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nwc(drafts[3])\\n\",\"ct\":1777864930.289808},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"drafts[4]=\\\"\\\"\\\"RLVR is often presented as a natural fit for tool-use because rewards can be computed from schema validity or exact function-call matches. This audit shows that such rewards can instead make GRPO fail by construction. We study Group Relative Policy Optimisation on Qwen3 LoRA adapters for GSM8K arithmetic and Salesforce xLAM-60k function calling across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. The central tool-use finding is an all-wrong-collapse regime: in a single-seed xLAM saturation analysis with 35 prompts, 10 rollouts per prompt, G=8, and 30 steps, every prompt has ZVF=1 from the first step and the batch reward mean stays at 0, because no completion satisfies the schema-validity predicate. In this regime, group-normalised advantages are zero and GRPO supplies no gradient, so the diagnostic identifies a reward-design failure rather than a model-capacity limit. With a relaxed schema reward, the full xLAM run reaches 94.2% schema-validity at convergence versus 78.5% for an SFT baseline, with held-out validity plateauing at 89.4%. We connect this behaviour to GSM8K group-size collapse and release logs, adapters, and diagnostic code.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nwc(drafts[4])\\n\",\"ct\":1777864940.502887},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"drafts[5]=\\\"\\\"\\\"This paper reports a deliberately conservative audit of GRPO for verifiable-reward language-model training, emphasising where the evidence does not support broad scaling claims. We train Qwen3 LoRA adapters from 0.6B to 14B parameters on GSM8K mathematical reasoning and xLAM-60k schema-valid function calling across Tinker SDK, Modal H100, and TRL on NVIDIA L4. The headline multi-seed result is negative: on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K, GRPO improves held-out last-10 accuracy from 82.0% to 83.3%, a +1.3 pp gain that is not statistically significant (t=1.32, p=0.26). Single-seed Tinker runs are useful case studies but not population estimates: G=8 reaches 84.4%, G=2 collapses to 37.5%, and Qwen3-14B regresses to 43.7% despite larger scale. We introduce Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) and a reward-stability coefficient to describe advantage collapse, then show that the attractive pooled ZVF--accuracy correlation is task-confounded and reverses within GSM8K. Tool-use experiments further expose an all-wrong-collapse regime under sparse rewards. The result is a reproducible artefact and a caution: GRPO can work, but backend, reward design, group size, and scale must be audited separately.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nwc(drafts[5])\\n\",\"ct\":1777864951.962757},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I found the abstract in the source and confirmed the PDF is already 12 pages. Since the current abstract is longer than 180–200 words, replacement frees space while preserving the author block.\",\"ct\":1777865018.312839},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"For all options below: **delete the current abstract body only, lines 69–97, and replace it with one option**. Leave `\\\\begin{abstract}`, `\\\\keywords{...}`, `\\\\end{abstract}`, and the full author block untouched. I count the current abstract body at about **256 words excluding keywords**; all five replacements are **180–192 words**, so each is a net deletion and does not require cutting elsewhere.\\n\\n## 1. GSM8K headline result  \\n**180 words; net deletion: ~76 words.**\\n\\n```latex\\nReinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR) promises to improve\\nmathematical reasoning without human-preference data, but its practical gains\\nunder critic-free Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) remain difficult\\nto separate from backend and sampling effects. We audit GRPO on GSM8K using\\nLoRA-adapted Qwen3 models from 0.6B to 14B parameters across Tinker, Modal\\nH100, and TRL-on-L4 backends. The primary held-out control is deliberately\\nconservative: on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K, five open-stack seeds yield 83.3\\\\%\\npost-GRPO last-10 accuracy versus 82.0\\\\% for the base model, a +1.3 pp gain\\nthat is not statistically significant ($t=1.32$, $p=0.26$). Single-seed\\nTinker case studies are stronger but treated as exploratory: Qwen3-8B reaches\\n84.4\\\\% last-10 accuracy, while a group-size sweep finds $G{=}8$ best among\\n$\\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}$ and $G{=}2$ collapsing to 37.5\\\\%. We introduce Zero-Variance\\nFraction (ZVF) and a reward-stability coefficient as lightweight diagnostics\\nfor advantage collapse and length bias, showing that pooled ZVF--accuracy\\ncorrelations are task-confounded. Results on xLAM-60k and a failed 14B run\\nfurther motivate reporting GRPO improvements as configuration-specific, not\\nuniversal, scaling claims.\\n```\\n\\n**Pitch:** Best for reviewers who want the main empirical takeaway up front, especially GSM8K/RLVR readers who care about whether GRPO actually improves reasoning.\\n\\n## 2. Platform and 14B regression angle  \\n**180 words; net deletion: ~76 words.**\\n\\n```latex\\nCritic-free reinforcement learning methods such as Group Relative Policy\\nOptimisation (GRPO) are attractive for large-language-model fine-tuning\\nbecause they avoid the value-network overhead of PPO, yet their behaviour\\nacross platforms and model scales is under-characterised. This paper audits\\nGRPO on LoRA-adapted Qwen3 models from 0.6B to 14B parameters, using GSM8K\\nreasoning and xLAM-60k function calling as verifiable-reward tasks. The most\\ninformative anomaly is a scaling regression at Qwen3-14B: under the Tinker\\nGRPO configuration that gives Qwen3-8B 84.4\\\\% last-10 GSM8K accuracy and\\nQwen3-4B 85.0\\\\%, the 14B model falls to 43.7\\\\% last-10 accuracy, with\\nZero-Variance Fraction above 0.6 within ten steps and reward--length\\ninstability by step 15. We therefore treat the 14B row as evidence of\\ndegenerate-policy collapse under default hyperparameters, not as evidence\\nthat GRPO fails at scale. A matched five-seed open-stack control on Qwen3-8B\\nfinds only a +1.3 pp non-significant post-GRPO gain, while Tinker and TRL\\nconfigurations differ by about 14 pp. The study releases scripts, LoRA\\ncheckpoints, logs, and diagnostics, and frames backend and scale effects as\\nconfiguration-level observations requiring controlled replication.\\n```\\n\\n**Pitch:** Best for systems, reproducibility, and applied-ML audiences who will be interested in backend sensitivity and the surprising 14B failure mode.\\n\\n## 3. ZVF diagnostic metric angle  \\n**190 words; net deletion: ~66 words.**\\n\\n```latex\\nGroup Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO) replaces a learned critic with\\ngroup-normalised rewards, but this shortcut can silently eliminate gradient\\nsignal when all completions for a prompt receive the same binary reward. We\\nintroduce Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF), the fraction of prompts in a batch\\nwith zero within-group reward variance, as a cheap descriptive sentinel for\\nGRPO advantage collapse. For binary rewards, ZVF obeys the exact identity\\n$\\\\mathrm{ZVF}(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G$, exposing two distinct failure regimes:\\nall-wrong collapse when $p$ approaches 0 and saturation when $p$ approaches 1.\\nWe audit this diagnostic on LoRA-adapted Qwen3 models across GSM8K and\\nxLAM-60k, with group sizes $G \\\\in \\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}$ and Tinker, Modal H100, and\\nTRL-on-L4 backends. The pooled ZVF--accuracy correlation appears strong\\n($r=-0.769$, $N=15$), but stratification shows task confounding: within GSM8K\\nthe sign flips to $r=+0.40$, and model-varying GSM8K runs fall to $r=+0.13$.\\nWe therefore position ZVF as a design-regime and logging diagnostic, not a\\nstandalone predictor of accuracy. Empirically, Qwen3-8B/GSM8K shows a\\nnon-significant +1.3 pp five-seed GRPO gain, while tool-use and 14B runs\\nillustrate high-ZVF collapse modes.\\n```\\n\\n**Pitch:** Best for a methods-oriented LNCS audience because it matches the title most directly and gives the paper a clear conceptual contribution.\\n\\n## 4. All-wrong-collapse tool-use angle  \\n**192 words; net deletion: ~64 words.**\\n\\n```latex\\nReinforcement learning from verifiable rewards is often presented as an easy\\nroute to tool-using language models: define a schema-validity reward, sample\\nmultiple completions, and optimise the policy with Group Relative Policy\\nOptimisation (GRPO). This paper shows why that recipe can fail before learning\\nbegins. We audit GRPO on GSM8K and Salesforce xLAM-60k with LoRA-adapted Qwen3\\nmodels, three execution backends, and group sizes from 2 to 16. Our diagnostic\\nfocus is Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF), the fraction of prompt groups whose\\nrewards are all identical. In the xLAM-60k saturation analysis, every prompt\\nremains at $\\\\mathrm{ZVF}{=}1$ from the first step while the batch reward mean\\nstays at 0: no sampled completion satisfies the strict schema predicate, so\\nGRPO supplies zero advantage signal by construction. This all-wrong-collapse\\nregime follows directly from $\\\\mathrm{ZVF}(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G$ as $p$ approaches\\n0. Relaxing the reward recovers useful learning, with $G{=}8$ reaching 94.2\\\\%\\ntraining schema-validity versus 78.5\\\\% for SFT and 89.4\\\\% held-out validity.\\nGSM8K results, including a non-significant +1.3 pp five-seed gain and a\\n$G{=}2$ collapse, show the same diagnostics can separate sparse-reward design\\nfailure from ordinary within-task variation.\\n```\\n\\n**Pitch:** Best for tool-use, agents, and structured-output audiences who may otherwise assume schema rewards are straightforward to optimize.\\n\\n## 5. Negative-result honesty and scaling caveat angle  \\n**180 words; net deletion: ~76 words.**\\n\\n```latex\\nVerifiable-reward reinforcement learning is appealing for improving\\nlanguage-model reasoning, but small empirical gains can be easy to overstate\\nwhen runs are short, single-seed, or backend-specific. This paper presents a\\ndeliberately conservative audit of Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO)\\nfor LoRA-adapted Qwen3 models on GSM8K and xLAM-60k. Our primary\\nQwen3-8B/GSM8K control uses five open-stack seeds and a fixed held-out\\nevaluation protocol: post-GRPO last-10 accuracy is 83.3\\\\% versus 82.0\\\\% for\\nthe base model, a +1.3 pp difference that is not statistically significant\\n($t=1.32$, $p=0.26$). We still report single-seed Tinker case studies because\\nthey are diagnostically useful: $G{=}8$ reaches 84.4\\\\% on GSM8K, $G{=}2$\\ncollapses to 37.5\\\\%, and Qwen3-14B regresses to 43.7\\\\% under default\\nhyperparameters. To explain such failures, we introduce Zero-Variance Fraction\\n(ZVF) and a reward-stability coefficient, showing that apparent pooled\\nZVF--accuracy trends are task-confounded and that xLAM can enter an\\nall-wrong-collapse regime with zero advantage signal. The resulting\\ncontribution is a reproducible negative-and-positive evidence package, with\\nexplicit resolution criteria, not a universal scaling claim for GRPO.\\n```\\n\\n**Pitch:** Best for skeptical empirical ML reviewers, because it foregrounds the non-significant main result and makes the paper look careful rather than promotional.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n\\nUse **Abstract 3, the ZVF diagnostic version**. It fits the title most tightly, gives reviewers a clean technical hook, and still preserves the paper’s strongest honesty signals: the non-significant GSM8K control, task-confounded pooled correlation, all-wrong-collapse regime, and 14B caveat.\",\"ct\":1777865223.239472}]},{\"id\":\"69f81047-0358-83e8-98cc-1179441058e0\",\"title\":\"Methodology Review Critique\",\"create_time\":1777864775.902462,\"update_time\":1777865137.151715,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nAct as a panel of 3 reviewers with deep expertise in RL fine-tuning of LLMs (think: PPO, GRPO, RLHF, RLVR). Each reviewer writes a focused critique of the METHODOLOGY only — base models, datasets, algorithm config, diagnostic metrics. For each reviewer give: (a) 3 strongest concerns ranked, with .tex line refs, (b) one concrete fix per concern that fits the page budget, (c) one piece of evidence in the paper that surprised them positively. End with a synthesis of which methodology fix is highest-leverage.\",\"ct\":1777864774.897},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n--- TEX head ---\\\\n' && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '1,120p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864780.238839},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n '^\\\\\\\\section\\\\|^\\\\\\\\subsection\\\\|^\\\\\\\\subsubsection\\\\|^\\\\\\\\paragraph\\\\|thebibliography' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864789.413342},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll anchor the methodology critique in exact `.tex` line references, using the PDF only for layout checks, and make every fix page-neutral with a concrete deletion.\",\"ct\":1777864791.56515},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '289,407p'\",\"ct\":1777864794.730537},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '407,577p'\",\"ct\":1777864798.147562},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '578,689p'\",\"ct\":1777864801.071912},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | sed -n '1,60p'; python /home/oai/skills/pdfs/scripts/render_pdf.py /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf --out_dir /mnt/data/_renders/conference --dpi 120 >/tmp/renderlog.txt && cat /tmp/renderlog.txt && ls -1 /mnt/data/_renders/conference | head\",\"ct\":1777864805.373256},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,288p'\",\"ct\":1777864814.537544},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve confirmed the source is 819 lines and the PDF is exactly 12 pages; methodology centers on lines 289–403, with key design claims also appearing in Results/Discussion.\",\"ct\":1777864986.977813},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I used the uploaded `.tex` line numbers and treated “methodology” broadly: base-model setup, data/reward design, RL algorithm config, backend comparability, evaluation protocol, and diagnostics. I do **not** suggest touching the author block at lines 42–61 or adding figures.\\n\\n## Reviewer 1 — RL experiment design and backend/model comparability\\n\\n### 1. Strongest concern: the “identical configurations / backend comparison” claim is not methodologically supported\\n\\n**Line refs:** contribution claim at lines 142–145; base models at lines 293–300; backend description at lines 354–364; results rows at lines 447–451; platform-gap discussion at lines 603–624.\\n\\nThe paper says it trains “identical GRPO configurations” and quantifies backend effects, but the methods later show multiple confounds: proprietary Tinker, Modal H100/veRL, TRL on L4, different hardware, possibly different batching, and unclear default implementations. There is also a direct inconsistency: line 360–361 says TRL on L4 is “Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds,” while Table 1 reports “Qwen3-8B TRL on NVIDIA L4 (5 seeds)” at line 449. That makes the backend comparison difficult to trust as a methodology claim.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 142–145 with a weaker, accurate contribution:\\n\\n```tex\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Configuration comparison.} We compare three runnable GRPO\\nconfigurations across Tinker, veRL, and TRL; because backend, hardware,\\nand minibatch defaults are not fully matched, we treat observed gaps as\\nconfiguration-level effects rather than isolated backend effects.\\n```\\n\\nThen resolve the TRL identity without adding space: either change line 361 to Qwen3-8B if Table 1 is correct, or change line 449 to Qwen2.5-0.5B and remove it from Qwen3-8B comparisons. This is a replacement, not an addition.\\n\\n### 2. Second concern: LoRA/base-model configuration is too underspecified to reproduce or interpret scaling\\n\\n**Line refs:** lines 293–300.\\n\\n“LoRA adapters with rank 8–64” is too broad. A reviewer cannot tell whether the 14B collapse, 4B success, or PPO/GRPO comparison is due to model scale, LoRA rank, target-module coverage, dropout, adapter alpha, precision, optimizer state, or trainable-parameter count. The paper also does not specify exact checkpoint identifiers, base vs instruct variants, tokenizer/chat template, or whether “reasoning mode” or system prompts differ across Qwen3 sizes.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 293–300 with a compact run-matrix sentence block:\\n\\n```tex\\nWe use Qwen3-\\\\{0.6B,1.7B,4B,8B,14B\\\\} checkpoints with the same tokenizer\\nand prompt template across GSM8K runs. LoRA targets q/k/v/o attention\\nprojections; each run manifest records checkpoint ID, rank $r$, $\\\\alpha$,\\ndropout, trainable-parameter count, seed, precision, and adapter hash.\\nUnless otherwise stated, PPO and GRPO use the same Qwen3-8B checkpoint and\\nLoRA target modules.\\n```\\n\\nTo pay for any extra words, delete the roadmap sentence at lines 168–173. It adds little under a 12-page LNCS limit.\\n\\n### 3. Third concern: the group-size ablation is single-seed and not clearly compute-matched\\n\\n**Line refs:** abstract caveat at lines 93–95; contribution at lines 146–148; group-size results at lines 456–478; interpretation at lines 580–599.\\n\\nThe text is careful in the abstract, but the contribution says the paper “show[s]” that \\\\(G=8\\\\) maximizes last-10 accuracy. Since \\\\(G \\\\in \\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}\\\\) changes the number of sampled completions per prompt, the ablation needs to say whether runs were matched by gradient steps, sampled tokens, wall-clock, number of prompts, or total completions. Without that, the result is a useful case study but not a clean group-size methodology conclusion.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 146–148 with:\\n\\n```tex\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Group-size case study.} We run a single-seed\\n$G \\\\in \\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}$ Qwen3-8B/GSM8K study and report it as\\ncompute-descriptive rather than population-level evidence.\\n```\\n\\nThen remove the “Peak Acc.” column from the group-size table at lines 473–478. That frees enough horizontal and textual space to add one sentence after line 458:\\n\\n```tex\\nAll four runs are matched on [gradient steps / sampled completions / token\\nbudget]; where not matched, we report the comparison as descriptive only.\\n```\\n\\nFill the bracket with the actual matching criterion.\\n\\n**Positive surprise:** The paper already contains unusually honest caveats about the five-seed negative control and single-seed Tinker results at lines 411–419. That substantially improves trust compared with a typical RL fine-tuning paper that would foreground only the best single run.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reviewer 2 — dataset, reward, and evaluation methodology\\n\\n### 1. Strongest concern: the evaluation split and checkpoint-selection protocol are unclear\\n\\n**Line refs:** GSM8K split at lines 305–306; held-out control at lines 411–419; peak-accuracy caveat at lines 432–434; Table 1 at lines 438–451.\\n\\nThe methods say the paper evaluates on the GSM8K test set, while the results report “last-10” and “peak” accuracies over many checkpoints. If the official GSM8K test set is used repeatedly for checkpoint tracking, then “peak” and potentially “last-10” become tuning-on-test artifacts. The paper acknowledges peak bias at lines 432–434, which is good, but the methodology should clearly separate training, validation/checkpoint monitoring, and final test reporting.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 305–306 with:\\n\\n```tex\\nWe train on GSM8K train, reserve a fixed validation subset for checkpoint\\nmonitoring, and use the 1,319-example official test set only for final\\nreporting; seed IDs and split files are released.\\n```\\n\\nTo pay for this, delete the “Peak Acc.” column from Table 1 at lines 445 and 447–451, or retain peak only in the released logs. This also strengthens the methodology because the paper already says peak is biased.\\n\\n### 2. Second concern: the GSM8K reward parser is precise but brittle without a prompt/template and invalid-output audit\\n\\n**Line refs:** reward extraction at lines 308–315; max sequence length at lines 363–364.\\n\\nThe regex details are welcome, but the method does not state whether the model is explicitly prompted to emit `####`, whether completions without that marker get reward 0, whether fallback numeric extraction is used, or how often outputs are malformed. In RLVR, parser brittleness can dominate the reward signal and create false negatives or formatting reward hacking.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 307–315 with a shorter but more complete protocol:\\n\\n```tex\\nGSM8K rewards use exact numeric match after a fixed prompt template that\\nrequires a final `#### answer' line. Gold and generated answers are parsed\\nwith the released regex/normalizer; missing or non-numeric final answers\\nreceive reward 0, and we log the malformed-output fraction per checkpoint.\\n```\\n\\nThis replacement is shorter than the current detailed regex paragraph. Keep the regex in the artifact repository rather than spending LNCS space on both patterns.\\n\\n### 3. Third concern: the xLAM reward is “schema validity,” not necessarily function-call correctness\\n\\n**Line refs:** xLAM reward at lines 320–326; all-wrong-collapse run at lines 544–553; relaxed-schema result at lines 554–558; contribution claim at lines 155–158.\\n\\nThe xLAM reward gives 1.0 for valid JSON with the correct function name and required arguments present/type-correct. That is useful, but it may not verify argument values or semantic correctness. The later “relaxed schema reward” at line 554 is undefined relative to the strict reward at lines 320–323. Also, using the “first 35 prompts” at line 325 is not a defensible sampling protocol unless the dataset ordering is known to be randomized and fixed.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 320–326 with:\\n\\n```tex\\nFor xLAM, we report schema-validity reward separately from semantic\\nfunction-call correctness. The strict reward checks JSON parse, function\\nname, required arguments, types, and argument values when available; the\\nrelaxed reward drops [state omitted fields exactly]. The 35-prompt\\nsaturation audit is a fixed stratified sample with seed [seed].\\n```\\n\\nTo pay for this, weaken lines 155–158 to avoid claiming full task transfer:\\n\\n```tex\\n\\\\item \\\\textbf{Cross-task evaluation.} We test whether GRPO improves a second\\nverifiable formatting task, schema-valid JSON function calling on xLAM-60k.\\n```\\n\\nThis keeps the contribution truthful and costs no extra space.\\n\\n**Positive surprise:** The paper’s xLAM all-wrong-collapse analysis at lines 544–553 is a strong methodological stress test. It uses a failure case to validate what ZVF is actually measuring, rather than reporting only successful runs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reviewer 3 — GRPO/PPO configuration and diagnostic metrics\\n\\n### 1. Strongest concern: the GRPO/PPO training configuration is too incomplete for RLFT reproducibility\\n\\n**Line refs:** GRPO objective at lines 330–343; PPO settings at lines 345–348; backend/config lines 354–364.\\n\\nThe paper gives the GRPO advantage formula, \\\\(\\\\epsilon_\\\\sigma\\\\), \\\\(\\\\beta\\\\), PPO GAE parameters, and optimizer learning rate. But the missing details are exactly the ones that often decide RLFT outcomes: rollout temperature, top-p/top-k, max new tokens, number of prompts per batch, completions per prompt, minibatch size, gradient accumulation, number of epochs per rollout batch, policy clipping, KL estimator, reference model definition, entropy bonus if any, gradient clipping, AdamW betas/weight decay, seed list, evaluation decoding, and stopping criterion.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 341–343 and 363–364 with a compact “config capsule”:\\n\\n```tex\\nUnless otherwise noted, GRPO uses $\\\\beta=0.01$, [clip $\\\\epsilon$],\\n[KL estimator], reference = frozen initial policy, rollout decoding\\n$temp=[ ]$, top-p=[ ], max-new-tokens=[ ], prompt batch=[ ], epochs=[ ],\\nminibatch=[ ], grad-clip=[ ], AdamW betas/decay=[ ], and greedy evaluation.\\n```\\n\\nThis can fit by deleting the less useful backend prose in lines 354–362 and replacing it with one compact sentence plus a pointer to manifests. The backend names are still visible in Table 1, so the method section should prioritize the RL knobs.\\n\\n### 2. Second concern: the ZVF identity is useful, but the interpretation around \\\\(G=16\\\\) is mathematically shaky\\n\\n**Line refs:** group-size background at lines 237–250; ZVF identity at lines 381–396; \\\\(G=16\\\\) interpretation at lines 462–464 and 589–591.\\n\\nThe identity \\\\(\\\\mathrm{ZVF}(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G\\\\) is valuable, but it assumes conditionally independent Bernoulli samples for a fixed prompt success probability \\\\(p\\\\). That assumption should be stated. More importantly, the discussion says larger \\\\(G\\\\) can drive ZVF high near the model’s ability boundary. Under the stated identity, for intermediate \\\\(p\\\\), increasing \\\\(G\\\\) usually lowers ZVF, not raises it. So the \\\\(G=16\\\\) regression should not be explained as “large groups drive ZVF high” unless the empirical logs actually show that.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 381–388 with:\\n\\n```tex\\nUnder conditionally iid binary rollouts with prompt success probability $p$,\\n$\\\\mathrm{ZVF}(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G$: all $G$ samples are either correct or\\nincorrect. We use this identity only as a mechanical guide; empirical ZVF\\nis measured directly from logged rewards.\\n```\\n\\nThen replace lines 589–591 with:\\n\\n```tex\\nAt $G=16$, the regression is therefore interpreted as an empirical\\nconfiguration failure, not as direct evidence that larger groups generally\\nincrease ZVF.\\n```\\n\\nNo extra space is needed; this is shorter than the current explanation.\\n\\n### 3. Third concern: the reward-stability coefficient is underspecified and risks conflating useful reasoning length with length bias\\n\\n**Line refs:** definition at lines 398–402; use in group-size collapse at lines 483–487; use in 14B collapse at lines 565–569.\\n\\nA rolling Pearson correlation between reward and completion length is plausible, but the paper does not specify whether the correlation is computed over completions, prompts, batch means, or checkpoints. A positive reward-length correlation can also be benign on GSM8K: longer reasoning may genuinely solve harder problems. Calling it “rewarded for verbosity rather than correctness” is too strong unless correctness, prompt difficulty, and malformed outputs are controlled.\\n\\n**Concrete page-budget fix:** Replace lines 398–402 with:\\n\\n```tex\\nFor each 20-step window, we compute $\\\\rho_{\\\\ell r}$ over individual\\ncompletions after grouping by prompt: length is generated-token count,\\nreward is binary task reward, and we report the windowed correlation\\ntogether with entropy, KL, malformed-output rate, and mean length.\\n```\\n\\nThen replace “confirmed by” at lines 486–487 with “accompanied by,” because the coefficient is a warning signal, not proof of reward hacking. This fits the page budget as a direct replacement.\\n\\n**Positive surprise:** The ZVF stratification section at lines 489–528 is methodologically mature. The paper does not simply introduce a diagnostic and oversell it; it shows the pooled correlation is task-confounded and reframes ZVF as a descriptive logging quantity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Synthesis: highest-leverage methodology fix\\n\\nThe highest-leverage fix is to **replace the current backend/config prose plus peak-accuracy columns with a compact, reproducible run-matrix/config capsule**.\\n\\nSpecifically: remove “Peak Acc.” from Tables 1 and 2, resolve the Qwen2.5/Qwen3 TRL inconsistency, and use the saved space to state exact checkpoint IDs, LoRA ranks/alpha/dropout, rollout decoding, batch/minibatch/epoch settings, KL/clipping details, seed lists, and split/evaluation protocol. That single change addresses the biggest methodological vulnerability: right now, model scale, backend, hardware, LoRA setup, rollout policy, and evaluation protocol are partly entangled. A compact run matrix would make the paper much more reviewable without touching the author block, adding figures, or exceeding 12 pages.\",\"ct\":1777865137.026957}]},{\"id\":\"69f81057-8b68-83e8-be47-69e2e7874929\",\"title\":\"Audit conceptual novelty\",\"create_time\":1777864792.910393,\"update_time\":1777865382.314861,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nAudit conceptual novelty. What is the actual delta between this paper and (a) the original GRPO paper, (b) RLVR papers from DeepSeek/Qwen teams, (c) tool-use RL prior art (RAGEN, ReTool, etc.). Quote the paper's stated contributions and grade each as: novel / incremental / re-statement. Be ruthless. Then suggest the sharpest possible re-framing of the contribution that's defensible given the actual results, in <=80 words to drop into the introduction.\",\"ct\":1777864791.413},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && echo '\\n--- DOCX SKILL? no need'\",\"ct\":1777864796.209572},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll treat this as a novelty/positioning audit: extract the paper’s own contribution claims from the PDF/TeX, then compare them against GRPO, RLVR, and tool-use RL prior art. Any rewrite will be page-neutral, under the 12-page cap, leave the author block unchanged, and add no figures.\",\"ct\":1777864807.676229},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -R /home/oai/skills | sed -n '1,120p'\",\"ct\":1777864808.16681},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find / -maxdepth 4 -type f -name SKILL.md 2>/dev/null | head -50\",\"ct\":1777864810.810458},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -l /mnt/data && file /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864814.147083},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdftotext -layout /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.txt && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.txt && sed -n '1,220p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.txt\",\"ct\":1777864823.370535},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc sed -n '220,520p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.txt\",\"ct\":1777864826.510377},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc sed -n '520,700p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.txt && echo '--- TEX contrib lines' && nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,220p'\",\"ct\":1777864828.910967},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '220,520p'\",\"ct\":1777864831.768322},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '520,860p'\",\"ct\":1777864834.326032},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"search_query\\\":[{\\\"q\\\":\\\"DeepSeekMath Group Relative Policy Optimization GRPO arxiv 2402.03300 group size advantage equation\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"DeepSeek-R1 Incentivizing reasoning capability via reinforcement learning verifiable reward GRPO arxiv 2501.12948\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Qwen reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards Qwen3 technical report RLVR GRPO\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"RAGEN ReTool tool use reinforcement learning LLM arxiv\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864837.768026},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"search_query\\\":[{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Qwen2.5-Math technical report GRPO reinforcement learning verifiable rewards\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Qwen2.5 Math Technical Report Group Relative Policy Optimization arxiv\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Qwen3 technical report reinforcement learning GRPO thinking non-thinking mode\\\"},{\\\"q\\\":\\\"Qwen team GRPO RLVR math reasoning paper\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864843.195517},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn311722search16\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn311722search5\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn900111search0\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn900111search8\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn311722search15\\\"},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn311722search11\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864846.8176},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"click\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view4\\\",\\\"id\\\":30}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864850.14325},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view0\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":229},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view1\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":150},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view2\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":380},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view5\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":110},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view3\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":310}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"long\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864856.614301},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view3\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":326},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view3\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":330},{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn671483view3\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":340}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777864862.460122},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | sed -n '1,40p'\",\"ct\":1777864864.607119},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"text = \\\"\\\"\\\"Rather than proposing a new GRPO variant, this paper stress-tests small-scale GRPO under verifiable rewards. Across Qwen3 LoRA runs, the headline GSM8K gain is not statistically significant, and both backend gaps and ZVF–accuracy correlations are shown to be configuration- or task-confounded. The main contribution is a reproducible diagnostic audit: ZVF exposes zero-gradient reward regimes, especially strict schema-valid function calling, while group-size results remain single-seed case studies rather than population claims.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nlen(text.split()), text\\n\",\"ct\":1777864879.117041},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve now anchored the grading to the contribution block around lines 139–166. The defensible framing is a negative/diagnostic audit, especially around misleading pooled ZVF, confounded backend gaps, and zero-signal GRPO cases.\",\"ct\":1777865011.843913},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I inspected both attachments: the rendered LNCS PDF is exactly 12 pages, and the source is a single 819-line `.tex` file with the author block at lines 36–61 and inline bibliography at line 690. I’m leaving the author block untouched and suggesting text-only changes only.\\n\\n## Bottom line\\n\\nThere is **no defensible “new GRPO method” or “new tool-use RL” contribution here**. The strongest defensible contribution is a **small, reproducible diagnostic audit** showing that some attractive GRPO narratives do **not** survive controls: the multi-seed GSM8K gain is non-significant, the pooled ZVF–accuracy correlation is task-confounded, and static JSON function-calling exposes a zero-gradient sparse-reward regime.\\n\\nDeepSeekMath already introduced GRPO as a PPO variant that removes the critic and estimates the baseline from group scores; DAPO later explicitly identifies the zero-advantage problem when all completions for a prompt receive identical rewards and filters all-0/all-1 groups through dynamic sampling. That makes ZVF useful as a logging name and audit variable, not a new mechanism. citeturn353328view0turn413741view1\\n\\nDeepSeek-R1 and Qwen-family RLVR work already cover the broad “GRPO + verifiable reward improves reasoning” story: DeepSeek-R1 uses GRPO with reward based on final-answer correctness; Qwen2.5-Math uses GRPO after SFT with query filtering based on how many of 8 sampled responses are correct; Qwen3 uses query-verifier pairs, large rollout counts, off-policy training, and entropy control; QwQ uses outcome rewards with math verifiers and code execution. Your actual delta is much narrower: low-compute Qwen3 LoRA audits, backend/configuration caveats, and negative/fragile diagnostics. citeturn590126view2turn590126view5turn788737view2turn590126view6\\n\\nAgainst tool-use RL prior art, xLAM here is **not** in the same class as ReTool or RAGEN. ReTool trains multi-turn code-interpreter use with real-time execution and outcome feedback; RAGEN studies multi-turn interactive agent RL with trajectory-level optimization and failure modes such as reward-variance cliffs and gradient spikes. Your xLAM experiment is static schema-valid JSON generation with a verifier, better framed as **structured-output RLVR**, not agentic tool-use RL. citeturn590126view8turn590126view7\\n\\n## Contribution-by-contribution novelty grade\\n\\n| Paper’s stated contribution | Grade | Ruthless audit |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| **“Platform comparison. We train identical GRPO configurations on three backends … and quantify the gap attributable to backend implementation versus algorithm choice.”** | **Incremental, overclaimed** | The evidence is useful, but not causal. The paper itself later admits the Tinker/TRL gap conflates implementation, hardware, and minibatch construction. So this is a **configuration-level comparison**, not a quantification of “backend implementation versus algorithm choice.” Also fix the internal inconsistency: line 361 says TRL L4 is “Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds,” while Table 1 reports Qwen3-8B TRL L4. |\\n| **“Group-size ablation. We run \\\\(G \\\\in \\\\{2,4,8,16\\\\}\\\\) on Qwen3-8B/GSM8K and show that \\\\(G=8\\\\) maximises the last-10 accuracy while \\\\(G=2\\\\) exhibits systematic collapse.”** | **Incremental** | This is a single-seed Tinker case study, not a robust group-size result. The word “systematic” is too strong unless repeated across seeds/backends. More seriously, the explanation for \\\\(G=16\\\\) is conceptually shaky: from your own identity, \\\\(p^G+(1-p)^G\\\\) decreases with \\\\(G\\\\) for fixed \\\\(0<p<1\\\\). Larger \\\\(G\\\\) does **not** raise expected ZVF; a better compute-normalized explanation is reduced prompt diversity under a fixed rollout budget. |\\n| **“Diagnostic toolkit, descriptively. We introduce the Zero-Variance Fraction (ZVF) with a closed-form mechanical identity … and a reward-stability coefficient.”** | **Re-statement, with one incremental audit result** | The identity \\\\(p^G+(1-p)^G\\\\) is a Bernoulli all-same event, not a new theory. DAPO already discusses the zero-advantage all-correct/all-wrong issue and filters such prompts. The incremental part is the **stratified audit** showing the pooled ZVF–accuracy correlation flips/weakens within task. The reward-stability coefficient is basically reward–length correlation; useful logging, not novelty. |\\n| **“Cross-task evaluation. We extend GRPO beyond arithmetic to schema-valid JSON function calling on xLAM-60k … showing that RL benefits transfer to a second exact-match-rewardable task.”** | **Incremental, needs reframing** | The task transfer is useful but should not be sold as tool-use RL. It is static schema validation. Also the xLAM paragraph mixes training convergence, SFT comparison, and held-out plateau; make clear whether SFT and GRPO are compared on the same held-out metric. |\\n| **“Reproducible artefact. We release training scripts, LoRA adapters, step-level W&B traces, and per-experiment manifests …”** | **Incremental engineering contribution** | Valuable, but not conceptual novelty. The strongest version of the paper is an artifact/audit paper. I did not verify the repository contents, so this claim should remain conditional on the rebuild actually reproducing every number. |\\n\\n## Conceptual fixes that should be made page-neutrally\\n\\nDo **not** add pages or figures. Make these as replacements/deletions.\\n\\n1. **Replace the contribution list, don’t expand it.**  \\n   Delete lines 139–166 and insert the 72-word reframing below. This saves substantial space and removes overclaiming.\\n\\n2. **Delete lines 168–173.**  \\n   The “remainder of the paper is structured as follows” boilerplate is expendable in a 12-page LNCS paper. Use the saved space for one sentence distinguishing xLAM from ReTool/RAGEN.\\n\\n3. **Delete or compress lines 274–284, “Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning.”**  \\n   The paper does not have enough evidence for scaling-law language, and the 14B result is an anomaly, not a scaling analysis. This deletion pays for missing RAGEN/ReTool citations without page growth.\\n\\n4. **Replace the \\\\(G=16\\\\) explanation.**  \\n   Current logic says larger groups reduce signal through high ZVF. That is not implied by Eq. 3. Replace lines 462–464 and 582–593 with a compute-budget framing:  \\n   `Under a fixed rollout budget \\\\(C\\\\), the expected number of usable prompt groups is approximately \\\\(C/G \\\\cdot [1-p^G-(1-p)^G]\\\\); the single-seed \\\\(G=16\\\\) regression is therefore better read as a prompt-diversity/compute trade-off or run-specific instability, not as evidence that larger \\\\(G\\\\) increases ZVF.`  \\n   Delete the existing “theoretical prediction” language to pay for this.\\n\\n5. **Soften the platform claim.**  \\n   Replace “quantify the gap attributable to backend implementation versus algorithm choice” with:  \\n   `document a configuration-level gap across backends, without causally isolating implementation, hardware, or batching.`  \\n   This is shorter and consistent with your Discussion.\\n\\n6. **Fix unsupported model-range claims.**  \\n   Lines 83, 283, and 293–295 claim experiments from 0.6B to 14B, but the visible results only show 4B, 8B, and 14B. Either add 0.6B/1.7B rows by deleting weaker text elsewhere, or page-neutrally change the claim to “4B–14B, with Qwen3-8B as the primary workhorse.”\\n\\n7. **Replace the conclusion overclaim.**  \\n   Line 668 says “ZVF is an effective early indicator of reward hacking.” The results support a narrower claim. Replace with:  \\n   `ZVF is a cheap sentinel for zero-gradient prompt groups and reward-design failures, but not a standalone cross-task performance predictor.`\\n\\n8. **Rename the xLAM contribution.**  \\n   Replace “Tool-use” in the keywords with “Function calling” or “Structured-output RLVR.” Same page cost, more accurate.\\n\\n## Sharpest defensible intro reframing\\n\\nReplace the current five-item contribution list at lines 139–166 with this:\\n\\n> This paper is best read not as a new GRPO method but as a small, reproducible audit of where GRPO diagnostics do and do not carry signal. Across Qwen3 LoRA runs, the only multi-seed held-out GSM8K gain is small and non-significant; the strongest ZVF–accuracy correlation is task-confounded; and a static function-calling task exposes the all-wrong zero-gradient regime. The contribution is a page-budgeted diagnostic artifact: traces, checks, and failure-mode cautions for low-compute GRPO.\\n\\nThat is 72 words. Delete lines 139–166 to insert it; delete lines 168–173 as additional page-budget margin.\",\"ct\":1777865382.187349}]},{\"id\":\"69f8104f-fd44-83e8-ac87-8c8e67187235\",\"title\":\"Empirical Rigor Audit\",\"create_time\":1777864785.52961,\"update_time\":1777865326.191014,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nAudit empirical rigor. Specifically: are seeds reported? Are error bars / confidence intervals shown? Is statistical significance tested for the GSM8K main result, the group-size ablation, and the PPO-vs-GRPO comparison? Is the n in each cell of the tables clear? Is the ZVF stratified analysis (Section 5.3) using the right denominator? List every empirical-rigor gap with .tex line refs and a one-line fix that's page-neutral. Then rank the gaps by how badly they'd hurt acceptance.\",\"ct\":1777864783.661},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && echo '\\n--- TEX FILE HEAD ---' && ls -l /mnt/data && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '1,120p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864788.900998},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find /home/oai -maxdepth 4 -type f -name SKILL.md 2>/dev/null | sort | sed -n '1,100p' && ls -l /mnt/data\",\"ct\":1777864798.94519},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll audit the .tex as source of truth, cross-check the rendered 12-page LNCS only where layout matters, and keep fixes page-neutral without touching authors or adding figures.\",\"ct\":1777864799.238967},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,180p'\",\"ct\":1777864802.338531},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n -E 'seed|Seed|signif|p\\\\b|confidence|interval|error bar|std|standard|Table|tab:|GSM8K|group|GRPO|PPO|ZVF|denominator|N\\\\{|n=|n\\\\{|sample|runs|replicate|Welch|t=|p=' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,240p'\",\"ct\":1777864805.155962},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '280,680p'\",\"ct\":1777864809.605137},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '456,529p'\",\"ct\":1777864812.264377},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,80p' || true; pdftotext -layout /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf - | sed -n '1,80p'\",\"ct\":1777864814.837136},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdftotext -layout /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf - | sed -n '300,520p'\",\"ct\":1777864831.363283},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport math\\ntry:\\n    from scipy import stats\\n    for r,n in [(-0.769,15),(0.40,19),(0.13,7)]:\\n        t=r*math.sqrt((n-2)/(1-r*r))\\n        p=2*stats.t.sf(abs(t), n-2)\\n        print(r,n,t,p)\\nexcept Exception as e:\\n    print(e)\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864856.012049},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ve narrowed the audit to Methods/Results lines 304–540, especially denominators near 368–374 and tables lacking explicit n, uncertainty, or matched-test status.\",\"ct\":1777864994.004227},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom math import *\\ntry:\\n from scipy import stats\\n diff=1.3\\n t=1.32\\n n=5\\n se=diff/t\\n ci=stats.t.ppf(.975,n-1)*se\\n print(se,ci,diff-ci,diff+ci)\\nexcept Exception as e: print(e)\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777865010.804909},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n -i 'seed' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777865015.59238},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n -i -E 'confidence|interval|error|std|standard deviation|mean \\\\\\\\pm|variance estimate|not statistically|significance|significant|t =' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777865018.009168},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I audited the `.tex` line-by-line against the rendered 12-page PDF. The paper is already unusually honest in places, especially lines 411–419 and 432–434, but several empirical claims still read stronger than the evidence supports.\\n\\n## Direct answers\\n\\n**Seeds reported?** Partially. Seed *counts* are reported for the headline five-seed control, the single-seed Tinker case studies, and the TRL five-seed row: lines 88, 93, 411–419, 421–429, 449, 604–606, 632–634. The actual random seed values are not reported, and the seed count is not visible in every table cell.\\n\\n**Error bars / confidence intervals shown?** No. There are no error bars, standard deviations, standard errors, or confidence intervals in Tables 1–3 or in the PPO/xLAM prose. The only inferential quantities shown are one headline `t`/`p` pair for GSM8K and one pooled ZVF correlation `p`.\\n\\n**Statistical significance tested?**\\n\\n| Claim | Tested? | Status |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| GSM8K main result | **Yes, but under-specified** | Lines 411–415 report `t=1.32, p=0.26`, but not the test type, df, seed-level mean±sd, CI, or whether the test is paired over seeds. |\\n| Group-size ablation | **No** | Lines 456–481 are single-seed; no significance test is possible from the current design. The text still uses “best”, “maximises”, and “sweet spot” language. |\\n| PPO-vs-GRPO comparison | **No** | Lines 537–538 explicitly say no formal test; additionally, PPO and GRPO are not matched by backend, seed count, or implementation. |\\n\\n**Is `n` in each table cell clear?** No. Table 1 has a visible “5 seeds” only for the TRL row; the base and Tinker rows rely on prose. Table 2 has no per-row `n`, though all rows appear to be `n=1`. Table 3 has an `N` column, but the sampling frame is not auditable because “pooled” has `N=15` while “within-GSM8K” has `N=19`.\\n\\n**Is the ZVF stratified analysis using the right denominator?** Not safely, as written. The *conceptual* denominator for ZVF should be the number of prompt groups, not completions, rollouts, tokens, or checkpoints. Lines 369–373 describe that idea, but Eq. line 373 uses `|\\\\text{batch}|`, which is ambiguous in GRPO code because a batch often contains `B × G` completions. Separately, Table 3’s correlation denominator is unclear/inconsistent: if the pooled sample is “all Tinker experiments” with `N=15`, then a within-GSM8K subset should not have `N=19` unless it comes from a different sample frame. That needs either recomputation or relabeling.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Ranked empirical-rigor gaps and page-neutral fixes\\n\\n### 1. Results/methods contradiction on the TRL baseline model  \\n**Lines:** 360–361 vs. 449 and 532–533  \\n**Gap:** Methods say TRL on L4 uses “Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds,” but Table 1 and PPO/GRPO discussion imply Qwen3-8B. This is the most damaging issue because it makes the baseline identity unclear.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace line 361 with the true model/run count, e.g. `Qwen3-8B, 5 seeds`, or change Table 1 line 449 and lines 532–533 to Qwen2.5-0.5B and remove Qwen3-8B comparisons.\\n\\n### 2. Table 1 does not expose the headline five-seed GSM8K result or uncertainty  \\n**Lines:** 411–415, 436–451  \\n**Gap:** The primary quantitative claim is the five-seed `83.3% vs 82.0%` result, but Table 1 does not show that row, seed-level variation, or CI. A reviewer cannot verify the headline from the table.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Delete the `Peak Acc.` column, which is already disclaimed at lines 432–434, and replace it with `n` plus `Last-10 mean ± sd` or `95% CI`; include the headline five-seed GRPO row.\\n\\n### 3. Actual seed values are not reported  \\n**Lines:** 88, 93, 411–429, 604–606, 632–634  \\n**Gap:** The paper reports “five-seed” and “single-seed” counts, but not the actual seed IDs or which randomness each seed controls: model sampling, data order, LoRA init, backend nondeterminism.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace lines 363–364 with a compressed reproducibility sentence: `All runs use AdamW, lr 5e-5, 10% warmup, max length 512; multi-seed runs use seeds {...}, single-run rows use seed ...`.\\n\\n### 4. No error bars or confidence intervals in empirical tables  \\n**Lines:** 436–451, 466–480, 505–519  \\n**Gap:** Tables report point estimates only. For rows with `n=5`, no variance is shown; for rows with `n=1`, that limitation is not visible inside the table.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** In Tables 1–2, replace the `Peak Acc.` emphasis with `n` and `mean ± sd`/CI where available; for `n=1`, write `single run` or `CI n/a`.\\n\\n### 5. GSM8K headline significance test is under-specified  \\n**Lines:** 411–415  \\n**Gap:** The result gives `t=1.32, p=0.26`, but omits test type, degrees of freedom, seed-level aggregation, and CI. It is also unclear whether the base-vs-GRPO comparison is paired over seeds.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace lines 412–415 with: `Across five paired seeds, GRPO obtains ... vs ...; paired t_4=1.32, p=0.26, 95% CI [..,..].`\\n\\n### 6. Group-size ablation is single-seed but repeatedly phrased as a finding  \\n**Lines:** 146–148, 458–464, 468–470, 580–593, 667–668  \\n**Gap:** The abstract caveats this well at lines 93–95, but the contribution, results, discussion, and conclusion still say “maximises,” “best,” “sweet spot,” and “trade-off” as if population-level.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace each strong phrase with `highest observed in this single-seed Tinker case study; no significance claim`.\\n\\n### 7. Table 2 lacks per-row `n` and no significance test is possible  \\n**Lines:** 466–480  \\n**Gap:** The group-size table has four point estimates but no seed count, no uncertainty, and no indication that each cell is `n=1`.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace the caption sentence at lines 468–470 with: `Single-seed Tinker case study; each row is one run, last-10 averages ten autocorrelated checkpoints, and no significance test is claimed.`\\n\\n### 8. PPO-vs-GRPO is not a controlled algorithm comparison  \\n**Lines:** 530–539  \\n**Gap:** PPO is Modal H100, GRPO is Tinker `G=8`; backend, implementation, hardware, seed count, and possibly batching differ. The current subsection title “PPO vs. GRPO” overstates algorithmic isolation.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Rename the subsection to `Unmatched PPO/GRPO Case Study` and replace lines 534–536 with `This design cannot isolate critic-vs-group-normalisation effects.`\\n\\n### 9. PPO-vs-GRPO has no uncertainty or test  \\n**Lines:** 532–538  \\n**Gap:** The paper reports an 81.2% vs 84.4% gap but explicitly avoids significance testing. That is honest, but the sentence “critic provides marginal benefit” still reads as an empirical conclusion.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace `suggesting that...` at lines 534–536 with `therefore we treat the 3.2 pp gap as descriptive only.`\\n\\n### 10. ZVF equation denominator is ambiguous  \\n**Lines:** 369–373  \\n**Gap:** Text says “fraction of prompts,” but Eq. line 373 divides by `|\\\\text{batch}|`. In GRPO, a batch can mean completions, prompt groups, or tokens. The right denominator for ZVF is the number of prompt groups `B`.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace line 373 with `\\\\text{ZVF}=B^{-1}\\\\sum_{b=1}^{B}\\\\mathbf{1}[\\\\sigma_{G,b}=0]`, where `B` is the number of prompt groups.\\n\\n### 11. ZVF stratified correlation sample frame is inconsistent  \\n**Lines:** 493–503, 505–517  \\n**Gap:** The pooled analysis has `N=15`, but “within-GSM8K (all)” has `N=19`. That can be valid only if the rows use different sample frames, but the table/caption do not say so.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Rename the `N` column to `independent runs` and relabel samples, e.g. `Pooled Tinker runs` vs. `All GSM8K runs across stacks`; otherwise recompute all rows under one sampling frame.\\n\\n### 12. ZVF correlation significance is incomplete  \\n**Lines:** 493–510, 515–517  \\n**Gap:** The pooled correlation gives `p=0.0008`, but the within-GSM8K rows do not give `p` or CI even though the caption says they do not survive a 5% threshold.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Change the Pearson column to `r (p)` and put the within-task p-values in the existing table cells, or delete the “5% threshold” claim.\\n\\n### 13. Correlation tests may treat non-independent observations as independent  \\n**Lines:** 493–503, 505–517  \\n**Gap:** It is unclear whether `N` counts independent runs/seeds, hyperparameter settings, checkpoints, or mixed observations. If repeated checkpoints or related hyperparameter sweeps are counted as independent, the p-values are inflated.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Add to the Table 3 caption: `Each point is one independent run after averaging over checkpoints; no checkpoint-level observations enter N.` If that is false, recompute with clustered bootstrap by run.\\n\\n### 14. xLAM saturation run mixes “10 rollouts per prompt” with `G=8`  \\n**Lines:** 545–547  \\n**Gap:** ZVF is defined over `G` completions per prompt, but the xLAM paragraph says 10 rollouts per prompt and `G=8`. The denominator/grouping is unclear.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace with the true statement: either `8 rollouts per prompt, G=8` or `10 diagnostic rollouts per prompt, not used as the GRPO group`.\\n\\n### 15. xLAM GRPO-vs-SFT result is single-run and lacks held-out `n`/uncertainty  \\n**Lines:** 554–558, also claim lines 155–158 and 672–673  \\n**Gap:** The paper says GRPO reaches 94.2% vs SFT 78.5% and held-out 89.4%, but does not report seeds, held-out set size, CI, or significance. The conclusion says “benefits transfer” too strongly.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace `showing that RL benefits transfer` / `GRPO benefits transfer` with `single-run evidence that RL can improve schema-validity`; add `held-out N=...` in line 558 by deleting “coinciding with saturation of the training set.”\\n\\n### 16. Platform comparison contribution overclaims attribution  \\n**Lines:** 142–145 vs. 603–624  \\n**Gap:** The contribution says “identical GRPO configurations” and “quantify the gap attributable to backend implementation,” but the discussion later correctly says the gap conflates implementation, hardware, and minibatch construction.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace lines 142–145 with: `We compare nominally similar GRPO configurations across three backends and report configuration-level gaps without attributing them to any single axis.`\\n\\n### 17. “Peak accuracy” is biased but still foregrounded in tables and prose  \\n**Lines:** 432–434, 438–451, 459–470  \\n**Gap:** The paper correctly says peak is upward biased, but Table 1 and Table 2 still display it prominently, and Table 2’s caption highlights “perfect peak performance.”  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Delete the `Peak Acc.` column or demote it to a footnote; replace “perfect peak performance” with `highest observed last-10 accuracy`.\\n\\n### 18. Reward-stability coefficient threshold is used as if validated  \\n**Lines:** 398–401, 485–487, 566–569  \\n**Gap:** The coefficient is defined, but the threshold `>0.6` is not calibrated, pre-registered, or tied to a null distribution. “Confirmed by” is too strong.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace `confirmed by` at line 486 with `accompanied by`, and add `heuristic` before `reward-stability coefficient` where first used.\\n\\n### 19. “First 35 prompts” for xLAM saturation can introduce order bias  \\n**Lines:** 325–326, 545–546  \\n**Gap:** The saturation analysis uses the first 35 prompts, not a randomized or stratified sample. That is acceptable for a smoke test, but not for a representative empirical claim.  \\n**Page-neutral fix:** Replace `first 35 prompts` with either `fixed seed-sampled 35-prompt subset` if true, or `non-random 35-prompt smoke-test subset`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Highest-priority edits before submission\\n\\nThe acceptance-critical fixes are: resolve the Qwen2.5/Qwen3 TRL contradiction; put `n` and uncertainty directly in Tables 1–2; weaken all group-size and PPO-vs-GRPO claims to “single-run/unmatched case study”; and repair the ZVF denominator/sample-frame wording. These changes can be made without touching the author block, without adding figures, and without increasing page count by deleting or replacing the peak-accuracy emphasis.\",\"ct\":1777865326.058387}]},{\"id\":\"69f81089-84a4-83e8-a00d-d3a9ad25c40d\",\"title\":\"Math Notation Consistency Check\",\"create_time\":1777864844.244982,\"update_time\":1777865900.19326,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nNotation and equations consistency check. Inspect every \\\\begin{equation} block and inline math. Verify: every symbol is defined before use, notation is consistent across sections, the GRPO loss formulation matches the cited reference, the ZVF and Pass@k metric definitions are unambiguous. List every issue with line refs, then provide diff patches that fix them. Don't introduce new notation; consolidate.\",\"ct\":1777864841.399},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && printf '\\\\n---FILES---\\\\n' && ls -l /mnt/data\",\"ct\":1777864847.715858},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll use the TeX for line-accurate notation and patches, checking the PDF only for layout/page-budget effects. I’ll review equations before inline math, verify GRPO against its citation, and keep fixes page-neutral without touching authors.\",\"ct\":1777864858.012219},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find /home/oai -maxdepth 4 -type f -name 'SKILL.md' -o -name '*pdf*' | head -100 && ls -la /home/oai/skills || true\",\"ct\":1777864862.919288},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pwd; ls -l /mnt/data; wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex; file /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864866.15926},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,220p'\",\"ct\":1777864869.708908},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '220,460p'\",\"ct\":1777864872.760487},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '460,690p'\",\"ct\":1777864876.162406},{\