\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{Reinforcement Learning for Language Models}\\n\\nReinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) was introduced to language\\nmodelling by Ziegler et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{ziegler2019fine} and scaled to\\ninstruction-following by Ouyang et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{ouyang2022training} with\\nInstructGPT\\\\\\\\@.\\nThe PPO-based RLHF recipe trains a reward model on human preference pairs and\\nuses it to fine-tune the policy while constraining KL divergence from a\\nfrozen reference.\\nWhile effective, this pipeline is operationally complex: it requires a\\npreference dataset, a separate reward model, and a critic network, each\\nadding engineering burden and memory cost.\\n\\nRafailov et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{rafailov2023direct} proposed Direct Preference\\nOptimisation (DPO) as an offline alternative that eliminates the reward\\nmodel by re-parameterising the preference objective directly in terms of\\npolicy log-ratios.\\nDPO removes the RL training loop entirely but requires pairwise preference\\ndata and optimises a supervised loss that does not support exploration.\\nSubsequent variants---ORPO~\\\\\\\\cite{hong2024orpo},\\nSimPO~\\\\\\\\cite{meng2024simpo}---share DPO's offline character and inherit its\\ninability to improve beyond the coverage of the preference dataset.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew1 = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{From Preference RL to Verifiable Rewards}\\n\\nReinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) brought policy-gradient\\npost-training to language models: Ziegler et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{ziegler2019fine}\\nintroduced the setup, and Ouyang et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{ouyang2022training} scaled it to\\ninstruction-following with InstructGPT\\\\\\\\@.\\nThe standard PPO recipe trains a reward model from preference pairs and then\\nupdates the policy under a KL constraint to a frozen reference.\\nIts cost is not only computational: human preferences, reward-model training,\\nand a critic network become separate moving parts whose errors can compound.\\n\\nOffline preference objectives such as DPO~\\\\\\\\cite{rafailov2023direct} and its\\nORPO/SimPO variants~\\\\\\\\cite{hong2024orpo,meng2024simpo} remove the online RL loop,\\nbut they still depend on pairwise preference coverage and replace exploration\\nwith a supervised loss.\\nFor tasks with automatically checkable outcomes, the relevant alternative is\\ntherefore not only ``simpler preference optimisation'' but online RL with a\\nprogrammatic reward, which motivates RL from verifiable rewards and GRPO.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\norig2 = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{Verifiable Rewards and GRPO}\\n\\nThe observation that mathematical and code-generation tasks admit\\n\\\\\\\\emph{verifiable} rewards---rewards computed by deterministic procedures\\nrather than learned reward models---motivates a simpler RL loop.\\nCobbe et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{cobbe2021training} used a learned verifier trained on\\nGSM8K as a process reward model; Lightman et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{lightman2023let}\\nextended this to per-step process rewards.\\nBoth approaches still require learned models.\\n\\nDeepSeek-Math~\\\\\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} and its successor\\nDeepSeek-R1~\\\\\\\\cite{deepseek2025r1} demonstrated that GRPO with exact-match\\nrewards, applied to mathematical reasoning, produces dramatic improvements\\nat scale, and DAPO~\\\\\\\\cite{yu2025dapo} subsequently revisits GRPO's\\nclipping and group-sampling defaults at production scale.\\nA closely related critic-free baseline is REINFORCE Leave-One-Out\\n(RLOO)~\\\\\\\\cite{ahmadian2024rloo}, which uses a per-prompt leave-one-out\\nmean as the baseline rather than the group mean.\\nGRPO groups $G$ completions per prompt, computes the mean reward within the\\ngroup as a baseline, and trains the policy to increase the log-probability\\nof completions whose reward exceeds the group mean.\\nThis is equivalent to a REINFORCE baseline that does not require a value\\nnetwork.\\n\\nOpenRLHF~\\\\\\\\cite{openrlhf2024}, TRL~\\\\\\\\cite{trl2020}, and veRL~\\\\\\\\cite{verl2024}\\nprovide open-source GRPO implementations.\\nOur work benchmarks these implementations against each other and against\\nthe closed-source Tinker SDK, providing one of the first systematic\\nmulti-backend comparisons.\\n\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Group Size and Sample Efficiency}\\n\\nThe sensitivity of GRPO to the group size $G$ is a practical concern that\\nhas received little systematic attention.\\nLarger $G$ produces a lower-variance advantage estimate but increases memory\\nproportionally and may reduce gradient signal when the group reward\\ndistribution is near-uniform (the high-ZVF regime).\\nSmaller $G$ is cheaper but produces noisier gradients and, as we show,\\ncollapses to reward hacking at $G{=}2$.\\n\\nA formal variance analysis of the group baseline as a function of $G$\\nand within-prompt success probability $p$ is given by the closed-form\\nidentity in Eq.~\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:zvf_identity}: zero-variance prompts dominate\\nthe regime $p\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\to\\\\\\\\!0$ or $p\\\\\\\\!\\\\\\\\to\\\\\\\\!1$, and gradient signal is maximised\\nat intermediate $p$. Our group-size ablation\\n(Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:results}) is consistent with this picture and\\nidentifies $G{=}8$ as a practical default at the model and task scales\\ntested; we do not claim a population-level optimum across regimes.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew2 = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{GRPO, Group Baselines, and Sample Efficiency}\\n\\nMathematical, code-generation, and structured-output tasks often admit\\n\\\\\\\\emph{verifiable} rewards---rewards computed by deterministic checks rather\\nthan by learned reward models.\\nCobbe et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{cobbe2021training} used a learned verifier trained on\\nGSM8K, and Lightman et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{lightman2023let} extended this to per-step\\nprocess rewards; these improve supervision but still require learned models.\\nDeepSeek-Math~\\\\\\\\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} and\\nDeepSeek-R1~\\\\\\\\cite{deepseek2025r1} instead popularised GRPO with outcome-level\\nexact-match rewards, while DAPO~\\\\\\\\cite{yu2025dapo} revisits clipping and\\ngroup-sampling defaults at production scale.\\n\\nAt the algorithmic level, GRPO is a critic-free REINFORCE baseline: for each\\nprompt it samples $G$ completions, centres their rewards by the group mean,\\nand increases the log-probability of completions whose reward exceeds that\\nmean.\\nRLOO~\\\\\\\\cite{ahmadian2024rloo} is the nearest leave-one-out variant.\\nOpenRLHF~\\\\\\\\cite{openrlhf2024}, TRL~\\\\\\\\cite{trl2020}, and\\nveRL~\\\\\\\\cite{verl2024} expose GRPO-style training in open stacks, but nominally\\nsimilar runs can still differ in batching, clipping, rollout reuse, and reward\\nnormalisation; our backend comparison is designed to make that implementation\\ngap visible.\\n\\nThe same group baseline makes $G$ a sample-efficiency knob rather than a\\nharmless batch-size detail.\\nLarger $G$ can reduce estimator noise but increases rollout cost and can\\nerase gradient signal when rewards are uniform; smaller $G$ is cheaper but\\nnoisier and, in our runs, enters a collapse regime at $G{=}2$.\\nFor binary rewards, Eq.~\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:zvf_identity} formalises when prompts\\nbecome zero-variance, with gradient signal maximised away from the all-wrong\\nand all-correct extremes.\\nOur ablation identifies $G{=}8$ as a practical default only for the tested\\nmodel, task, and backend scales.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\norig3 = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{Reward Hacking and Length Bias}\\n\\nReward hacking --- the phenomenon whereby a policy exploits proxy-reward\\nidiosyncrasies rather than solving the intended task --- is a well-documented\\nfailure mode in RL from learned reward models~\\\\\\\\cite{skalse2022specificationgaming}.\\nFor verifiable rewards the failure mode manifests differently: length bias,\\nwhere models learn to produce longer outputs that satisfy formatting\\nrequirements without improving reasoning quality.\\nYuan et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{yuan2024free} and Singhal et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{singhal2023long}\\ndocument length bias in RLHF\\\\\\\\@; we extend the analysis to GRPO and propose\\nthe reward-stability coefficient as an online detector.\\n\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Tool-Use and Function Calling}\\n\\nEnabling LLMs to invoke external tools via structured API calls is an active\\nresearch area~\\\\\\\\cite{schick2023toolformer,qin2023toolllm}.\\nThe Salesforce xLAM family~\\\\\\\\cite{liu2024xlam} provides both training data\\nand evaluation benchmarks for function-calling.\\nWe use xLAM-60k as a second task to test whether GRPO's benefits on\\narithmetic generalise to structured output generation with schema-validity\\nrewards.\\n\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning}\\n\\nKaplan et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{kaplan2020scaling} established power-law scaling for\\npretraining loss; whether similar laws hold for RL fine-tuning reward is\\nless clear.\\nSnell et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{snell2024scaling} provide early evidence that\\ncompute-optimal test-time compute exhibits different scaling behaviour\\nthan pretraining loss; whether analogous scaling laws hold for the RL\\nfine-tuning reward signal is to our knowledge an open empirical question.\\nOur cross-model experiments (0.6B to 14B) provide additional data points on\\nreward scaling as a function of parameter count.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew3 = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\\\\\subsection{Failure Modes in Sparse and Structured Rewards}\\n\\nReward hacking --- the phenomenon whereby a policy exploits proxy-reward\\nidiosyncrasies rather than solving the intended task --- is a well-documented\\nfailure mode in RL from learned reward models~\\\\\\\\cite{skalse2022specificationgaming}.\\nFor verifiable rewards the proxy is deterministic but still incomplete: an\\noutput can satisfy length or formatting correlates without improving reasoning,\\nand a strict verifier can assign zero reward to every rollout.\\nYuan et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{yuan2024free} and Singhal et al.~\\\\\\\\cite{singhal2023long}\\ndocument length bias in RLHF\\\\\\\\@; our reward-stability coefficient logs whether\\nreward tracks verbosity rather than correctness during GRPO.\\n\\nFunction calling makes the sparse-reward failure mode concrete.\\nToolformer and ToolLLM study structured API invocation\\n~\\\\\\\\cite{schick2023toolformer,qin2023toolllm}, while the Salesforce xLAM\\nfamily~\\\\\\\\cite{liu2024xlam} provides data and benchmarks for function-calling.\\nWe use xLAM-60k not as a general agent benchmark but as a schema-validity\\nstress test: the reward is exactly checkable, sparse, and capable of producing\\nthe all-wrong-collapse regime predicted by Eq.~\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:zvf_identity}.\\n\\n\\\\\\\\subsection{Empirical Gap Addressed by This Audit}\\n\\nScaling-law work on pretraining and test-time compute provides useful context\\n~\\\\\\\\cite{kaplan2020scaling,snell2024scaling}, but our evidence is not a scaling\\nlaw.\\nThe gap we target is narrower: whether critic-free RLVR supplies usable\\nwithin-prompt signal under limited compute, multiple backends, multiple group\\nsizes, and two reward regimes.\\nThis positions the paper as a diagnostic and reproducibility audit rather than\\nas a claim that GRPO improves monotonically with scale.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport re\\ndef wc(s): \\n    return len(re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b[\\\\w{}=\\\\\\\\'-]+\\\\b\\\", s))\\nfor name,o,n in [('1',orig1,new1),('2',orig2,new2),('3',orig3,new3)]:\\n    print(name, wc(o), wc(n), \\\"delta\\\", wc(n)-wc(o), \\\"lines\\\", len(o.splitlines()), len(n.splitlines()))\\n\",\"ct\":1777864992.611361},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"Section 2’s new issue is structural: six micro-subsections fragment the positioning, and the stand-alone Scaling Laws subsection likely needs folding or deletion to foreground the GRPO/ZVF diagnostic gap.\",\"ct\":1777865036.441051},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn813864search0\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"short\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777865066.580517},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"My recommendation: **reduce Section 2 from 6 subsections to 4** and use the saved space to make the paper’s real positioning sharper: **critic-free verifiable-reward RL + group-size/ZVF diagnostics + sparse structured-output rewards**.\\n\\n## (a) Least-contributing subsection\\n\\n**Least useful: `2.6 Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning`, lines 274–284.**\\n\\nIt contributes least because the paper does **not** make a scaling-law claim. The results actually report a **Qwen3-14B regression/anomaly**, not a monotonic scaling trend. As written, this subsection creates a reviewer expectation that the paper will test RL fine-tuning scaling laws, but Section 4 instead presents a diagnostic audit and a small cross-model case study.\\n\\nSpecific deletion:\\n\\n```tex\\n% Delete lines 274--284 entirely:\\n\\\\subsection{Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning}\\n...\\nOur cross-model experiments (0.6B to 14B) provide additional data points on\\nreward scaling as a function of parameter count.\\n```\\n\\nBecause the bibliography is inline, also delete the now-unused bibliography entries:\\n\\n```tex\\n% Delete lines 723--727:\\n\\\\bibitem{kaplan2020scaling}\\n...\\n\\n% Delete lines 793--797:\\n\\\\bibitem{snell2024scaling}\\n...\\n```\\n\\nThat gives real page budget back instead of leaving uncited references in the rendered bibliography.\\n\\n## (b) Weak transitions\\n\\nThe main weak transitions are:\\n\\n1. **2.1 → 2.2:** The text jumps from DPO/ORPO/SimPO to verifiable rewards without explicitly saying why offline preference optimisation is the wrong comparison point for this paper. Add a bridge: preference methods remove online exploration, while GRPO is relevant because exact-match tasks permit online sampling with automatic rewards.\\n\\n2. **2.2 → 2.3:** The final paragraph of 2.2 discusses implementations/backends, then 2.3 suddenly moves to group size. The transition should say that across GRPO implementations, the most exposed algorithmic knob is group size `G`.\\n\\n3. **2.3 → 2.4:** Group-size collapse and reward hacking are currently separated even though the paper’s own story links them. Merge these into one subsection on **group size, advantage collapse, and reward gaming**.\\n\\n4. **2.4 → 2.5:** Length bias and tool-use appear unrelated. The bridge should be: sparse exact-match rewards are not only a math issue; schema-valid function calling is another binary-reward regime where all-wrong collapse can occur.\\n\\n5. **2.5 → 2.6:** Tool-use to scaling laws is the weakest jump. Delete 2.6 and fold the cross-model scope into a short sentence elsewhere.\\n\\n## (c) What a reviewer will flag\\n\\nThe biggest missing positioning is this:\\n\\n**The Related Work never explicitly states that prior GRPO work reports reward/accuracy curves but does not isolate the per-prompt zero-variance fraction as a first-class diagnostic.** Since the title foregrounds “Zero-Variance Fraction,” reviewers will ask: *Is ZVF new, or just reward variance / success rate under another name?*\\n\\nAlso flag-worthy:\\n\\n**The current OpenRLHF sentence overclaims.** Lines 227–231 say the paper benchmarks OpenRLHF, TRL, veRL, and Tinker “against each other.” But the methods list Tinker, Modal H100/veRL, and TRL on L4; OpenRLHF is cited as ecosystem context, not actually benchmarked.\\n\\n**The current group-size explanation risks a technical objection.** With binary rewards, the identity later given in Eq. 3 is  \\n\\\\[\\n\\\\mathrm{ZVF}(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G.\\n\\\\]  \\nFor fixed \\\\(0<p<1\\\\), increasing \\\\(G\\\\) generally reduces the probability of an all-identical group. So the paper should not imply that large `G` mechanically increases ZVF. A safer framing is: larger `G` lowers all-identical probability but increases memory cost and may reduce prompt diversity/effective batch size under fixed compute.\\n\\n## Reordered Section 2 structure\\n\\nUse this 4-subsection structure:\\n\\n```tex\\n2.1 Preference RL and the Move to Verifiable Rewards\\n2.2 Critic-Free GRPO, Group Baselines, and Implementations\\n2.3 Group Size, Advantage Collapse, and Reward Gaming\\n2.4 Structured Outputs and Cross-Model Scope\\n```\\n\\nThis keeps every needed idea but removes the “scaling laws” detour.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Diff 1 — sharpen 2.1 transition into verifiable rewards\\n\\nPage-neutral: replaces one paragraph with a similar-length but more positioning-focused paragraph.\\n\\n```diff\\n@@\\n-Rafailov et al.~\\\\cite{rafailov2023direct} proposed Direct Preference\\n-Optimisation (DPO) as an offline alternative that eliminates the reward\\n-model by re-parameterising the preference objective directly in terms of\\n-policy log-ratios.\\n-DPO removes the RL training loop entirely but requires pairwise preference\\n-data and optimises a supervised loss that does not support exploration.\\n-Subsequent variants---ORPO~\\\\cite{hong2024orpo},\\n-SimPO~\\\\cite{meng2024simpo}---share DPO's offline character and inherit its\\n-inability to improve beyond the coverage of the preference dataset.\\n+Offline preference methods define the boundary of our setting.\\n+Rafailov et al.~\\\\cite{rafailov2023direct} proposed Direct Preference\\n+Optimisation (DPO), which removes the learned reward model by rewriting the\\n+preference objective in terms of policy log-ratios.\\n+Subsequent variants---ORPO~\\\\cite{hong2024orpo},\\n+SimPO~\\\\cite{meng2024simpo}---further simplify preference optimisation, but\\n+they remain offline supervised objectives: they require preference pairs and\\n+cannot explore new completions during training.\\n+This motivates the verifiable-reward setting studied here, where task success\\n+can be checked automatically and online RL remains meaningful.\\n```\\n\\n## Diff 2 — make the GRPO/ZVF gap explicit and fix backend overclaim\\n\\nPage-positive: slightly shorte