cit.\\n+This paper makes that failure mode explicit through ZVF and audits whether\\n+it behaves as a diagnostic rather than a cross-task performance predictor.\\n+\\n+OpenRLHF~\\\\cite{openrlhf2024}, TRL~\\\\cite{trl2020}, and veRL~\\\\cite{verl2024}\\n+provide open-source RLHF/GRPO infrastructure.\\n+Our empirical comparison uses TRL/veRL-style open stacks and the closed-source\\n+Tinker SDK, treating backend differences as configuration-level effects rather\\n+than attributing them to a single framework.\\n```\\n\\n## Diff 3 — merge group size, reward hacking, tool-use, and delete scaling-law detour\\n\\nPage-positive: replaces four subsections with two tighter ones. This is the main structural fix.\\n\\n```diff\\n@@\\n-\\\\subsection{Group Size and Sample Efficiency}\\n-\\n-The sensitivity of GRPO to the group size $G$ is a practical concern that\\n-has received little systematic attention.\\n-Larger $G$ produces a lower-variance advantage estimate but increases memory\\n-proportionally and may reduce gradient signal when the group reward\\n-distribution is near-uniform (the high-ZVF regime).\\n-Smaller $G$ is cheaper but produces noisier gradients and, as we show,\\n-collapses to reward hacking at $G{=}2$.\\n-\\n-A formal variance analysis of the group baseline as a function of $G$\\n-and within-prompt success probability $p$ is given by the closed-form\\n-identity in Eq.~\\\\eqref{eq:zvf_identity}: zero-variance prompts dominate\\n-the regime $p\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!0$ or $p\\\\!\\\\to\\\\!1$, and gradient signal is maximised\\n-at intermediate $p$. Our group-size ablation\\n-(Section~\\\\ref{sec:results}) is consistent with this picture and\\n-identifies $G{=}8$ as a practical default at the model and task scales\\n-tested; we do not claim a population-level optimum across regimes.\\n-\\n-\\\\subsection{Reward Hacking and Length Bias}\\n-\\n-Reward hacking --- the phenomenon whereby a policy exploits proxy-reward\\n-idiosyncrasies rather than solving the intended task --- is a well-documented\\n-failure mode in RL from learned reward models~\\\\cite{skalse2022specificationgaming}.\\n-For verifiable rewards the failure mode manifests differently: length bias,\\n-where models learn to produce longer outputs that satisfy formatting\\n-requirements without improving reasoning quality.\\n-Yuan et al.~\\\\cite{yuan2024free} and Singhal et al.~\\\\cite{singhal2023long}\\n-document length bias in RLHF\\\\@; we extend the analysis to GRPO and propose\\n-the reward-stability coefficient as an online detector.\\n-\\n-\\\\subsection{Tool-Use and Function Calling}\\n-\\n-Enabling LLMs to invoke external tools via structured API calls is an active\\n-research area~\\\\cite{schick2023toolformer,qin2023toolllm}.\\n-The Salesforce xLAM family~\\\\cite{liu2024xlam} provides both training data\\n-and evaluation benchmarks for function-calling.\\n-We use xLAM-60k as a second task to test whether GRPO's benefits on\\n-arithmetic generalise to structured output generation with schema-validity\\n-rewards.\\n-\\n-\\\\subsection{Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning}\\n-\\n-Kaplan et al.~\\\\cite{kaplan2020scaling} established power-law scaling for\\n-pretraining loss; whether similar laws hold for RL fine-tuning reward is\\n-less clear.\\n-Snell et al.~\\\\cite{snell2024scaling} provide early evidence that\\n-compute-optimal test-time compute exhibits different scaling behaviour\\n-than pretraining loss; whether analogous scaling laws hold for the RL\\n-fine-tuning reward signal is to our knowledge an open empirical question.\\n-Our cross-model experiments (0.6B to 14B) provide additional data points on\\n-reward scaling as a function of parameter count.\\n+\\\\subsection{Group Size, Advantage Collapse, and Reward Gaming}\\n+\\n+The sensitivity of GRPO to the group size $G$ is a practical concern that\\n+has received little systematic attention.\\n+Larger $G$ lowers sampling noise and reduces all-identical binary groups away\\n+from the extremes, but it increases memory cost and can reduce prompt diversity\\n+under a fixed compute budget.\\n+Smaller $G$ is cheaper but noisier and can make the policy overreact to sparse\\n+correct samples.\\n+We therefore treat $G$ as a diagnostic knob rather than search for a universal\\n+optimum: in binary-reward GRPO, a prompt contributes no advantage signal when\\n+all $G$ completions receive the same reward, whether all are wrong or all are\\n+correct.\\n+The closed-form identity in Eq.~\\\\eqref{eq:zvf_identity} makes this zero-signal\\n+regime explicit and motivates ZVF as a per-step audit quantity.\\n+\\n+Reward hacking --- the phenomenon whereby a policy exploits proxy-reward\\n+idiosyncrasies rather than solving the intended task --- is a well-documented\\n+failure mode in RL from learned reward models~\\\\cite{skalse2022specificationgaming}.\\n+Under exact-match rewards, the analogous failure can be mundane but damaging:\\n+models may learn verbosity, formatting shortcuts, or schema padding without\\n+improving task correctness.\\n+Yuan et al.~\\\\cite{yuan2024free} and Singhal et al.~\\\\cite{singhal2023long}\\n+document length bias in RLHF\\\\@; we extend this concern to GRPO by pairing ZVF\\n+with a reward-stability coefficient based on reward--length correlation.\\n+\\n+\\\\subsection{Structured Outputs and Cross-Model Scope}\\n+\\n+Verifiable rewards are not limited to arithmetic.\\n+Tool-use and function-calling tasks require structured API invocations, and\\n+prior work has studied both self-supervised tool use and large API-calling\\n+benchmarks~\\\\cite{schick2023toolformer,qin2023toolllm}.\\n+The Salesforce xLAM family~\\\\cite{liu2024xlam} provides training data and\\n+evaluation tasks for function-calling, making xLAM-60k a natural second\\n+binary-reward setting for testing all-wrong collapse and schema-validity\\n+learning.\\n+Our 0.6B--14B model sweep is used only as empirical scope for these diagnostics,\\n+not as evidence for a scaling law.\\n```\\n\\nNet result: the Related Work becomes more coherent, removes the weakest subsection, fixes an overclaim, and explicitly positions the paper’s core contribution: **ZVF is not a magic accuracy predictor; it is a cheap audit signal for zero-advantage regimes in binary-reward GRPO.**\",\"ct\":1777865410.635891}]},{\"id\":\"69f810bd-6c7c-83e8-93c8-091d793010d9\",\"title\":\"Conference Paper Review\",\"create_time\":1777864895.543913,\"update_time\":1777865332.180389,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nDeep dive on Section 5.6 (Scaling Regression at Qwen3-14B, lines 560-577). This is the paper's most controversial result — Tinker performance regresses relative to the vLLM baseline at 14B. Tasks: (a) Is the framing currently calibrated to the evidence, or does it over- or under-claim? Quote the strongest claim verbatim. (b) What alternative explanations should be acknowledged (data, hyperparams, hardware, RNG)? (c) What single experiment, if added later, would solidify the claim? (d) Provide a diff-style rewrite of just this subsection that maximizes the chance of a reviewer accepting the negative result. Page-neutral.\",\"ct\":1777864893.025},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll inspect Section 5.6 in the TeX and cross-check the PDF for wording and page impact, then provide a calibrated critique plus a page-neutral diff rewrite.\",\"ct\":1777864911.434343},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bas