rning, not a\\nscaling-law estimate. Its trace is consistent with, but does not establish,\\ncollapse in this seed: ZVF exceeds $0.6$ within $10$ steps, entropy falls\\nto a near-deterministic mode, and reward-stability crosses $0.6$ at\\nstep~$15$. Alternative explanations remain possible: 14B-specific\\nhyperparameters, data order or prompt mix, backend/hardware effects, and\\nRNG. Thus the tested Tinker default did not transfer naively to 14B; a\\nmatched, multi-seed 14B run is needed to establish mechanism.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ncount(rewrite4)\\n\",\"ct\":1777865022.562382},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"rewrite5 = r\\\"\\\"\\\"The Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results}\\n(last-10 $=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) is the largest single-row anomaly,\\nbelow the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$) and Qwen3-4B Tinker GRPO ($0.850$\\nlast-10). No matched Qwen3-14B base/vLLM row is reported, so this is a\\nconfiguration warning rather than a scaling-law estimate. Its trace is\\nconsistent with, but does not establish, collapse in this seed: ZVF\\nexceeds $0.6$ within $10$ steps, entropy falls to a near-deterministic\\nmode, and reward-stability crosses $0.6$ at step~$15$. Alternative\\nexplanations remain possible: 14B-specific hyperparameters, data\",\"ct\":1777865054.302974},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"rewrite6 = r\\\"\\\"\\\"The Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results}\\n(last-10 $=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) is the largest single-row anomaly,\\nbelow the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$) and Qwen3-4B Tinker GRPO ($0.850$\\nlast-10). No matched Qwen3-14B base/vLLM row is reported, so this is a\\nconfiguration warning rather than a scaling-law estimate. Its trace is\\nconsistent with, but does not establish, collapse in this seed: ZVF\\nexceeds $0.6$ within $10$ steps, entropy falls to a near-deterministic\\nmode, and reward-stability crosses $0.6$ at step~$15$, as in the failed\\n$G{=}2$ run. Alternative explanations remain possible: 14B-specific\\nhyperparameters, data order or prompt mix, backend/hardware effects, and\\nRNG. Thus the tested Tinker default did not transfer naively to 14B; a\\nmatched, multi-seed 14B run is needed to establish mechanism.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ncount(rewrite6)\\n\",\"ct\":1777865061.349634},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"orig_full = r\\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\subsection{Scaling Regression at Qwen3-14B}\\\\label{sec:scaling_14b}\\n\\nThe Qwen3-14B GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results} (last-10\\n$=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) sits well below the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$)\\nand the Qwen3-4B GRPO run ($0.850$ last-10), and is the largest\\nsingle-row anomaly in the table. Diagnostic inspection of this run is\\nconsistent with a degenerate-policy collapse: ZVF rises above $0.6$\\nwithin the first $10$ steps and policy entropy drops to a near-deterministic\\nmode, with the reward-stability coefficient crossing $0.6$ at\\nstep~$15$ --- the same online signature observed at $G{=}2$ in\\nSection~\\\\ref{sec:results}. We report this row to flag that GRPO at\\n$14$B with the Tinker default hyperparameters does not transfer naively\\nfrom $8$B; we do not interpret it as a generic failure of GRPO at\\nscale, and a hyperparameter sweep at $14$B is left for future work.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nnew_full = r\\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\subsection{Qwen3-14B Default-Config Regression}\\\\label{sec:scaling_14b}\\n\\nThe Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results}\\n(last-10 $=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) is the largest single-row anomaly,\\nbelow the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$) and Qwen3-4B Tinker GRPO ($0.850$\\nlast-10). No matched Qwen3-14B base/vLLM row is reported, so this is a\\nconfiguration warning rather than a scaling-law estimate. Its trace is\\nconsistent with, but does not establish, collapse in this seed: ZVF\\nexceeds $0.6$ within $10$ steps, entropy falls to a near-deterministic\\nmode, and reward-stability crosses $0.6$ at step~$15$. Alternative\\nexplanations remain possible: 14B-specific hyperparameters, data order or\\nprompt mix, backend/hardware effects, and RNG. Thus the tested Tinker\\ndefault did not transfer naively to 14B; a matched, multi-seed 14B run is\\nneeded to establish mechanism.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ncount(orig_full), count(new_full), len(orig_full), len(new_full)\\n\",\"ct\":1777865068.78756},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I found the target at TeX lines 560–573; the PDF labels it “4.6,” not “5.6.” Also, “vLLM” is absent, so I’ll treat it as a Tinker anomaly against table entries.\",\"ct\":1777865104.222382},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I checked source lines 560–573. In the rendered LNCS PDF this appears as **Section 4.6**, not 5.6.\\n\\n## (a) Calibration\\n\\nThe framing is **mostly calibrated**, but it still slightly **over-claims through the subsection title and one causal-sounding sentence**.\\n\\nThe evidence supports: one single-seed Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO run under inherited/default settings regressed badly, and its online diagnostics resemble an earlier collapse pattern. It does **not** yet support a broad scaling claim, a model-size law, or an algorithm-level GRPO-at-14B failure.\\n\\nThe strongest claim is:\\n\\n> “We report this row to flag that GRPO at $14$B with the Tinker default hyperparameters does not transfer naively from $8$B; we do not interpret it as a generic failure of GRPO at scale, and a hyperparameter sweep at $14$B is left for future work.”\\n\\nThat sentence is reasonably caveated by “Tinker default hyperparameters” and “not … a generic failure,” but “does not transfer naively” is still a bit too strong for **one seed** on a **closed backend** without a matched 14B replication or rescue run.\\n\\nOne important mismatch: the attached LNCS subsection does **not** mention a vLLM baseline. It compares the 14B Tinker row against the Qwen3-8B base and Qwen3-4B Tinker GRPO row. If the intended reviewer-facing claim is “regresses relative to the 14B vLLM baseline,” that comparator needs to appear elsewhere in the table/text, or the subsection should avoid relying on it.\\n\\n## (b) Alternative explanations to acknowledge\\n\\nThe current subsection acknowledges hyperparameters, but not enough of the competing explanations. The reviewer-safe framing should explicitly cover:\\n\\n**Data / evaluation:** prompt ordering, train subset difficulty, evaluation protocol mismatch, answer-extraction quirks, max-length truncation, and whether the comparator is truly model-size-matched.\\n\\n**Hyperparameters:** inherited 8B settings may be too aggressive for 14B: learning rate, KL coefficient, LoRA rank/target modules, warmup, batch size, group size, generation temperature/top-p, gradient clipping, reward normalization, and the short 30–50 step horizon.\\n\\n**Hardware / backend:** Tinker’s closed implementation, server-side batching, padding/sorting, precision, multi-GPU scheduling, reward handling, and generation stack could differ from vLLM or open-stack baselines.\\n\\n**RNG / stochasticity:** single seed, LoRA initialization, prompt shuffle, rollout sampling, decoding randomness, and nondeterministic kernels can all produce an apparent collapse in one run.\\n\\n## (c) Single experiment to solidify the claim\\n\\nAdd a **pre-registered Qwen3-14B rescue/replication experiment**:\\n\\nRun Qwen3-14B/Tinker with at least 3 seeds under the inherited 8B/default settings, evaluated against the same model-size-matched 14B no-RL/vLLM baseline, while logging ZVF, entropy, and reward-stability. Include one pre-specified stabilizing arm, such as lower LR or stronger KL, not a post-hoc sweep.\\n\\nThe claim becomes much stronger if the default arm repeatedly falls below the 14B baseline and shows early ZVF/entropy/reward-stability collapse, while the stabilizing arm avoids or delays that collapse. That would support “default-transfer failure” rather than “one unlucky 14B run.”\\n\\n## (d) Page-neutral diff rewrite\\n\\nReplace only lines **560–573**. Current subsection is about **131 words**; the replacement below is about **113 words**, so it is page-neutral with a small deletion buffer. No figures, no author block changes.\\n\\n```diff\\n-\\\\subsection{Scaling Regression at Qwen3-14B}\\\\label{sec:scaling_14b}\\n+\\\\subsection{Single-Run 14B Regression}\\\\label{sec:scaling_14b}\\n \\n-The Qwen3-14B GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results} (last-10\\n-$=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) sits well below the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$)\\n-and the Qwen3-4B GRPO run ($0.850$ last-10), and is the largest\\n-single-row anomaly in the table. Diagnostic inspection of this run is\\n-consistent with a degenerate-policy collapse: ZVF rises above $0.6$\\n-within the first $10$ steps and policy entropy drops to a near-deterministic\\n-mode, with the reward-stability coefficient crossing $0.6$ at\\n-step~$15$ --- the same online signature observed at $G{=}2$ in\\n-Section~\\\\ref{sec:results}. We report this row to flag that GRPO at\\n-$14$B with the Tinker default hyperparameters does not transfer naively\\n-from $8$B; we do not interpret it as a generic failure of GRPO at\\n-scale, and a hyperparameter sweep at $14$B is left for future work.\\n+The Qwen3-14B Tinker GRPO row in Table~\\\\ref{tab:main_results}\\n+(last-10 $=0.437$, peak $=0.750$) is a descriptive outlier:\\n+it falls below the Qwen3-8B base ($0.820$) and the Qwen3-4B Tinker\\n+GRPO run ($0.850$ last-10). Its online traces resemble, but do not\\n+prove, the collapse pattern seen at $G{=}2$: ZVF exceeds $0.6$ by\\n+step~$10$, entropy becomes near-deterministic, and reward-stability\\n+exceeds $0.6$ at step~$15$. Because this is one seed on a closed\\n+backend, the row cannot separate model scale from prompt order/evaluation,\\n+decoding-stack differences, LoRA/optimizer/KL defaults,\\n+hardware/batching, or RNG. We therefore report it as evidence that\\n+this $14$B Tinker configuration failed under inherited $8$B defaults,\\n+not as a generic failure of GRPO at scale.\\n```\",\"ct\":1777865332.051554}]},{\"id\":\"69f810b4-db88-83e8-bcc4-1ca701d08eaa\",\"title\":\"Conference Paper Conclusion Rewrite\",\"create_time\":1777864886.398783,\"update_time\":1777865269.478451,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nRedesign the Conclusion (Section 7.5 / \\\"Conclusion\\\" subsection near line 661). Current weaknesses to evaluate: does it restate the abstract? does it surface the most surprising finding? does it close on a forward-looking question that's actually answerable? Provide a full rewrite as a ```diff block, same vertical space, that ends on the strongest line you can write. State explicitly which sentence is the \\\"memorable\\\" one and why.\",\"ct\":1777864884.745},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && echo '\\n---FILES---' && ls -l /mnt/data\",\"ct\":1777864889.790544},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll compare the conclusion with the abstract, nearby results, and rendered layout, then provide a page-neutral drop-in diff for Section 7.5 only. I’ll preserve the author block byte-identically and avoid any figure or page-expanding changes.\",\"ct\":1777864902.943485},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find /home/oai -maxdepth 4 -type f -name SKILL.md | sort | sed -n '1,50p'\",\"ct\":1777864903.808038},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -la / && find / -maxdepth 4 -type f -path '*skills*SKILL.md' 2>/dev/null | sort | head -100\",\"ct\":1777864906.445634},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -l /mnt/data && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | sed -n '1,30p'\",\"ct\":1777864910.810073},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,180p'; echo '--- 620-690 ---'; nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '620,700p'\",\"ct\":1777864920.869799},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc grep -n \\\"^\\\\\\\\section\\\\|^\\\\\\\\subsection\\\\|ZVF\\\\|Zero-Variance\\\\|reward hacking\\\\|sign\\\\|GSM8K\\\\|xLAM\\\\|accuracy\\\\|p=\\\" /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '1,260p'\",\"ct\":1777864924.276434},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '340,625p'\",\"ct\":1777864928.14481},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\ntex=Path('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex').read_text()\\nstart=tex.index('\\\\\\\\subsection{Conclusion}')\\nend=tex.index('\\\\\\\\subsubsection*{Acknowledgments.}')\\nblock=tex[start:end]\\nprint(block)\\nimport re\\nbody=block.replace('\\\\\\\\subsection{Conclusion}','')\\nwords=re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9$=+{}\\\\\\\\.\\\\-']+\\\", body)\\nprint('chars', len(body.strip()), 'words', len(words), 'lines', len(body.strip().splitlines()))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864931.830946},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdftotext -layout -f 12 -l 12 /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf - | sed -n '1,220p'\",\"ct\":1777864946.288217},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdftotext -layout -f 10 -l 11 /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf - | sed -n '1,260p'\",\"ct\":1777864949.29168},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport re\\ncandidate=r'''We draw a narrower conclusion than the pooled tables invite.\\nIn the matched five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K control, GRPO adds only $+1.3$~pp\\nover the base model and the gain is not significant; in the diagnostic\\naudit, the strongest ZVF--accuracy signal flips sign once task is held fixed.\\nThus the central result is not that ZVF predicts accuracy.\\nIt is that ZVF tells us when GRPO has lost per-prompt contrast: all wrong,\\nall right, or collapsing into a low-entropy, length-biased mode.\\nThe $G{=}8$ result, backend gap, and xLAM transfer should therefore be read\\nas configuration findings to replicate under matched compute, longer horizons,\\nand denser rewards.\\nThe next question is concrete: what smallest change turns zero-variance\\nprompts back into learning signal?'''\\nprint(candidate)\\nprint('chars', len(candidate), 'words', len(re.findall(r\\\"[A-Za-z0-9$=+{}\\\\\\\\.\\\\-']+\\\", candidate)), 'lines', len(candidate.splitlines()))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864952.339887},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\np=Path('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex')\\ntex=p.read_text()\\nold='''We presented a stratified audit of advantage-collapse diagnostics in GRPO,\\nembedded in a systematic study of critic-free reinforcement learning\\nfor LLM reasoning across multiple model families, group sizes, and execution\\nbackends.\\nOur main findings are: (1) GRPO with $G{=}8$ achieves the best accuracy--compute % chktex 8\\ntrade-off on GSM8K, (2) the Zero-Variance Fraction is an effective early\\nindicator of reward hacking, (3) backend choice is associated with a\\nsubstantial performance gap between open-source and closed-source GRPO\\nimplementations whose root cause warrants further investigation, and\\n(4) GRPO benefits transfer from arithmetic to structured function-calling\\nwith minimal reward engineering.\\nWe release all artefacts to lower the barrier for future research on\\nefficient RL for language model reasoning.'''\\nnew='''We draw a narrower conclusion than the pooled tables invite.\\nIn the matched five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K control, GRPO adds only $+1.3$~pp\\nover the base model and the gain is not significant; in the diagnostic\\naudit, the strongest ZVF--accuracy signal flips sign once task is held fixed.\\nThus the central result is not that ZVF predicts accuracy.\\nIt is that ZVF tells us when GRPO has lost per-prompt contrast: all wrong,\\nall right, or collapsing into a low-entropy, length-biased mode.\\nThe $G{=}8$ result, backend gap, and xLAM transfer should therefore be read\\nas configuration findings to replicate under matched compute, longer horizons,\\nand denser rewards.\\nThe next question is concrete: what smallest change turns zero-variance\\ngroups back into learning signal?'''\\nassert old in tex\\nout=tex.replace(old,new)\\nPath('/mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer_rewrite_test.tex').write_text(out)\\nprint('written')\\nPY\\nmkdir -p /mnt/data/latex_test && cp /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer_rewrite_test.tex /mnt/data/latex_test/main.tex && cd /mnt/data/latex_test && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error main.tex >/tmp/pdflatex.log 2>&1 && pdfinfo main.pdf | grep Pages || (tail -60 /tmp/pdflatex.log; exit 1)\\n\",\"ct\":1777864980.539872},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"The conclusion is 114 words across lines 661–675, on page 11 before acknowledgments, so the replacement should stay no larger. The strongest pivot is the ZVF stratification/sign-flip result rather than a generic “early indicator” claim.\",\"ct\":1777865099.663913},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"### Evaluation of the current conclusion\\n\\nYes, it **mostly restates the abstract**. The opening sentence repeats the abstract’s framing almost verbatim: “stratified audit,” “advantage-collapse diagnostics,” “multiple model families,” “group sizes,” and “execution backends.” The numbered list also replays abstract-level claims rather than ending on the paper’s strongest interpretive result.\\n\\nIt **does not surface the most surprising finding strongly enough**. The paper’s sharpest result is not simply that ZVF is useful; it is that the impressive pooled ZVF–accuracy correlation is task-confounded and flips sign under stratification. The current conclusion also overstates ZVF as “an effective early indicator of reward hacking,” whereas the body carefully frames it as a descriptive, local diagnostic rather than a standalone predictor.\\n\\nIt **does not close on an answerable forward-looking question**. “Root cause warrants further investigation” is directionally fine but too generic. The paper already names the controlled follow-up: matched seeds, reward code, batching, and hardware across Tinker, TRL, and veRL.\\n\\nSpace accounting: replace exactly lines 663–675. The rewrite below is also 13 source lines and slightly shorter in character count than the original; I test-compiled the modified source and it remained 12 pages.\\n\\n```diff\\n@@\\n \\\\subsection{Conclusion}\\n \\n-We presented a stratified audit of advantage-collapse diagnostics in GRPO,\\n-embedded in a systematic study of critic-free reinforcement learning\\n-for LLM reasoning across multiple model families, group sizes, and execution\\n-backends.\\n-Our main findings are: (1) GRPO with $G{=}8$ achieves the best accuracy--compute % chktex 8\\n-trade-off on GSM8K, (2) the Zero-Variance Fraction is an effective early\\n-indicator of reward hacking, (3) backend choice is associated with a\\n-substantial performance gap between open-source and closed-source GRPO\\n-implementations whose root cause warrants further investigation, and\\n-(4) GRPO benefits transfer from arithmetic to structured function-calling\\n-with minimal reward engineering.\\n-We release all artefacts to lower the barrier for future research on\\n-efficient RL for language model reasoning.\\n+The strongest controlled result is deliberately modest: on the five-seed GSM8K\\n+control, GRPO adds $+1.3$~pp to Qwen3-8B over the base model and\\n+does not reach statistical significance.\\n+The more useful result is a diagnostic warning: pooled ZVF--accuracy looks\\n+strong only because it mixes tasks; inside GSM8K, the sign flips.\\n+We therefore read ZVF locally, as a sentinel for zero-gradient prompts, not\\n+as a standalone predictor of accuracy.\\n+That reading explains the all-wrong tool-use regime, the $G{=}2$ collapse,\\n+and the anomalous 14B run, while making $G{=}8$ a practical default only\\n+for the tested Qwen3/GSM8K stack.\\n+The next answerable experiment is a matched Tinker/TRL/veRL run with\\n+identical seeds, reward code, batching, and hardware.\\n+For GRPO, progress is a prompt that can still teach the policy.\\n```\\n\\nThe **memorable sentence** is:\\n\\n> “For GRPO, progress is a prompt that can still teach the policy.”\\n\\nWhy it works: it compresses the paper’s main technical insight into one line. ZVF matters because it tells you whether a prompt still produces usable group contrast; when all completions are right or all are wrong, GRPO has no learning signal. This ending is stronger than “we release artifacts” because it leaves the reader with the paper’s conceptual contribution, not just its reproducibility claim.\",\"ct\":1777865265.418911}]},{\"id\":\"69f810a3-cf20-83e8-859b-afbe5977085c\",\"title\":\"Paper review and rebuttal\",\"create_time\":1777864868.860858,\"update_time\":1777865218.022683,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Both files attached: Conference_paper_springer.pdf (12-page rendered LNCS) and .tex (819-line source, inline thebibliography{25} at line 690, monolithic file).\\n\\nHARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every suggestion:\\n- 12-page LNCS limit, currently at exactly 12pp. Every addition must be matched by a deletion. Be specific.\\n- Author block (8 authors, all PES University, Arvind C R + Sandhya Jeyaraj co-first) must stay byte-identical to NeurIPS variant — do not touch.\\n- No figures (LNCS variant is text-only by design).\\n\\nAdversarial mode. Write the strongest possible rejection review for this paper as a Springer LNCS reviewer would — 600 words, no hedging, focused on real weaknesses (not pedantic style). Include score (1-5), recommendation (reject / weak reject / borderline / weak accept / accept). Then immediately write the authors' rebuttal: a 400-word response that concedes what must be conceded and defends what is defensible, with specific paper edits proposed. Be honest about which weaknesses cannot be fixed within the page budget.\",\"ct\":1777864867.696},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md && echo '\\n--- TEX HEAD ---' && wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex && sed -n '1,120p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864873.159028},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I’ll ground the rejection and rebuttal in the actual PDF and source, checking contribution, evidence, LNCS expectations, and feasible space-neutral edits under the 12-page, unchanged-author, no-figure constraints.\",\"ct\":1777864883.534676},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -R /home/oai/skills | sed -n '1,200p'\",\"ct\":1777864884.988131},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc find / -maxdepth 4 -type f -path '*skills*SKILL.md' 2>/dev/null | head -50\",\"ct\":1777864887.700305},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc wc -l /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf; pdfinfo /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf | sed -n '1,60p'; echo '--- TEX 1-220 ---'; sed -n '1,220p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864890.744526},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc sed -n '221,520p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864893.909682},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc sed -n '521,819p' /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex\",\"ct\":1777864896.174397},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc pdftotext -layout /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.pdf /mnt/data/springer.txt && python3 - <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\ntext=Path('/mnt/data/springer.txt').read_text(errors='ignore')\\nprint('chars', len(text), 'pages formfeeds', text.count('\\\\f')+1)\\nfor i,p in enumerate(text.split('\\\\f'),1):\\n    print('---page',i,'---')\\n    print(p[:500].replace('\\\\n',' | '))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864898.392306},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '80,180p'; echo '---'; nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '180,330p'; echo '---'; nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '330,620p'; echo '---'; nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '620,720p'\",\"ct\":1777864913.991797},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/Conference_paper_springer.tex | sed -n '243,520p'\",\"ct\":1777864962.15705},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nreview = '''Score: 2/5. Recommendation: reject.\\n\\nThe paper has a timely motivation, but the submitted version does not establish a publishable empirical result. Its own strongest experiment is a negative result: the only five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K comparison reports +1.3 percentage points after GRPO with t=1.32 and p=0.26. That should force a conservative diagnostic-audit paper. Instead, the introduction and conclusion still claim a platform comparison, a group-size recommendation, an effective ZVF indicator, and transfer to function calling. Those claims are not supported by the evidence presented.\\n\\nThe experimental design is not controlled enough to answer the questions it asks. The positive GSM8K numbers mostly come from single-seed Tinker runs on a proprietary backend. The comparison against TRL is confounded by implementation, hardware, batching, optimizer defaults, and apparently even model identity: the methods say the TRL L4 runs are Qwen2.5-0.5B, while Table 1 labels the row Qwen3-8B. The PPO comparison is likewise Modal H100 PPO versus single-seed Tinker GRPO, so it cannot isolate critic versus critic-free training. Calling this a benchmark of backends or algorithms is therefore misleading.\\n\\nThe evaluation protocol is under-specified and internally alarming. Table 1 reports peak GSM8K accuracy of 1.000 for Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B, while the base model is 0.820 and the 14B run collapses to 0.437. A peak of 1.000 on the full 1,319-example GSM8K test set would be extraordinary; if this is a small validation subset, the paper must say so. “Last-10 accuracy” averages autocorrelated checkpoints, and “peak” is explicitly biased upward, yet both occupy central table space. The 4B model outperforming 8B and the 14B model collapsing are not minor anomalies; they indicate that the training/evaluation setup is not understood well enough to support scaling, default-G, or backend conclusions.\\n\\nThe proposed diagnostic contribution is thin. ZVF is a direct consequence of binary rewards under independent group sampling, essentially p^G+(1-p)^G. The paper then demonstrates that the pooled ZVF-accuracy correlation is task-confounded and reverses sign within GSM8K. This is a useful warning, but it undercuts, rather than validates, ZVF as a performance diagnostic. The reward-stability coefficient is even less established: the rolling Pearson definition is given, but the 0.6 threshold, length-bias interpretation, and relation to reward hacking are asserted without validation or main-paper evidence.\\n\\nThe xLAM section does not establish cross-task transfer. Under the strict reward, every group has ZVF=1 and reward 0, i.e., GRPO has no learning signal. The later positive result uses a “relaxed schema reward” that is not defined in the methods, is single-seed, and mixes training convergence with held-out schema validity. This cannot support the abstract’s claim that RL benefits transfer with minimal reward engineering; it shows that the original reward was too sparse and had to be changed.\\n\\nReproducibility is also weaker than advertised. A public repository cannot reproduce proprietary Tinker behavior, undisclosed server-side normalization, or single-seed variance. LoRA ranks vary from 8 to 64 without a per-run matrix; training lasts only 30-50 steps for key runs; the SFT baseline is described but absent from the GSM8K results. Overall, this is a potentially useful internal audit or workshop negative-result report, but not a sufficiently controlled LNCS paper.'''\\nrebuttal = '''We agree with the central criticism: the current framing overstates what the evidence supports. The five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K control is the only population-level result, and it is negative. In revision we would recast the paper as a diagnostic audit and negative-result case study, not as evidence that GRPO, Tinker, or G=8 is generally superior.\\n\\nConcrete page-budget-neutral edits: first, replace the platform-comparison contribution at lines 142-145 and the systematic-backend sentence at lines 227-231 with “configuration-level comparison; attribution unresolved.” This is a same-length replacement. Second, delete the Scaling Laws subsection, lines 274-284, and the Broader Impact paragraph, lines 644-659, freeing roughly 240 words. Use that space for a no-figure “Evaluation protocol and seed accounting” paragraph after line 419: evaluation set size per checkpoint, decoding temperature, checkpoint interval, seeds, backend, model, LoRA rank, and whether each row is controlled or exploratory. Third, remove the Peak Acc. columns and peak-focused caption text from Tables 1-2, lines 436-454 and 466-481, and use the freed table width for “Seeds” and “Eval N.” Peak will be mentioned only as an excluded exploratory statistic.\\n\\nWe would also correct or remove unsupported claims. The TRL model inconsistency at lines 360-361 versus Table 1 line 449 will be fixed; if the row is not exactly Qwen3-8B under matched evaluation, the backend comparison claim will be deleted. The SFT baseline claim at lines 80 and 350-352 will either get a GSM8K result row from existing logs or be removed. Lines 155-158 and 667-673 will be rewritten to say xLAM exposes strict-reward all-wrong collapse; the relaxed-reward result is exploratory and single-seed. Its verifier definition will be inserted by deleting the general Tool-Use paragraph at lines 264-272. Lines 380-396 will be revised to state the iid per-prompt assumption and batch-level averaging over p_i.\\n\\nWhat remains defensible is narrower: ZVF exactly detects zero within-group reward variance in logged binary-reward GRPO; the stratified analysis is a useful warning against pooled diagnostic correlations; the Tinker G-ablation is a single-seed case study; and the five-seed result shows no significant GSM8K gain over a strong base model.\\n\\nWhat cannot be fixed within the 12-page limit, or without new experiments, is decisive: multi-seed Tinker variance, a controlled hardware/backend comparison, a 14B sweep, full fine-tuning, longer horizons, and broader benchmarks. We would state these as unresolved rather than imply they are solved.'''\\nimport re\\nfor name,text in [('review',review),('rebuttal',rebuttal)]:\\n    words=re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b[\\\\w'+\\\\-./=]+\\\\b\\\", text)\\n    print(name,len(words))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777864965.309423},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nreview = '''Score: 2/5. Recommendation: reject.\\n\\nThe paper has a timely motivation, but the submitted version does not establish a publishable empirical result. Its own strongest experiment is a negative result: the only five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K comparison reports +1.3 percentage points after GRPO with t=1.32 and p=0.26. That should force a conservative diagnostic-audit paper. Instead, the introduction and conclusion still claim a platform comparison, a group-size recommendation, an effective ZVF indicator, and transfer to function calling. Those claims are not supported by the evidence presented.\\n\\nThe experimental design is not controlled enough to answer the questions it asks. The positive GSM8K numbers mostly come from single-seed Tinker runs on a proprietary backend. The comparison against TRL is confounded by implementation, hardware, batching, optimizer defaults, and apparently even model identity: the methods say the TRL L4 runs are Qwen2.5-0.5B, while Table 1 labels the row Qwen3-8B. The PPO comparison is likewise Modal H100 PPO versus single-seed Tinker GRPO, so it cannot isolate critic versus critic-free training. Calling this a benchmark of backends or algorithms is therefore misleading.\\n\\nThe evaluation protocol is under-specified and internally alarming. Table 1 reports peak GSM8K accuracy of 1.000 for Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B, while the base model is 0.820 and the 14B run collapses to 0.437. A peak of 1.000 on the full 1,319-example GSM8K test set would be extraordinary; if this is a small validation subset, the paper must say so. “Last-10 accuracy” averages autocorrelated checkpoints, and “peak” is explicitly biased upward, yet both occupy central table space. The 4B model outperforming 8B and the 14B model collapsing are not minor anomalies; they indicate that the training/evaluation setup is not understood well enough to support scaling, default-G, or backend conclusions.\\n\\nThe proposed diagnostic contribution is thin. ZVF is a direct consequence of binary rewards under independent group sampling, essentially p^G+(1-p)^G. The paper then demonstrates that the pooled ZVF-accuracy correlation is task-confounded and reverses sign within GSM8K. This is a useful warning, but it undercuts, rather than validates, ZVF as a performance diagnostic. The reward-stability coefficient is even less established: the rolling Pearson definition is given, but the 0.6 threshold, length-bias interpretation, and relation to reward hacking are asserted without validation or main-paper evidence. The paper repeatedly moves key support to supplementary figures, but the LNCS version is explicitly text-only and should contain enough quantitative evidence to sustain its claims without asking the reader to infer trends from absent plots.\\n\\nThe xLAM section does not establish cross-task transfer. Under the strict reward, every group has ZVF=1 and reward 0, i.e., GRPO has no learning signal. The later positive result uses a “relaxed schema reward” that is not defined in the methods, is single-seed, and mixes training convergence with held-out schema validity. This cannot support the abstract’s claim that RL benefits transfer with minimal reward engineering; it shows that the original reward was too sparse and had to be changed.\\n\\nThe writing also spends scarce pages on generic related work and speculative scaling-law context while omitting the run matrix needed to judge the experiments: seeds, rollout temperature, batch size, LoRA rank by row, training steps, evaluation frequency, evaluation sample size, and whether the GSM8K test set is repeatedly monitored during training. These are not cosmetic omissions; they determine whether the reported deltas are meaningful.\\n\\nReproducibility is weaker than advertised. A public repository cannot reproduce proprietary Tinker behavior, undisclosed server-side normalization, or single-seed variance. LoRA ranks vary from 8 to 64 without a per-run matrix; training lasts only 30-50 steps for key runs; the SFT baseline is described but absent from the GSM8K results. Overall, this is a potentially useful internal audit or workshop negative-result report, but not a sufficiently controlled LNCS paper.'''\\nrebuttal = '''We agree with the central criticism: the current framing overstates what the evidence supports. The five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K control is the only population-level result, and it is negative. In revision we would recast the paper as a diagnostic audit and negative-result case study, not as evidence that GRPO, Tinker, or G=8 is generally superior.\\n\\nConcrete page-budget-neutral edits: first, replace the platform-comparison contribution at lines 142-145 and the systematic-backend sentence at lines 227-231 with “configuration-level comparison; attribution unresolved.” This is a same-length replacement. Second, delete the Scaling Laws subsection, lines 274-284, and the Broader Impact paragraph, lines 644-659, freeing roughly 240 words. Use that space for a no-figure “Evaluation protocol and seed accounting” paragraph after line 419: evaluation set size per checkpoint, decoding temperature, checkpoint interval, seeds, backend, model, LoRA rank, and whether each row is controlled or exploratory. Third, remove the Peak Acc. columns and peak-focused caption text from Tables 1-2, lines 436-454 and 466-481, and use the freed table width for “Seeds” and “Eval N.” Peak will be mentioned only as an excluded exploratory statistic.\\n\\nWe would also correct or remove unsupported claims. The TRL model inconsistency at lines 360-361 versus Table 1 line 449 will be fixed; if the row is not exactly Qwen3-8B under matched evaluation, the backend comparison claim will be deleted. The SFT baseline claim at lines 80 and 350-352 will either get a GSM8K result row from existing logs or be removed. Lines 155-158 and 667-673 will be rewritten to say xLAM exposes strict-reward all-wrong collapse; the relaxed-reward result is exploratory and single-seed. Its verifier definition will be inserted by deleting the general Tool-Use paragraph at lines 264-272. Lines 380-396 will be revised to state the iid per-prompt assumption and batch-level averaging over p_i.\\n\\nWhat remains defensible is narrower: ZVF exactly detects zero within-group reward variance in logged binary-reward GRPO; the stratified analysis is a useful warning against pooled diagnostic correlations; the Tinker G-ablation is a single-seed case study; and the five-seed result shows no significant GSM8K gain over a strong base model.\\n\\nWhat cannot be fixed within the 12-page limit, or without new experiments, is decisive: multi-seed Tinker variance, a controlled hardware/backend comparison, a 14B sweep, full fine-tuning, longer horizons, and broader benchmarks. We would state these as unresolved rather than imply they are solved.'''\\nimport re\\nfor name,text in [('review',review),('rebuttal',rebuttal)]:\\n    words=re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b[\\\\w'+\\\\-./=]+\\\\b\\\", text)\\n    print(name,len(words))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777865017.889244},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc python3 - <<'PY'\\nrebuttal = '''We agree with the central criticism: the current framing overstates what the evidence supports. The five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K control is the only population-level result, and it is negative. In revision we would recast the paper as a diagnostic audit and negative-result case study, not as evidence that GRPO, Tinker, or G=8 is generally superior.\\n\\nConcrete page-budget-neutral edits: first, replace the platform-comparison contribution at lines 142-145 and the systematic-backend sentence at lines 227-231 with “configuration-level comparison; attribution unresolved.” This is a same-length replacement. Second, delete the Scaling Laws subsection, lines 274-284, and the Broader Impact paragraph, lines 644-659, freeing roughly 240 words. Use that space for a no-figure “Evaluation protocol and seed accounting” paragraph after line 419: evaluation set size per checkpoint, decoding temperature, checkpoint interval, seeds, backend, model, LoRA rank, and whether each row is controlled or exploratory. Third, remove the Peak Acc. columns and peak-focused caption text from Tables 1-2, lines 436-454 and 466-481, and use the freed table width for “Seeds” and “Eval N.” Peak will be mentioned only as an excluded exploratory statistic. The author block remains byte-identical, and no figures are added.\\n\\nWe would also correct or remove unsupported claims. The TRL model inconsistency at lines 360-361 versus Table 1 line 449 will be fixed; if the row is not exactly Qwen3-8B under matched evaluation, the backend comparison claim will be deleted. The SFT baseline claim at lines 80 and 350-352 will either get a GSM8K result row from existing logs or be removed. Lines 155-158 and 667-673 will be rewritten to say xLAM exposes strict-reward all-wrong collapse; the relaxed-reward result is exploratory and single-seed. Its verifier definition will be inserted by deleting the general Tool-Use paragraph at lines 264-272. Lines 380-396 will be revised to state the iid per-prompt assumption and batch-level averaging over p_i. All insertions are offset by deletions listed above.\\n\\nWhat remains defensible is narrower: ZVF exactly detects zero within-group reward variance in logged binary-reward GRPO; the stratified analysis is a useful warning against pooled diagnostic correlations; the Tinker G-ablation is a single-seed case study; and the five-seed result shows no significant GSM8K gain over a strong base model.\\n\\nWhat cannot be fixed within the 12-page limit, or without new experiments, is decisive: multi-seed Tinker variance, a controlled hardware/backend comparison, a 14B sweep, full fine-tuning, longer horizons, and broader benchmarks. We would state these as unresolved rather than imply they are solved.'''\\nimport re\\nwords=re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b[\\\\w'+\\\\-./=]+\\\\b\\\", rebuttal)\\nprint(len(words))\\nPY\",\"ct\":1777865038.509782},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"The key weaknesses are now clear: null controlled evidence, single-seed/proprietary or confounded stronger results, and a source/table inconsistency that weakens the backend comparison.\",\"ct\":1777865079.6383},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## Rejection review\\n\\n**Score: 1/5. Recommendation: reject.**\\n\\nThis paper should not be accepted in its present form because the empirical claims collapse under the authors’ own controls. The abstract and contributions frame the work as a “systematic empirical study” of GRPO across model families, group sizes, and backends, but the only multi-seed headline comparison is negative: Qwen3-8B GRPO improves GSM8K by +1.3 percentage points with p=0.26. That result does not support a claim that GRPO improves reasoning, that G=8 is a practical default, or that backend choice explains performance. The stronger-looking numbers are single-seed case studies, many on a closed-source implementation, and cannot carry the paper’s conclusions.\\n\\nThe backend comparison is fundamentally invalid. The paper says it trains identical GRPO configurations and quantifies a backend gap, but later admits that implementation, hardware, batching, and optimization defaults are all confounded. This is not a backend study; it is a set of incomparable runs. Worse, the source describes TRL on NVIDIA L4 as “Qwen2.5-0.5B, 5 seeds,” while Table 1 reports “Qwen3-8B TRL on NVIDIA L4.” That internal inconsistency undermines confidence in the experimental ledger and raises the risk that other table entries are not traceable to the stated experiments.\\n\\nThe group-size ablation is also not a publishable ablation. Four single-seed Tinker runs over G={2,4,8,16}, apparently over 30–50 steps, cannot establish a default. The explanation for G=16 is speculative and untested. The table reports peak accuracies of 1.000 for Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-4B on GSM8K, while last-10 accuracy is much lower. If peak is computed on the full 1,319-example test set, the result is implausible; if it is computed on a small rolling evaluation subset, it is not comparable to the base model or held-out controls. The paper acknowledges peak is biased, but still prints it prominently and uses it in captions.\\n\\nThe diagnostic contribution is weak. ZVF(p,G)=p^G+(1-p)^G is a trivial consequence of drawing G Bernoulli outcomes and checking whether they are all equal. The paper’s own stratified analysis shows the pooled correlation is task-confounded and the within-GSM8K relationship changes sign. Thus ZVF is neither a validated predictor nor a causal diagnostic; it is a restatement of reward sparsity and saturation. The reward-stability coefficient is underdefined and unvalidated: no threshold calibration, no examples of false positives, no comparison against entropy, length, KL, or reward mean, and only anecdotal use at “0.6”.\\n\\nThe xLAM section does not establish transfer. The strict reward run produces zero reward and ZVF=1 throughout, demonstrating only that the reward was unusable. The full run switches to a “relaxed schema reward” without defining it precisely enough in the main paper, then reports schema validity rather than semantic function-call correctness. Schema-valid JSON is an insufficient tool-use metric, and the SFT baseline is underspecified.\\n\\nThe methodology omits essential details: per-run LoRA ranks, batch sizes, sampling temperature, top-p, rollout counts, evaluation frequency, evaluation subset size, seed handling, KL implementation, prompt formatting, and exact training horizons. Releasing logs does not substitute for a self-contained LNCS paper, especially when the primary backend is proprietary. The related-work section is broad but does not sharpen the novelty claim: the paper does not compare ZVF against obvious existing collapse indicators such as entropy, reward variance, KL drift, response length, or success-rate histograms, so the proposed diagnostic is not shown to add information beyond quantities already logged by standard RL pipelines.\\n\\nThe paper is honest in places, but honesty does not compensate for overclaiming. The defensible contribution is a negative audit note: some GRPO diagnostics are confounded, and several small-budget runs collapse. That is far narrower than the submitted framing, and the 12-page limit does not excuse omitting the protocol details required to verify the central tables.\\n\\n## Authors’ rebuttal\\n\\nWe accept the core criticism. The present draft overstates what the evidence supports. The five-seed Qwen3-8B/GSM8K result is a null result, the Tinker group-size runs are single-seed case studies, the backend comparison is confounded, and the xLAM result is only a schema-validity experiment unless semantic function-call correctness is measured. We also accept that the “peak accuracy” column is misleading and that the TRL model/backbone inconsistency must be corrected.\\n\\nWhat remains defensible is narrower: this is a small-budget audit showing that binary-reward GRPO can enter all-wrong or saturated zero-advantage regimes, and that pooled ZVF correlations are misleading unless stratified by task. We do not defend ZVF as a novel theorem or as a validated predictor. Its value is operational: it is a cheap logged sentinel that explains why a run with all-zero rewards cannot learn under group-normalized advantages. That point is useful, but the paper must be reframed around it.\\n\\nWe will make only space-neutral edits and will not touch the author block or add figures. First, delete the “Scaling Laws for RL Fine-Tuning” subsection, lines 274–284, and the current Broader Impact paragraph, lines 644–659. Use that space for a compact run-ledger paragraph after line 364 specifying, for each table row, backbone, LoRA rank, seed count, batch size, rollouts, sampling temperature/top-p, training steps, evaluation set size, and evaluation frequency. This also fixes the TRL Qwen2.5/Qwen3 inconsistency.\\n\\nSecond, revise the abstract and contributions without adding length: replace “systematic empirical study” with “small-budget diagnostic audit”; replace “quantify the gap attributable to backend implementation” with “report a confounded configuration-level gap”; replace “show that G=8 maximises” with “observe a single-seed G=8 case-study maximum”; and replace “RL benefits transfer” with “schema-validity improves under a relaxed reward, without semantic tool-use validation.”\\n\\nThird, delete the peak-accuracy columns from Tables 1 and 2 and use the freed width to mark seed counts and whether the row is open-stack or proprietary. Fourth, rewrite Section 4.5 to define the relaxed xLAM reward in one sentence and explicitly state that semantic correctness is not measured. Fifth, revise the conclusion line claiming ZVF is an “effective early indicator” to “ZVF is a descriptive collapse check.”\\n\\nThe weaknesses that cannot be fixed within this 12-page budget are new Tinker seeds, a controlled H100 TRL/veRL replication, long-horizon training, full fine-tuning, 14B sweeps, and new MATH/tool-use benchmarks.\",\"ct\":1777865212.58209}]},{\"id\":\"69ef885a-95c4-83e8-9783-95936a50fdfe\",\"title\":\"ZVF NeurIPS Acceptance Strategies\",\"create_time\":1777305691.253673,\"update_time\":1777306460.86451,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"Round 5: maximum-leverage round. The author has applied every v4 blocker fix and now wants:\\n\\n(A) **ZVF main: do whatever it takes to push it over the NeurIPS 2026 main-track bar.**\\nAcross rounds 3 and 4 you said ZVF is \\\"narrow, mostly closed-backend, single-seed, underpowered\\\" and gave 6.0/10 with ~12-18% acceptance probability post-polish. I want the highest-probability path to acceptance, even if it requires new analyses or new experiments. The author has:\\n- Time and compute (can run multi-seed expansions, new ablations, partial-correlation analyses, bootstrap/permutation tests, additional within-task replications on other tasks)\\n- Existing data: full reward traces for 79 runs across Modal H100/TRL/Tinker, including all G-sweep cells, frontier models (qwen3-8b, kimi-k2, deepseek-v3.1, gpt-oss, nemotron-120b), held-out 5-seed Qwen3-8B GSM8K control, ZVF time-series at step granularity, tool-use cross-task case study\\n- Willingness to retitle, restructure, drop sections, add sections, reframe entirely\\n\\nBe concrete and prescriptive. For each high-leverage move, tell me:\\n1. What exactly to add/change/run\\n2. Expected impact on acceptance probability (in percentage-point terms)\\n3. Estimated cost (analysis hours, training hours, $)\\n4. Whether the move is necessary, sufficient-on-its-own, or marginal\\n\\nSpecifically address:\\n- Should the title be retitled to \\\"When Zero-Variance Fraction Misleads: A Stratified Audit of GRPO Reward-Collapse Diagnostics\\\" (your v3 suggestion)? What's the alternative?\\n- What's the strongest version of the **positive companion claim** — ZVF as per-step zero-gradient sentinel? Concrete demonstration via existing tool-use trace + which figure/table to add?\\n- Should the author run more multi-seed replications on a key within-task ZVF cell (e.g., 3-seed Qwen3-8B at G=8 with full ZVF telemetry)? Cost ~15-30 H100 hrs per seed; would the resulting CI on within-GSM8K r make a meaningful difference?\\n- Should the author add bootstrap 95% CIs and permutation p-values for the within-GSM8K Pearson r=+0.40 (N=19) and the model-varying r=+0.13 (N=7)? These are computable from existing data.\\n- Should the author add partial correlations (ZVF | task identity, ZVF | task + accuracy, ZVF | task + group_size) to formally quantify the Simpson's paradox?\\n- Are there other within-task replications possible from existing data — e.g., within-G=8 across stacks (Tinker vs Modal-TRL vs OpenRLHF), or step-level ZVF predicting step-level reward change within a single run?\\n- What's the realistic ceiling acceptance probability if the author does *all* the recommended moves?\\n- If even all moves cap acceptance probability below ~30%, say so and recommend the alternative venue.\\n\\n(B) **DNB E&D: increase acceptance probability beyond the current ~35-45% estimate.**\\nWhat specific moves between now and the deadline would meaningfully raise expected acceptance? E.g., an additional multi-seed run on a key cell, a cleaner claims-hierarchy rewrite, a sharper \\\"what this artifact does and doesn't establish\\\" section, additional reproducibility evidence?\\n\\n(C) **Workshop: increase acceptance probability beyond the current ~60-75% estimate.**\\nWhat's the marginal move that turns a \\\"weak accept\\\" into a \\\"clear accept\\\" at workshop? Tighter scope? Stronger figure? An additional small ablation that fits in the page budget?\\n\\n(D) **Anonymity polish.** What's the minimum-effort set of edits to remove the amber-level residue (US-East / non-OECD wording, non-canonical anonymous URLs `wandb.ai/anonymous/...` and `huggingface.co/anonymous/...`)? Are there any deanonymizing risks I'm missing?\\n\\n(E) **Submission strategy.** Given a single author who can submit to multiple venues, what's the optimal portfolio? E.g., ZVF to main + Workshop, DNB to E&D + Workshop fallback, etc.\\n\\nFormat: be terse and prescriptive. I do not want philosophical discussion of whether negative results \\\"should\\\" be at NeurIPS. I want a concrete checklist of moves, each with expected probability impact and cost. Treat this as a final pre-submission optimization round — assume I will do every move you recommend.\",\"ct\":1777305690.341},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc ls -l /mnt/data && mkdir -p /mnt/data/neurips_v5 && unzip -q /mnt/data/neurips_review_v5.zip -d /mnt/data/neurips_v5 && find /mnt/data/neurips_v5 -maxdepth 3 -type f | sed 's#/mnt/data/neurips_v5/##' | head -200\",\"ct\":1777305694.593004},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cat /home/oai/skills/pdfs/SKILL.md\",\"ct\"