ed -n '3137,3570p'\",\"ct\":1777299393.900953},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_dnb_fitz.txt | sed -n '3570,3635p'\",\"ct\":1777299397.586868},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_workshop_fitz.txt | sed -n '1,520p'\",\"ct\":1777299430.255179},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_workshop_fitz.txt | sed -n '520,1040p'\",\"ct\":1777299434.667922},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_workshop_fitz.txt | sed -n '1040,1600p'\",\"ct\":1777299437.095096},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_workshop_fitz.txt | sed -n '1600,2100p'\",\"ct\":1777299442.820095},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc nl -ba /mnt/data/review_v3_py/main_workshop_fitz.txt | sed -n '260,310p'\",\"ct\":1777299445.844039},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"I reviewed the v3 PDFs against the round-3 change list. My bottom-line answer: **the ZVF reframing is much better and scientifically honest, but it still has not crossed the NeurIPS main-track threshold.** The best home is still **E&D or workshop**, with E&D now the more natural primary target if the author can fix the consistency blockers below. fileciteturn0file0\\n\\n## A. NeurIPS-worthiness verdicts\\n\\n| Variant | Would I accept today? | Updated score | Main blocker |\\n|---|---:|---:|---|\\n| **ZVF main-track, `main_zvf.pdf`** | **No / borderline reject** | **6.0 / 10** | The reframed negative result is useful, but not yet robust or broad enough for main track: one within-task GSM8K stratification, mostly closed-backend/single-seed cells, underpowered N=7 model-varying subset, and no strong positive companion claim. |\\n| **E&D, `main_dnb.pdf`** | **Conditional** | **6.5 / 10** | Good track fit, but it still has internal numerical and manifest inconsistencies, and it should import the ZVF within-task null rather than continuing to foreground the pooled r=-0.769 story. |\\n| **Workshop, `main_workshop.pdf`** | **Yes, weak accept / accept** | **7.5 / 10** | The workshop framing is appropriate and honest. Remaining issues are mostly polish/blocker-fix items: broken `Table ??`, stale scale wording, and a few cross-variant inconsistencies. |\\n\\nFor E&D: the 2026 NeurIPS E&D call explicitly includes work on evaluation design, failure modes, audits, negative results, critical analyses, and use-case-inspired evaluations, and says submissions need not introduce a new model or outperform prior work. That is a much better fit for this artifact than main track. But the same call also emphasizes clear articulation of what claims are supported, under what assumptions, and with what limitations, so the internal consistency issues matter. citeturn993678view0turn993678view1\\n\\n## B. ZVF main-track: did the null-result reframing cross the threshold?\\n\\n**No. It crossed the “honest and defensible” threshold, but not the “main-track NeurIPS contribution” threshold.**\\n\\nThe new framing is a real improvement. The paper no longer asks reviewers to believe that a pooled cross-task ZVF correlation is a predictive diagnostic. It now says the pooled r=-0.769 is task-confounded, the larger N=33 pooled correlation is weak, and the within-GSM8K relationship does not support the original negative-predictor story. That is the right scientific move.\\n\\nBut as a main-track contribution, the headline result is still too narrow:\\n\\n1. **The within-task result is not a strong null; it is an underpowered non-confirmation.**  \\n   Within-GSM8K r=+0.40, p=0.09, N=19 is not “there is no relationship.” It is “the negative cross-task relationship does not survive stratification; the within-task point estimate even goes positive, but the interval is likely wide.” The model-varying N=7 result r=+0.13, p=0.79 is closer to null, but N=7 is too small to carry a main-track negative-result paper by itself.\\n\\n2. **The empirical base is still mostly descriptive, not confirmatory.**  \\n   The paper itself says many Tinker rows are single-seed, closed-backend, short-horizon, and train-set-reward-based. That is fine for an artifact or measurement paper, but weak for a main-track claim that a diagnostic does or does not predict GRPO performance.\\n\\n3. **The “positive” part of the story is still nearly definitional.**  \\n   “ZVF=1 means zero group-relative advantage” is mechanically true and useful, but it is not a main-track empirical discovery. The positive operational claim—ZVF is useful per-step instrumentation inside a task—is plausible, but the current evidence is mostly the synthetic tool-use failure where reward is 0 and ZVF is 1. Reviewers may see that as a sanity check, not a novel finding.\\n\\n4. **The result mostly corrects the paper’s own earlier overclaim.**  \\n   Negative results can absolutely be main-track-worthy, but the strongest ones overturn a widely held claim with broad, rigorous evidence. Here, the clearest target is the paper’s own earlier pooled-correlation interpretation plus adjacent ZVF enthusiasm, not a settled community belief.\\n\\nA negative result **can** be main-track-defensible without a positive companion claim, but only when it is broad, powered, surprising, and methodologically hard to dismiss. This one is interesting but too easy for a reviewer to dismiss as “small-N Simpson’s-paradox audit on one task family.”\\n\\nThe strongest realistic no-new-experiments version would be a **measurement warning paper**, not a benchmark paper:\\n\\n**Title/framing:** something like *“When Zero-Variance Fraction Misleads: A Stratified Audit of GRPO Reward-Collapse Diagnostics.”*\\n\\n**Core contribution:** a clean Simpson’s-paradox-style analysis showing that cross-task ZVF correlations are non-identifiable unless task identity, reward sparsity, group size, and baseline accuracy are controlled.\\n\\n**Main figure:** pooled scatter, then task-stratified scatter, then bootstrap/permutation confidence intervals for the within-GSM8K estimates.\\n\\n**Evidence language:** downgrade the within-task null from “A-grade” to “B: stratified, single-seed/closed-backend, underpowered but directionally important.” Keep only the mechanical theorem as A-grade.\\n\\n**Positive-but-narrow companion:** “ZVF is valid as a per-step zero-gradient sentinel, not as a benchmark score.” Demonstrate this with existing tool-use traces and pseudocode, but avoid predictive language.\\n\\nThat version might be a respectable E&D or workshop paper. It would still be a low-probability main-track paper.\\n\\n## C. New or remaining issues introduced by the edits\\n\\n### Cross-paper / serious consistency issues\\n\\n**The run manifest is not actually reconciled with the results.** This is now the biggest factual problem. In the ZVF paper, the model-varying GSM8K subset used for the N=7 within-task result names Qwen3.5-4B, GPT-OSS-20B, Kimi-K2, DeepSeek-V3.1, and Nemotron-120B. But the run manifest marks several of these corresponding cells as Tier C, failed/partial/not-started, 0-step/no-log rows, while the main text uses them as evidence. DNB has the same problem: Table 4 reports completed frontier/Bitter Lesson rows that the manifest does not represent consistently. If the manifest is “source of truth,” it currently undercuts the paper.\\n\\n**The closed-backend boilerplate conflicts with the headline statistics.** The Tinker acknowledgment says primary statistical claims are drawn from open-source Modal/TR/veRL/OpenRLHF runs and Tinker-only results are descriptive. But the ZVF main headline within-GSM8K correlations are Tinker-derived, and DNB/Workshop also use Tinker-derived case-study statistics prominently. This needs to be rewritten as: “Primary inferential claims are limited to X; Tinker-derived stratified correlations are reported as artifact analyses with closed-backend caveats.”\\n\\n**The group-size story is still inconsistent across tables, figures, and manifest.** ZVF Figure 4 and Appendix C now say fixed-step G=4 has last-10 0.52, G=8 has 0.34, and token-budget T≥16M favors G≈32. But DNB Table 4 still lists a Group Size Ablation row where Qwen3-8B G=8 has last-10 84.4%, while Figure 5 and Appendix D say G=8 last-10 is 0.34. That is a clear blocker.\\n\\n**The “G=32” claim survives in ethics text.** All three variants still have language like “critic-free RL with careful group-size selection (G=32) can be run on modest budgets.” That conflicts with the new “no universal G; budget-dependent optimum” framing. Replace with “budget-dependent group-size selection.”\\n\\n### ZVF main-specific issues\\n\\n**Evidence Table 1 overgrades the headline null.** It labels the within-task null as A-grade even though the row itself says “within-task, single-seed per cell.” That contradicts the table’s own legend, where A is definitional or multi-seed/open-stack. This should be B, or perhaps “B/C: stratified but underpowered and closed-backend.”\\n\\n**“Please cite these” language is too advocacy-coded.** It is fine to say “headline claims,” but “please cite these” reads defensive and unusual for a NeurIPS paper. Reviewers may not like being instructed what to cite.\\n\\n**The title still mismatches the paper.** “A Unified Benchmark for RL Post-Training of Language Models” no longer describes the ZVF main-track variant. The paper is now about a diagnostic null result and a stratified cautionary analysis.\\n\\n**Appendix A.3 still overstates causality.** It says high ZVF coincides with plateaus/regressions “because” group-relative advantage collapsed. The mechanical local claim is true; the performance-causal claim is not established by the within-task null.\\n\\n**GU is overloaded.** Main text uses GU=1-ZVF, while Appendix C defines a token-budget-normalized gradient-utilization estimator involving gradient norm/advantage variance/token budget. These should have distinct names.\\n\\n### DNB-specific issues\\n\\n**DNB still propagates the pooled r=-0.769 more than it should.** Section 5.2 and Figure 4 still foreground the pooled correlation. Given the ZVF variant’s new core lesson, DNB should say: “This pooled correlation is task-confounded; within-GSM8K it does not survive stratification.” Otherwise the two submissions contradict each other.\\n\\n**Table 4 vs Figure 5 is a hard numerical inconsistency.** The G=8 group-size row is the clearest example. Fix before any submission.\\n\\n**Main table vs manifest is not reconciled.** Table 4 contains many completed Tinker frontier/Bitter Lesson rows; the appendix manifest either omits them or marks similar cells as C/failed/0-step. This is exactly the kind of thing E&D reviewers will inspect.\\n\\n**The E&D paper may be trying to be too many things at once.** The strongest E&D story is the artifact plus “what claims are and are not supported.” The paper still reads partly like a benchmark performance paper, partly like a ZVF diagnostic paper, partly like a cross-stack systems audit. It needs a stronger hierarchy of claims.\\n\\n### Workshop-specific issues\\n\\n**There is still a broken cross-reference: “Table ??” on page 8.** This should be fixed immediately.\\n\\n**Workshop Section 3 still says the N=15 ZVF sample spans “3B–671B parameters.”** That violates the new harmonized 0.6B–671B / 0.6B–37B-active wording.\\n\\n**Workshop Table 1 says “TRL-GRPO (Modal H100) Qwen2.5-0.5B,” but the manifest says Modal L4 for qwen2.5-0.5B.** Pick one.\\n\\n**The workshop version still uses the pooled r=-0.769 as a visible headline statistic.** It qualifies it as task-confounded, so this is not fatal, but adding the N=19/N=7 within-GSM8K result would make the workshop version align with the new ZVF framing.\\n\\n## D. Updated scores\\n\\n**ZVF main-track:** **6.0/10**  \\nUp from 5 because the core claim is now honest and coherent. Still below main-track accept because the contribution is narrow, underpowered, and mostly cautionary.\\n\\n**E&D:** **6.5/10**  \\nBetter fit than before and potentially submit-worthy, but current internal contradictions prevent a clean accept. With the Table 4/Figure/manifest/ZVF-stratification fixes, I would move this to around **7.0/10**.\\n\\n**Workshop:** **7.5/10**  \\nThe right venue, the right level of ambition, and appropriately cautious. Fix the broken reference and stale consistency issues before submission.\\n\\n## E. Updated submission strategy\\n\\n**Do not submit the ZVF paper to NeurIPS main as the primary bet.** It is no longer embarrassing or confused, but it is still unlikely to survive main-track review. My subjective acceptance probability for ZVF main is roughly:\\n\\n**As-is:** **8–12%**  \\n**After no-new-experiment polish:** **12–18%**  \\n**Only with new multi-seed/open-stack/within-task replication or intervention evidence:** plausibly higher, but that is outside the current constraint.\\n\\nI would make the strategy:\\n\\n**Primary:** E&D, after fixing the consistency blockers.  \\n**Backup / parallel community target:** workshop artifact.  \\n**Do not spend the main-track slot on ZVF unless the author has no opportunity cost and wants feedback more than acceptance probability.**\\n\\nThe E&D track is now explicitly about evaluation as a scientific object, including failure modes, critical analyses, negative results, evaluation protocols, benchmarks, and tools. That is almost exactly the mature version of this work. But because E&D review is intended to be comparably stringent to the main conference, the paper must be internally airtight before submission. citeturn993678view0turn489097view1\",\"ct\":1777300160.942638}]},{\"id\":\"69ef5adc-9d20-83e8-a4e5-03f8985b3014\",\"title\":\"Review of Revised TinkerRL-Bench\",\"create_time\":1777294045.526777,\"update_time\":1777295144.993841,\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"slug\":null,\"t\":\"You previously reviewed three NeurIPS 2026 paper variants of TinkerRL-Bench (main_zvf.pdf for NeurIPS main track, main_dnb.pdf for Evaluations & Datasets, main_workshop.pdf for a NeurIPS workshop) and gave detailed reviews scoring each 4/10, 5/10, and 6/10 respectively. The author has now revised all three based on your feedback. Please review again.\\n\\nThree changes the author flagged for you (so you can verify they actually landed, not just take their word for it):\\n\\n1. **ZVF variant (main_zvf.pdf):**\\n   - Section 4.1 label renamed from sec:kl to sec:instability and figure label from fig:kl_proxy to fig:instability_proxies; \\\"KL proxy\\\" framing removed from caption.\\n   - Tool-use confound is now disclosed inline at the headline correlation r = -0.769 (was previously buried).\\n   - New Section 4.4 \\\"Evidence Grade of Each Claim\\\" table separates A / B / B* / C grade claims so reviewers don't have to reconstruct from scattered caveats.\\n   - Cross-reference from main text to Appendix group-size reconciliation; figure caption discloses single-seed-per-G.\\n\\n2. **Evaluations & Datasets variant (main_dnb.pdf):**\\n   - Figure 2 caption no longer claims verifiable rewards \\\"eliminate reward hacking\\\" â now says \\\"reduce reward-model subjectivity ... do not eliminate reward hacking, format gaming, or train-set reward inflation\\\".\\n   - Parameter scale harmonized to 0.6Bâ671B total (active 0.6Bâ37B for the largest MoE) across abstract and intro (was inconsistently \\\"0.6B to ~1T\\\" / \\\"0.6Bâ235B\\\" / \\\"3Bâ671B\\\").\\n   - Run manifest expanded from 8 to 12 columns (Tier, Backend, Model, Task, Split, N, Seeds, Steps_max, Reward type [BV/SH/PW], Held-out, Logs released, Checkpoint released).\\n   - Stale \\\"11 of 14 / 4 of 6\\\" infrastructure-failure counts replaced with general \\\"non-trivial subset\\\" language consistent with current limitations section.\\n   - \\\"ChatGPT Pro\\\" / \\\"GitHub Copilot\\\" replaced with vendor-neutral \\\"commercial chat assistant\\\" / \\\"code-completion assistant\\\" in LLM-usage declaration.\\n\\n3. **Workshop variant (main_workshop.pdf):**\\n   - Full restructure: 27 pages â 15 pages total (~4 scientific content pages + ethics + appendix). NeurIPS checklist input dropped (workshops don't require it). Library taxonomy figure, hyperparameter / task / implementation tables removed. 60-row dumping-ground results table replaced with 3-bucket evidence-graded headline (multi-seed open-stack vs single-seed Tinker case studies vs failure probes). New workshop-tightened abstract/intro/conclusion. Conclusion now includes 3 explicit \\\"Discussion prompts for the workshop\\\" (closed-API benchmarking, raw artifact requirements, short-horizon-reward inflation).\\n\\nAnonymity fixes (all variants):\\n- Bare anonymous-org / anonymous-mirror slugs removed; canonical anonymous.4open.science URL used everywhere.\\n- Regional grid intensity 0.716 kg/kWh widened to non-OECD range 0.4â0.8 kg/kWh.\\n- Pronoun leak (\\\"his scripts\\\") was already fixed before your prior review; we re-checked.\\n\\nWhat the author wants from you:\\n\\nA. **Per-variant updated review** â same format as before: recommendation, numeric score (out of 10), three biggest strengths, five most concerning weaknesses ranked, specific actionable improvements before final upload, anonymity check, single most important remaining fix.\\n\\nB. **Cross-variant note** updating your prior submission strategy: given the revisions, do you still recommend (i) workshop as primary, (ii) Evaluations & Datasets as fallback, (iii) main-track ZVF excluded? Has the workshop variant in particular crossed the line from \\\"borderline accept\\\" to \\\"clear accept\\\"?\\n\\nC. **Sanity flag any new issues** the revision introduced â e.g., new internal inconsistencies, new stale references, things that broke during the trim.\\n\\nDo not pull punches. If a fi