assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"search_query\\\":[{\\\"q\\\":\\\"NeurIPS 2026 Call for Papers anonymized double blind page limit checklist\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"short\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777276879.2916},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"bash -lc cd /mnt/data/group6_review && sed -n '2520,2585p' group6_paper.txt && sed -n '3820,3880p' group6_paper.txt && sed -n '3940,4113p' group6_paper.txt\",\"ct\":1777276883.35987},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"{\\\"open\\\":[{\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"turn601301search0\\\"}],\\\"response_length\\\":\\\"medium\\\"}\",\"ct\":1777276899.433233},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"slug\":\"gpt-5-5-pro\",\"t\":\"## 1. Acceptance probability\\n\\n**0% as submitted.** The paper would almost certainly be **desk-rejected before scientific review**: the PDF is non-anonymous, lists author names, institutions, emails, acknowledgments, named mentors, GitHub/HuggingFace/W&B links, and the main paper content runs far beyond the NeurIPS nine-page content limit. NeurIPS 2026 explicitly limits main text to **nine content pages**, requires submissions and linked materials to be anonymized, forbids identifying information and acknowledgments at submission time, and says page-limit/style/anonymity violations may be desk-rejected or rejected without merits review. citeturn728259view0 For context, valid NeurIPS 2025 main-track submissions had a 24.52% acceptance rate, but this would likely not count as a valid reviewable submission. citeturn728259view2 If I ignore desk-rejection mechanics and judge only the scientific paper after anonymization and page-limit fixes, I would still put it around **2–4%** for main track: it is honest and ambitious, but the central claims are mostly exploratory, underpowered, confounded, single-seed, train-reward-heavy, and weaker than the breadth implied by the title.\\n\\n## 2. Flaws a NeurIPS reviewer would flag\\n\\n### Formal / desk-rejection issues\\n\\n1. **Non-anonymous title page.** The paper lists all authors, institutions, and emails on page 1. That alone violates double-blind policy.\\n\\n2. **Identifying links everywhere.** The paper points to author-named GitHub, HuggingFace, and W&B projects. NeurIPS says linked supplementary material must also preserve anonymity. citeturn728259view0\\n\\n3. **Acknowledgments are included.** The paper thanks mentors, vendors, university groups, and teams. NeurIPS explicitly says not to include acknowledgments at submission time. citeturn728259view0\\n\\n4. **Main text is massively over the page limit.** The scientific narrative continues through roughly page 35 before references; NeurIPS main text is limited to nine content pages including figures and tables. citeturn728259view0\\n\\n5. **The paper is formatted like a preprint, not an anonymous submission.** Page 1 says “Preprint. Under review.” with named authors. The current PDF is not submission-ready.\\n\\n6. **The final report is also non-anonymous.** If used as supplementary material, it contains title-page identities, author contributions, project roles, usernames, and institutional details.\\n\\n7. **The artifact links would de-anonymize the authors immediately.** Even if the PDF title page were scrubbed, the linked repos and W&B/HF accounts would break blinding.\\n\\n8. **Table 10’s “ACM/NeurIPS ready ✓” claim is false as submitted.** It directly conflicts with the actual anonymity and page-limit status.\\n\\n### Core contribution / novelty problems\\n\\n9. **The title overpromises.** “A Unified Benchmark for RL Post-Training of Language Models” sounds broad and mature, but the paper repeatedly admits the strongest evidence is narrow: short-horizon GSM8K, mostly train-time rewards.\\n\\n10. **The main empirical win is weak.** The headline held-out Qwen3-8B GRPO control is only **83.3% vs. 82.0%**, with **p = 0.26**. That is not a convincing NeurIPS-scale result.\\n\\n11. **The benchmark breadth is inflated by heterogeneous, partial, or exploratory runs.** The abstract says 79 runs across 7 libraries and 5 model families, but many runs are single-seed, partial, dry-run placeholders, interrupted, or side artifacts.\\n\\n12. **ZVF is useful but not deeply novel.** The zero-variance issue follows almost mechanically from group-relative binary rewards, and multiple concurrent GRPO/RLVR papers already study zero-variance prompts, homogeneous groups, learning cliffs, negative-group rescue, and shrinkage-style fixes. citeturn365013search0turn365013search1turn804682search0turn804682search1turn804682search2\\n\\n13. **The paper itself disclaims priority.** It says “we are rigorous, not first.” That honesty is good, but it weakens originality.\\n\\n14. **The paper is closer to a project audit than a clean research contribution.** It contains “legacy,” “audit trail,” “retracted,” “rerun required,” and “camera-ready” language throughout. Reviewers will read this as unfinished.\\n\\n15. **The central scientific question keeps shifting.** It alternates between benchmark release, ZVF diagnostic, framework gap, GRPO vs. PPO, scaling laws, group size, tool use, code, frontier models, and reproducibility. There is no single crisp thesis.\\n\\n16. **Main-track fit is questionable.** A benchmark artifact might be more plausible for an evaluation/datasets-style venue or workshop, but as a main-track paper it lacks a strong new method, theory, or decisive empirical finding.\\n\\n17. **The “implementation gap” result is not surprising enough.** Classic RL libraries failing on autoregressive LLM training with poor rollout/token handling is plausible, but not enough for a top-conference claim without rigorous matched baselines.\\n\\n18. **The “framework matters” claim is not cleanly isolated.** The strongest framework comparisons mix Tinker vs. TRL, managed vs. open-source, closed vs. visible implementation, different checkpoints, different hardware, and different defaults.\\n\\n19. **The “scaling” claim is not actually a scaling law.** The paper fits exponential curves with low mean R² around 0.21 and admits collapse trajectories violate the model.\\n\\n20. **The frontier-model results are case studies, not evidence.** Single-seed API runs on large models cannot support claims about frontier GRPO behavior.\\n\\n### Experimental design flaws\\n\\n21. **Too many single-seed runs.** Most Tinker/API and frontier experiments are single-seed. Reviewers will not accept statistical claims from them.\\n\\n22. **Training horizons are extremely short.** Thirty gradient steps is not enough to infer convergence, stability, generalization, reward hacking, or asymptotic RL behavior.\\n\\n23. **Interrupted runs are included in main tables.** Qwen3.5-27B, Qwen3-32B, Nemotron-120B, Qwen3-235B, and others are partial. Partial trajectories should not be mixed with completed runs as comparable results.\\n\\n24. **Train-set reward dominates the evidence.** Most results report peak reward and last-10 training reward, not held-out performance.\\n\\n25. **Held-out evaluation is post-hoc and selected.** The top-10 Tinker checkpoint evaluation is selected by training last-10 reward, creating selection bias.\\n\\n26. **Held-out evaluation lacks matched controls.** Most checkpoints in Table 9 do not have base-model controls under the same evaluator.\\n\\n27. **Held-out sample sizes are inconsistent.** The paper uses N=500 for some held-out evaluations, 263 for a truncated run, and the report describes 200-example-per-seed evaluations.\\n\\n28. **The held-out rank correlation is essentially zero.** The paper reports Spearman ρ = −0.02 between last-10 and held-out accuracy for the top checkpoints. That undercuts using training last-10 as a selector.\\n\\n29. **Framework comparisons are confounded by checkpoint differences.** The Task 4 comparison uses Tinker Qwen3-8B-Base versus TRL/verl/OpenRLHF Qwen3-8B. Base vs. instruct is a major confound.\\n\\n30. **Framework comparisons are confounded by hidden defaults.** Tinker’s server-side GRPO details are not visible, so differences cannot be attributed to algorithm or framework.\\n\\n31. **Framework comparisons are confounded by hardware/runtime.** Tinker API, Modal H100, NVIDIA L4, Colab T4, and PES A100 appear in the project. Hardware and software stack effects are not isolated.\\n\\n32. **Algorithm comparisons are not same-stack.** PPO vs. GRPO comparisons mix implementation stack, backend, checkpoint, and sometimes task. That makes algorithm-level conclusions invalid.\\n\\n33. **The paper uses LoRA only.** No full fine-tuning comparison is provided, so all conclusions are LoRA-conditional.\\n\\n34. **The reward functions are mostly sparse binary verifiers.** That is fine for a narrow RLVR paper, but not for broad “RL post-training” claims.\\n\\n35. **No process-reward comparison.** The paper discusses process reward models but does not evaluate dense/process reward alternatives.\\n\\n36. **No direct KL tracking result.** KL tracking failed due to a gradient bug. The paper substitutes reward-trajectory proxies, but admits proxy-vs.-KL correspondence is unvalidated.\\n\\n37. **The policy-drift metrics are not validated.** Stability Index, Peak-to-Tail Drift, and Rolling Variance may correlate with reward, but they are not demonstrated to measure KL or distributional drift.\\n\\n38. **The tool-use task design failed.** Cross-architecture tool-use rows score 0% for Qwen3-32B and Llama-3.1-8B. The paper admits this is likely a task-design / sparse-reward problem.\\n\\n39. **The positive tool-use results are not comparable.** Qwen3-8B SFT→GRPO starts from SFT warm-up, while the 0% cross-architecture runs do not. Those are different regimes.\\n\\n40. **The code-generation results are weakly controlled.** HumanEval numbers include self-reported HF-card results and exploratory runs, not controlled multi-seed benchmark evaluations.\\n\\n41. **The full HumanEval evaluation timed out.** The report lists full HumanEval/MBPP with pass@k and CIs as a remaining critical gap.\\n\\n42. **MATH-500 coverage is too small.** The report says MATH extended has only two runs. That cannot support broad mathematical reasoning claims.\\n\\n43. **No robust OOD evaluation.** The paper lacks MT-Bench, ArenaHard, TruthfulQA, HarmBench, ToxicChat, broader math, code, multilingual, or non-English evaluation.\\n\\n44. **No safety-regression evaluation.** RL post-training can change refusal behavior, harmful content tendencies, or tool-use risk, but no safety suite is run.\\n\\n45. **No contamination analysis.** GSM8K and HumanEval are likely exposed in pretraining data for many modern models. The paper acknowledges HumanEval contamination concerns but does not test for them.\\n\\n46. **No best-of-n / self-consistency baseline.** Since GRPO effects are tiny on held-out GSM8K, inference-time sampling baselines are essential.\\n\\n47. **No SFT-only baseline for many tasks.** Several improvements may be format learning or SFT warm-start effects rather than RL.\\n\\n48. **No DPO/IPO/KTO/ORPO/SimPO baselines.** The related work discusses no-rollout preference baselines, but the paper does not compare against them on the same tasks.\\n\\n49. **No comparison against ZVF-mitigation methods.** Given that ZVF is central, reviewers would ask for RL-ZVP, NGRPO, Scaf-GRPO, LENS, EBPO, or at least one representative mitigation baseline.\\n\\n50. **No token-budget-normalized main comparison.** Group size changes rollout cost. The main group-size result is not convincingly normalized by total sampled tokens / completions.\\n\\n51. **Group-size conclusions are contradictory.** The paper’s main Qwen3-8B sweep says G=8 is best; the report’s older registry includes a G=32 control with stronger last-10 reward. This needs reconciliation.\\n\\n52. **The group-size ablation is single-model, single-task, single-seed.** It cannot justify any general group-size claim.\\n\\n53. **Scale is entangled with family and checkpoint lineage.** Larger models are different families, training histories, APIs, and instruction-tuning regimes. Model size is not a causal variable here.\\n\\n54. **Dense-vs-MoE comparisons are undercontrolled.** Active-parameter matching is not enough; training data, routing, instruction tuning, and tokenizer differences confound the result.\\n\\n55. **MoE routing is not measured.** The report itself lists routing entropy / load-balance logging as a remaining gap.\\n\\n56. **Evaluation protocols are inconsistent.** Training uses stochastic rollouts; held-out uses greedy decoding. Last-10 reward and held-out accuracy are not directly comparable.\\n\\n57. **The task suite is not coherent.** Table 1 includes arithmetic, GSM8K, chat SFT, DPO, distillation, and online distillation, but the results center mostly on GSM8K GRPO.\\n\\n58. **The “seven-library” story is confusing.** The paper has two seven-library rosters: classic RL libraries and LLM-RL launchers. It explicitly warns not to conflate them, but the abstract still sells a broad seven-library benchmark.\\n\\n59. **OpenRLHF and veRL are not real measured baselines in the current artifact.** The appendix says their rows are dry-run placeholders in the framework comparison JSON.\\n\\n60. **Some “baseline” comparisons are meaningless.** Comparing LLM-native GRPO to SB3/CleanRL/Tianshou defaults on language-model arithmetic mostly shows that the classic RL setup is structurally mismatched.\\n\\n### Statistical flaws\\n\\n61. **The study is severely underpowered.** The paper computes that n=5 per arm only detects very large effects, MDE d≈2.024. Most realistic effects are invisible at that seed budget.\\n\\n62. **Single-seed Tinker runs have zero inferential power.** The paper says this, but still presents many single-seed results prominently.\\n\\n63. **Legacy invalid statistics remain in the paper.** The paper retains a 20-test BH table and legacy effect-size table even though it says some tests used single-seed or per-step pseudo-replication.\\n\\n64. **Pseudo-replication appears in earlier analyses.** The paper says an earlier p<10⁻¹⁰ arose from treating per-step observations as i.i.d. across runs. Retaining this history damages reviewer trust.\\n\\n65. **The multiple-comparison story is confusing.** The paper says 19/20 survived in a legacy family, but the restricted Tier-A/B addendum says only F3 survives. Reviewers will ask why the invalid table remains.\\n\\n66. **Post-hoc statistical correction is not a substitute for preregistration.** The paper admits it is exploratory and not preregistered.\\n\\n67. **ANOVA variance decomposition is observational and confounded.** Library, algorithm, model family, scale, task, and platform are entangled. η² values cannot be read causally.\\n\\n68. **Correlations across runs are not independent evidence.** Runs share tasks, models, prompts, code paths, and design decisions. Treating them as independent samples is questionable.\\n\\n69. **The ZVF correlation is dominated by task type.** Tool-use reaches ZVF=100%, GSM8K averages low ZVF. That makes ZVF partly a proxy for task/reward design, not an independent diagnostic.\\n\\n70. **ZVF adds uncertain incremental value.** The paper admits it has not shown incremental predictive power beyond reward mean, entropy, advantage variance, or KL proxies.\\n\\n71. **The ZVF theoretical formula assumes independent rollouts and fixed per-prompt accuracy.** Real model samples are correlated, prompt difficulty varies, and decoding settings matter.\\n\\n72. **Effect sizes are misleading in near-zero-variance failures.** Cohen’s d≈21.84 for LLM-native vs. classic RL is a structural failure artifact, not a meaningful magnitude.\\n\\n73. **Bootstrap CIs on selected checkpoints do not fix selection bias.** Post-hoc top-checkpoint evaluation still overestimates generalization.\\n\\n74. **Several p-values are attached to non-causal contrasts.** Even when statistically significant, they often compare different stacks, checkpoints, or task regimes.\\n\\n### Reproducibility / artifact issues\\n\\n75. **Closed-source Tinker is a major reproducibility blocker.** The paper cannot expose exact GRPO loss, reward normalization, minibatching, gradient accumulation, hardware, or telemetry.\\n\\n76. **Tinker-derived headline numbers cannot be independently reproduced without Tinker access.** That is especially damaging because many strongest-looking numbers come from Tinker.\\n\\n77. **The paper promises open reproducibility but relies on commercial/proprietary infrastructure.** This contradiction is acknowledged but not resolved.\\n\\n78. **Some artifact paths are local or ephemeral.** The report references `/tmp/...`, `/home/user/workspace/...`, and `tinker://...` style paths, which are not stable reviewer artifacts.\\n\\n79. **W&B access may not be anonymous or public.** Even if public, W&B projects under author accounts break anonymity.\\n\\n80. **Some code/logs are missing or incomplete.** The report says GRPO logs are not committed for some contributors, some repo links return 404, and W&B uploads remain outstanding.\\n\\n81. **Dry-run placeholders exist in result files.** That is dangerous in a benchmark paper: reviewers may distrust all automation if placeholder rows appear near measured rows.\\n\\n82. **Self-reported model-card results are mixed with controlled results.** HumanEval 86% and other side artifacts should not appear as if they are part of the same evidence tier.\\n\\n83. **No permanent archival snapshot is described.** GitHub/HF/W&B links can change. NeurIPS reviewers would prefer immutable artifact snapshots or DOI-style archival.\\n\\n84. **Credential requirements reduce reproducibility.** Tinker keys, gated model tokens, and API quotas caused failures and will also affect reviewers.\\n\\n85. **The Llama held-out row is missing due to gated-tokenizer authentication.** A benchmark paper should not have missing rows because of auth friction.\\n\\n86. **The paper’s own limitations say Tinker experiments are not fully reproducible.** That is an unusually serious limitation for a benchmark submission.\\n\\n87. **Licensing is muddy.** Open-Platypus is CC BY-NC; the paper also claims Apache-2.0 code and releases adapters. The exact license boundaries for derivative checkpoints should be cleaner.\\n\\n### Presentation / writing flaws\\n\\n88. **The paper is far too long and unfocused.** It reads like a compressed capstone report plus rebuttal appendix, not a polished NeurIPS manuscript.\\n\\n89. **The abstract is overstuffed.** It tries to claim benchmark breadth, ZVF, group size, PPO/GRPO heterogeneity, frontier stability, held-out controls, tool use, and code all at once.\\n\\n90. **The strongest caveats are buried after bold claims.** Reviewers will see large tables and “benchmark spanning 79 runs,” then later discover many rows are descriptive, partial, or invalid for inference.\\n\\n91. **Figure/table captions are sometimes misleading.** Example: Figure 1’s caption says it is a taxonomy of RL libraries, while the visible figure is a task taxonomy.\\n\\n92. **Table 10 is self-congratulatory.** Marking the authors’ benchmark as “ACM/NeurIPS ready ✓” while competitors get crosses is not reviewer-friendly and is not substantiated.\\n\\n93. **The paper uses too many internal labels.** “Task 4,” “Wave 6,” “F3,” “F4,” “Tier-A/B/C,” “10x Structural Ceiling,” and “Scaling Campaign” make it feel like an internal project log.\\n\\n94. **The report and paper contradict each other in tone.** The report still contains stronger claims like “framework matters more than algorithm choice,” while the paper later walks those back.\\n\\n95. **The narrative repeatedly retracts earlier claims.** Retractions are honest, but a submission should present the corrected argument, not preserve a trail of invalid earlier arguments.\\n\\n96. **Several references appear duplicated or messy.** For example, veRL/HybridFlow-like references appear repeated in the reference list. A reviewer will view this as lack of polish.\\n\\n97. **The final report emphasizes team contributions and course-project provenance.** That is appropriate for a capstone report, but it hurts the perception of a mature anonymous research submission.\\n\\n98. **The paper includes “camera-ready” promises.** Phrases like “will be filled in before camera-ready” are inappropriate for a first submission; reviewers evaluate the submitted artifact.\\n\\n99. **The results are hard to audit.** Tables mix peak training reward, last-10 reward, held-out accuracy, schema compliance, model-card claims, partial runs, and failure rows.\\n\\n100. **The paper does not clearly separate evidence tiers early enough.** Many readers will be confused before discovering that only a small subset supports inferential claims.\\n\\n### Ethical / safety / broader-impact gaps\\n\\n101. **Tool-use safety is not actually evaluated.** The paper discusses tool-use risks but does not run open-ended tool-calling safety tests.\\n\\n102. **The “no direct dual-use concern” claim is too casual.** RL post-training scripts and tool-call adapters can be repurposed; the mitigation mostly relies on scope statements.\\n\\n103. **No red-team evaluation of trained adapters.** There is no jailbreak, misuse, harmful-completion, or unsafe-tool-call assessment.\\n\\n104. **Reward hacking is observed but not systematically mitigated.** The paper finds verbosity-trap behavior in GRPO runs but does not run mitigation baselines.\\n\\n105. **Carbon accounting is speculative.** Tinker hardware and GPU-hours are inferred, not measured. The estimate is better than nothing but not precise.\\n\\n106. **Data provenance is incomplete for contamination risk.** Public benchmark licensing is described, but contamination and benchmark memorization are not seriously tested.\\n\\n107. **Human-subjects / IRB statement is fine but not sufficient.** The higher-risk issues are model behavior, tool-use, reward hacking, and release practices, not human-subject data.\\n\\n### Bottom line\\n\\n108. **The paper’s best version is a useful workshop or artifact/benchmark report, not a NeurIPS main-track paper yet.** The honest limitations are a strength, but the current evidence does not support the breadth implied by the title.\\n\\n109. **The fastest path to a competitive submission would be to shrink the scope radically.** A credible NeurIPS version would need one clean claim, anonymous formatting, nine-page main text, fully matched open-stack experiments, multi-seed held-out results, meaningful baselines, direct KL/advantage/entropy logging, and no placeholder or self-reported rows.\\n\\n110. **As-is, the scientific review would likely reject even after fixing desk-rejection issues.** The paper is ambitious and transparent, but NeurIPS reviewers evaluate quality, clarity, significance, and originality; this version is weakest on quality of causal evidence and clarity of contribution. citeturn293460view0\",\"ct\":1777277663.721519}]}]"